{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'identity|clean-arch|opening' /Users/lchingen/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md\"","aggregated_output":"149:- Saved Step 1 output allows independent Step 2 tests, but Step 2 cannot recover facts Step 1 missed. `node clean-arch/render.mjs --check` checks PRD/HTML sync, not whether either contains the latest decisions; both matched but omitted D388/D389 during this review. [Task 1]\n150:- Seven archived requests × three draws × two arms completed 42 calls. C036 q7/q9 remained `supersedes` 3/3 in both arms; C057 q1 changed once to unsupported `same_statement`; selected controls retained labels. The candidate failed its predeclared criterion and was rejected. See `reports/step2-next-action-2026-11-09/RESULTS.md`, `PROTOCOL.md`, and `clean-arch/step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md` §14. [Task 2]\n151:- D392: `70:e4` originally linked only to v18; an unsupported `69:e5`/`e181` relation made experimental adapter output `e185` inherit v20. Saved judge requests included `e185` and a revision marker 23 times; v20 and v7 judgments cited it eight times each. This establishes historical propagation, not a changed conclusion. `clean-arch/e2e-2026-11-09/adapter.py` is experimental, not production integration. [Task 3]\n184:- At review time Step 1 was a strong candidate but the full system was not production-ready: Step 2 standards/conflict rules remained open, Step 3 lacked persistence and crash recovery, Step 4 did not exist, source quality was unresolved, and independent held-out validation was missing. For an updated assessment inspect `docs/CURRENT_STATE.md`, `clean-arch/takeover-2026-11-07/LOG.md` §§13–15, `clean-arch/step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md` §§3–11, and `clean-arch/step3/library.py`. [Task 1]\n193:scope: architecture PRD changes and the human-readable HTML in clean-arch, including prompt visibility and status.\n194:applies_to: cwd=/Users/lchingen/hivemind; reuse_rule=apply to each architecture PRD change in clean-arch; check current files and commands if the checkout changes.\n204:- clean-arch, PRD.md, architecture.html, render.mjs, docs/check.py, Step 1 Luna, Step 2 Jev, draft prompts\n213:- `clean-arch/PRD.md` is the source of truth; generate `clean-arch/architecture.html` with `node clean-arch/render.mjs` from the repository root. Check drift with `node clean-arch/render.mjs --check` and `python3 docs/check.py`. [Task 1] [ad-hoc note]\n259:# Task Group: Hivemind temperature removal and historical configuration identity\n262:applies_to: cwd=/Users/lchingen/hivemind; reuse_rule=reuse the config/cache and preflight checks for changes to runtime request identity; recheck current default versions and providers.\n281:- `model.call` caches by request SHA alone; an identical request under a newer config can return the existing call ID whose archive row retains the old config identity. Explicit before/after request-byte comparison verified equivalent OpenAI requests; tests verified neither provider sends temperature. Focused tests: 21 passed; full suite: 281 passed; `python3 docs/check.py` passed. [Task 1]\n287:- Preflight checks historical identity and selected saved-request portions, not reconstructed complete request bytes; test bytes explicitly for equivalence claims. An old alternate-provider request containing temperature could miss the new request-hash cache and trigger a resend; none was found among 82 local archives, which does not establish external archive contents. [Task 1]\n325:- The main chat renders discussion messages directly in `app/renderer.js`; filter internal “Bro update” status traffic into Activity. `app/messages.mjs` formats a limited Markdown subset and makes approved local/HTTP(S) links clickable; `app/main.cjs` validates external opening through Electron `shell`. [Task 2]\n484:- Before another comparison, use an unseen full article, prewrite the reference before seeing output, blind the evaluator to arm identity, and exercise the actual downstream reader/selector. [Task 1]\n505:- extraction-variance, gpt-6-luna, DeepSeek V4.1 Flash, prompt-audit, strict schema, tool-calls, source-route, evidence-gate, HANDOFF.md, D244, quote-prefix identity\n515:- One call couples exhaustive reading, selection, quotes/summaries, attribution/date/tier, thesis links, candidates, and grouping; this is a plausible variance surface. The earlier two-step source-first experiment selected more content but failed end-to-end on grouping, ownership, date/identity, and summaries. [Task 1]\n521:- Symptom: instability appears larger than it is. Cause: quote-prefix identity treats merged/split representations as different facts. Fix: compare normalized source facts/record IDs separately from semantic correctness. [Task 1]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '问题怎么写|同一题|还没签|开题|续期' docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html\"","aggregated_output":"3:<title>Hivemind 抽取与开题标准</title>\n130:    <li><a href=\"#opening\"><span class=\"num\">4</span>开题标准</a></li>\n145:  <h1>抽取与开题标准</h1>\n155:  <dt>开题</dt>\n194:<tr><td>2</td><td>\"共同量\"按 D191 落实：任何一家公司之外、好几家都会受它影响的量或价格（合计支出、合约价、利率）都可以开题，开题理由叫 <code>shared_quantity</code>；期间按自然年。提示词和代码要跟着改</td><td class=\"nowrap\">第 3 节 D 类</td></tr>\n208:<p>Alex 逐个听了例子、逐个选的，七个都选 A；改了第 2、3、4 节，标准升为第 4 版，金标要按第 4 版重推：开题部分逐条重判，证据部分逐条核对（D244）。</p>\n213:<tr><td>1</td><td>同一个计划的几种说法，最后核对同一个数的，是一题；各自会变、会分别盯的数才分开；属于已按金额开题的计划的一笔交易并进去</td><td class=\"nowrap\">第 4 节</td></tr>\n214:<tr><td>2</td><td>同一件事的不同截止日：早的那题开着时，晚的截止日是它的证据；早的到期没有结果、事情还在追，系统开下一个截止日的续期题，带上证据，不等新文章</td><td class=\"nowrap\">第 4 节</td></tr>\n216:<tr><td>4</td><td>公司自己的量只按财年开题；按日历年的估计是财年那题的证据</td><td class=\"nowrap\">第 4 节</td></tr>\n218:<tr><td>6</td><td>单笔交易、产品、项目，规模要大到能改变公司的收入、利润、现金或信用风险才开题；够不上的不开题，它的陈述留不留照第 5 节判，留下的是证据</td><td class=\"nowrap\">第 3 节 C 类、第 4 节</td></tr>\n233:<p>尺子跟着修：金标的证据部分按第 5 版重推、另存冻结，打分程序遇到证据部分版本对不上就拒绝打分；开题部分重推之前不报开题的分（第 8 节）。</p>\n253:<tr><td class=\"nowrap\">开题理由</td><td>候选问题值得开的几类理由之一，答案里写在 <code>opens_as</code><span class=\"src\">D184；英文 admission reason</span></td><td></td></tr>\n266:<li><b>问题</b>问题句、结果档、到期日写下不改；当前判断随证据更新，到期定格<span class=\"src\">PRD 06</span>。问题分标的和主题，按开题理由分五类（第 3 节）。问题从三处来：文章里的候选问题；用户的备忘录<span class=\"src\">PRD 06 State</span>：\"A thesis from the user's memo is not yet judged until evidence first links to it\"；分析员开始覆盖一家公司时种下的驱动问题<span class=\"src\">D208，设计未建</span>。</li>\n267:<li><b>研究组</b>一件正在进行的事（一个产品换代及其部署、一笔融资或收购、一个政策过程、一轮投资或需求周期），把几个问题组织在一起，常常跨公司。它不是问题，也不代替开题判断。公司自己的报表行（收入、毛利率、费用、税率、资本开支）不带研究组，除非这一行本身就在追这样一件事；它也可以加入别的问题建的组<span class=\"src\">D165</span>。</li>\n269:<p><b>不进账本的：</b>归档找不到家的证据，记 <code>no home</code> 扔掉<span class=\"src\">D209</span>。评估时逐条读被扔的，分四种：抽取的垃圾；库里缺这个问题（覆盖缺口）；问题在却没挂上（归档错）；该开没开（开题错）。各归各的环节<span class=\"src\">第 1 版 §7</span>。存储的表结构归 PRD 06 管。</p>\n272:<p>开题理由是 D184 定的四类，D236 把主驱动量扩到产品节点，所以写成五类。判两件事，互不顶替：这是谁的问题（标的还是主题）；为什么值得开（开题理由）。</p>\n274:<thead><tr><th>类</th><th>开题理由</th><th>标的或主题</th><th>问什么</th><th>量还是事件</th><th>期间和到期</th><th>核对材料 <code>settled_by</code></th><th>出处</th></tr></thead>\n278:<tr><td class=\"nowrap\"><b>C</b> 约束性义务</td><td><code>binding_obligation</code></td><td>标的</td><td>公司以自己名义承担的：为别人的债务、租约或收入担保或提供信用支持；借钱给别人；承诺购买（向别人买产能或服务也算）；为客户的产能或收入兜底；或写明规模、尚未完成的入股或收购<span class=\"src\">D236；收购按 D243 第 7 条</span>。规模要大到能改变公司的收入、利润、现金或信用风险才开；够不上的单笔入股、收购、租约、授信不开题，陈述留不留照第 5 节判<span class=\"src\">D248 第 6 条</span>。不算：自己借钱、自己的租约、已完成或没写规模的投资。D239 选定的 role-v29 原文：\"Its own borrowing, its own leases and a completed or unsized investment are not obligations\"；D238 的记录写作 \"the company's own borrowing and leases and a completed or unsized stake do not\"</td><td>还没签：是非两档。已经承担（一笔或一个计划）：金额，五档</td><td>检查日最多在文章后 18 个月<span class=\"src\">D185</span>；已承担的按下一份年报</td><td>签约公告、年报承诺附注</td><td>D184、D185、D210、D236、D238、D243</td></tr>\n296:<li><b>宏观量</b>（利率、通胀）：按 D 类共同量开题<span class=\"src\">D243 第 2 条</span>。只来自一篇纯宏观文章时，只有主题在名单上才开<span class=\"src\">D191 第 3 条</span>。宏观对每家公司的正负，在判断那家公司时读主题块决定<span class=\"src\">D191 第 1 条、D195</span>。</li>\n297:<li><b>开题理由是封闭的</b><span class=\"src\">D184</span>：一件事属不属于五类之一，按上表逐条看，比如一个监管决定看它是不是 E 类。哪一类都不属于的不开；它是不是证据、挂到哪，照第 5 节和归档规则。</li>\n300:<h3>问题怎么写：PRD 06 的梯子规则，不重写</h3>\n308:<h2 id=\"opening\"><span class=\"num\">4</span>开题标准</h2>\n310:<p>颗粒度按一个高端对冲基金分析员：只有会单独下注的大事开题，鸡毛蒜皮不开题（它的陈述留不留照第 5 节判）；同一个赌注的几种说法是一题，截止日、里程碑、财年和日历年各按本节\"同一题、续期，还是另一题\"里的那一条判<span class=\"src\">D248</span>。</p>\n315:<li><b>值得追</b>写得出\"这个结果会改变哪项收入、利润、现金或信用风险、竞争位置\"吗？写不出就留作证据，不开题。\"数字很大\"不算理由。单笔交易、产品、项目，规模够不上改变公司的收入、利润、现金或信用风险的，不开题，陈述留不留照第 5 节判<span class=\"src\">D248 第 6 条：问的就是小交易、小产品</span>；这一测试的其余理由（包括竞争位置）不变。</li>\n318:<li><b>不是重复</b>和已有问题比，只是预测值、措辞、来源、方向或同一总量的另一种口径不同，最终核对的是同一个结果吗？是就是同一题。抽取看不到库<span class=\"src\">D211</span>，这一条在抽取时只对同一篇文章里提出的几个问题判；和库里的比由匹配做。</li>\n322:<li><b>原文支持：</b>主体、量或事件、时间依据都能在原文找到。原文没给日期、而规则允许研究员定检查日的，用那个日期并标明：没写日期的待决监管事项用本财年末（第 3 节 E 类）；还没签的约束性义务用文章后 18 个月以内能核对签约的那天<span class=\"src\">D185、D210</span>。不把\"拟议\"\"预计\"写成\"已签\"\"已完成\"。</li>\n337:<tr><td>以约束性义务为理由开：公司自己借钱、自己的租约、已完成或没写规模的投资。它们不是约束性义务；对别的问题仍可以是证据，或按别的开题理由判</td><td>D236、D238</td><td>证据</td></tr>\n343:<h3>同一题、续期，还是另一题：匹配这一步的事，抽取不判库里的</h3>\n345:<li><b>总规则：</b>同一个结果能判掉两题，就是同一题；答案能分开走、而且分析员会分别下注的，才是另一题<span class=\"src\">D248</span>。</li>\n346:<li><b>同一题：</b>同一个结果能同时判两个问题。另一个估计、来源、措辞、方向<span class=\"src\">第 1 版 §6 测试 4</span>；同一总量的另一种人群或代用口径（D190 碰撞裁决提示 collision-v1：\"another population or proxy of one aggregate\"；D197 引的 role-v12 原文：\"another population or another wording of the same aggregate is evidence to the shown aggregate thesis\"）。候选并进已开问题，用它的结果档和到期日<span class=\"src\">PRD 06</span>。只有以共同量理由开题、带量的候选由代码自动判，其余是匹配和碰撞裁决的判断。</li>\n348:<li><b>同一件事的不同截止日：</b>早的那题开着时，晚的截止日是它的证据，不另开；早的到期没有结果、事情还在追，系统开下一个截止日的续期题，带上并进来的证据，不等新文章<span class=\"src\">D248 第 2 条</span>。</li>\n350:<li><b>公司自己的量：</b>只按财年开题；按日历年的估计是财年那题的证据，两种年份差得多时分量低一点<span class=\"src\">D248 第 4 条；PRD 06 梯子第 5 条</span>。</li>\n352:<li><b>续期：</b>同一个量或同一串事件的下一个期间<span class=\"src\">PRD 06 Succession、D190</span>。</li>\n354:<li><b>约束性义务：</b>\"一笔或一个计划\"一题<span class=\"src\">D210</span>；一笔交易是不是某个已按金额开题的计划的一部分，由匹配判，是就并入<span class=\"src\">D248 第 1 条</span>。还没签的单笔交易是\"签不签\"的是非题，照旧可以开，不并进计划的金额题<span class=\"src\">D210；PRD 06；D248 未改</span>。</li>\n355:<li><b>共同量：</b>以共同量理由开题、带量的候选（量的类别是 <code>other</code> 的不算），由代码按\"主题＋量＋期间\"判同一题；政策阶段和别的事件保留按事件判的身份；不带这个开题理由的主题量由模型判<span class=\"src\">D197</span>。</li>\n362:<tr><td>测试 1–3、同一篇文章里的测试 4、属于哪一类、问题怎么写</td><td>抽取的模型（判断）</td></tr>\n364:<tr><td>和库里的同一题、续期、另一题</td><td>匹配（Jev）和碰撞裁决<span class=\"src\">D190</span>；共同量由代码<span class=\"src\">D197</span></td></tr>\n472:<p class=\"note\">下面这份第 3 版金标是历史记录。第 4 版（D248）改了开题的规则，没有改动证据条目（逐条核对见 <span class=\"path\">research/2026-10-30-noise/gold-v4-evidence-check.md</span>）；第 5 版（D250）改了证据的四条界线。金标的证据部分按第 5 版重推，两位互不知情的读者逐条判，分歧对着原文定，另存成新文件冻结，每条改动写改前、改后和理由，第 3 版文件不动（D243 第 11 条）。开题部分要按第 4 版逐条重判，重判之前打分程序不报开题的分；证据部分版本对不上，打分程序拒绝打分（D244、D250）。</p>\n487:<li><b>问题：</b>必须开的（类、开题理由、标的或主题、要点、<code>settled_by</code>）；可以开的（另一种合理写法）；不该开的（第 4 节哪一行或哪个测试）。</li>\n531:<li><b>每一句都对得上这一页。</b>提示词里每一句关于留、不留、分量、开题的话，在这一页找得到出处；找不到的，是给 Alex 的提议，不是改动<span class=\"src\">AGENTS §6</span>。</li>\n605:<tr><td class=\"nowrap\">10-29 D241</td><td class=\"nowrap\">这份标准</td><td>\"几个大类型的账本 - 开题标准和档次 这两个要完整的定义好写好\"；\"档位根据信息量作定标 -以上有你自己trace 过的golden set 来做为改prompt测试标准\"</td></tr>\n618:<tr><td>B3</td><td>约束性义务：一笔或一个计划一题（D210），还是一组入股是一个判断（D201 规则 G）</td><td>按 D210 \"一笔或一个计划\"；一笔交易属不属于已开的计划，由匹配判；同一计划的几种说法是一题；够不上公司规模的单笔交易不开题，陈述留不留照第 5 节判</td><td>D210、D236、D238、D248</td></tr>\n619:<tr><td>B4</td><td>开题测试三套，提示词和代码都没用</td><td>第 1 版 §6 四个测试加评审三条写法检查（第 4 节）；测试 4 和库比的部分归匹配</td><td>第 1 版 §6；评审 §1；D211</td></tr>\n627:<tr><td>B12</td><td>研究组、驱动层</td><td>研究组不是问题，不代替开题判断；七类驱动是重要性的理由；词汇表只禁 \"thesis driver\"</td><td>D165、D208；评审 §6</td></tr>\n629:<tr><td>B14</td><td>组成部分算不算同一题</td><td>同一总量的另一种人群或口径是同一题；有自己驱动、答案能分开走、分析员会分别下注的是另一题，要过独立意义测试</td><td>D190；第 1 版 §6；D248</td></tr>\n652:<p>新案例上留不留的一致率从第二轮的 80–94% 升到 93–98%，连分量从 72–87% 升到 81–89%。剩下的分歧几乎都在核心和辅助的界线，由 D243 第 1 条定下判法。三位读者判对、作者判错的，记作作者的错：最大客户带来的收入和中国销售占比不该开题（独立意义测试），还有两处陈述。记录在 <span class=\"path\">simulation-log.md</span>。</p>\n655:<p>Codex 只读，逐条对照签过的第 1 版、全部决策、PRD 06，找\"作者自己加的规则\"、和决策矛盾的地方、引错的原话。每一轮的发现都改掉并记下处理。第一轮查出 18 处自己加的规则和 9 处和决策矛盾，比如\"名单外公司的量不开题\"违反 D155、D187，\"抽取要和库里比重复\"违反 D211，把达标线缩短，把\"在谈的金额\"排除出核心。最后三轮只剩措辞层面的问题；第十一轮的发现在 2.14 版改掉，没有再复查。</p>\n687:<footer>Hivemind · 抽取与开题标准 · 第 5 版 · 2026-10-29 Alex 签字（D243），2026-10-30 按 Alex 逐个选的七条修订（D248），2026-10-31 按 Alex 定的证据四条界线和计数单位修订（D250）。页首的 standard-version 是规则的版本号：改任何一条规则都要升它，金标随之重定，打分程序遇到版本对不上就拒绝打分（D244）。</footer>\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '272,325p;343,355p' docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '272,325p;343,355p' docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html\"","aggregated_output":"<p>开题理由是 D184 定的四类，D236 把主驱动量扩到产品节点，所以写成五类。判两件事，互不顶替：这是谁的问题（标的还是主题）；为什么值得开（开题理由）。</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap wide\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>类</th><th>开题理由</th><th>标的或主题</th><th>问什么</th><th>量还是事件</th><th>期间和到期</th><th>核对材料 <code>settled_by</code></th><th>出处</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td class=\"nowrap\"><b>A</b> 主驱动量</td><td><code>main_driver</code></td><td>标的</td><td>承载公司价值的那个年度量，不管公司指不指引：撑起业务的分部收入、毛利率、资本开支；跨行业如银行的净利息收入、零售商的同店销售、公用事业的费率基数、航空公司的燃油成本（当它是争论焦点时）。不是每一行报表数</td><td>量，五档</td><td>公司财年的今年或明年；到期日是这个数的发布日</td><td>公布这个数的公开材料，通常是年报</td><td>D184：<code>settled_by</code> 是 \"the public material that resolves it\"；PRD 06 梯子第 5 条</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"nowrap\"><b>B</b> 主产品节点</td><td><code>main_driver</code></td><td>标的</td><td>承载公司价值的那一代产品哪天走到能带来收入的那一步（通常是开始出货）；发布、送样是它的证据，只有会分别下注的里程碑（新药的三期数据和获批）各一题<span class=\"src\">D248 第 3 条</span></td><td>事件，五档，或是非两档<span class=\"src\">PRD 06 梯子第 1 条</span></td><td>能判出结果的那天</td><td>公司公告、发货确认</td><td>D236</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"nowrap\"><b>C</b> 约束性义务</td><td><code>binding_obligation</code></td><td>标的</td><td>公司以自己名义承担的：为别人的债务、租约或收入担保或提供信用支持；借钱给别人；承诺购买（向别人买产能或服务也算）；为客户的产能或收入兜底；或写明规模、尚未完成的入股或收购<span class=\"src\">D236；收购按 D243 第 7 条</span>。规模要大到能改变公司的收入、利润、现金或信用风险才开；够不上的单笔入股、收购、租约、授信不开题，陈述留不留照第 5 节判<span class=\"src\">D248 第 6 条</span>。不算：自己借钱、自己的租约、已完成或没写规模的投资。D239 选定的 role-v29 原文：\"Its own borrowing, its own leases and a completed or unsized investment are not obligations\"；D238 的记录写作 \"the company's own borrowing and leases and a completed or unsized stake do not\"</td><td>还没签：是非两档。已经承担（一笔或一个计划）：金额，五档</td><td>检查日最多在文章后 18 个月<span class=\"src\">D185</span>；已承担的按下一份年报</td><td>签约公告、年报承诺附注</td><td>D184、D185、D210、D236、D238、D243</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"nowrap\"><b>D</b> 共同量</td><td><code>shared_quantity</code><span class=\"src\">D191、D243 第 2 条</span>；现行提示词和代码还叫 <code>industry_aggregate</code>，按这一页改</td><td>主题</td><td>任何一家公司之外、好几家都会受它影响的量或价格：几家公司的合计（云厂商资本开支）、价格（内存合约价、油价）、利率。同一总量的另一种人群、另一种说法是这个量的证据</td><td>量，五档</td><td>自然年的今年或明年。同一个量、同一年只有一个问题，由代码按\"主题＋量＋期间\"判<span class=\"src\">D197</span>；量的类别是 <code>other</code> 的不算</td><td>几家公司年报、统计发布、官方决定</td><td>D184、D191、D197、D243；PRD 06</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"nowrap\"><b>E</b> 政策阶段</td><td><code>policy_stage</code></td><td>问的是一家公司的收入、利润、份额或产品进度，算标的，即使由政策驱动；问的是世界上的事（许可制度、利率、合计支出），算主题。D184 采纳的 role-v12 原文：\"A question about a company's revenue, profit, share or product progress is an asset thesis; put its ticker in object, even when policy or macroeconomics drives it\"</td><td>一个政策或监管过程到了哪一步，写明谁说了算</td><td>事件，五档或两档</td><td>能判出那一步的那天。文章没写日期的待决监管事项，用本财年末（role-v12 原文：\"A pending regulatory decision may lack a date; use the current fiscal year-end as its due date\"）；这个日期是研究员定的检查日，要标明<span class=\"src\">评审 §1 第 1 条</span></td><td>官方决定、公报</td><td>D184</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n<p>例子（跨行业）：</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>A：</b>NVIDIA FY2027 数据中心收入；一家地区银行 FY2026 净利息收入；一家公用事业公司 2027 年费率基数。</li>\n<li><b>B：</b>下一代机架系统能否在 2027 年底前开始出货；一家车企的新平台能否在 2026 年内开始交付。</li>\n<li><b>C：</b>NVIDIA 为云客户产能兜底的义务在 FY2027 年报达到多少；一家保险公司是否在 2027 年中前为合资公司 5 亿美元债务签署担保。</li>\n<li><b>D：</b>五家最大云厂商 2026 年合计资本开支；2027 年 DRAM 合约价相对 2026 年的变化、美联储 2026 年最后一次会议后的利率上限。</li>\n<li><b>E：</b>中国是否在 2027-01-31 前正式批准本国公司进口 H200；美国是否在某日前对某类芯片实行出口许可。这两题问的是许可这件世界上的事，所以是主题，role-v12 原文的例子就是 \"licensing\"。一个监管决定对一家公司收入或产品进度的影响，问成那家公司的量或产品节点时，是标的问题（同一句原文）。</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>五类之外的东西去哪</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>七类驱动不是问题类型。</b>它们是一条陈述为什么重要的理由，用来判分量和归档。D208 设计的\"每家公司种下的驱动问题\"由分析员开始覆盖时写好，不从文章里开；还没建。</li>\n<li><b>别家公司自己的量</b>（对手的份额、客户的资本开支）：D187 定了\"任何公司自己的量是那家公司的问题，私营公司也算\"，但还没实现，要先在覆盖名单里加私营公司这一类。D155 定了\"公司清单可以作为初始参考，但清单外不能忽略\"。现行代码在匹配时跳过名单外的对象<span class=\"src\">PRD 06 Step 4</span>。所以抽取照常可以提这样的问题，它要过第 4 节的测试；名单外的现在会被匹配跳过，这不算抽取错。</li>\n<li><b>宏观量</b>（利率、通胀）：按 D 类共同量开题<span class=\"src\">D243 第 2 条</span>。只来自一篇纯宏观文章时，只有主题在名单上才开<span class=\"src\">D191 第 3 条</span>。宏观对每家公司的正负，在判断那家公司时读主题块决定<span class=\"src\">D191 第 1 条、D195</span>。</li>\n<li><b>开题理由是封闭的</b><span class=\"src\">D184</span>：一件事属不属于五类之一，按上表逐条看，比如一个监管决定看它是不是 E 类。哪一类都不属于的不开；它是不是证据、挂到哪，照第 5 节和归档规则。</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>问题怎么写：PRD 06 的梯子规则，不重写</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>一个问题只问一个量的一个期间，或一件事；能各自朝相反方向走、而且分析员会分别下注的部分要分开<span class=\"src\">PRD 06 梯子第 3 条；\"分别下注\"是 D248 加的，PRD 06 签字后更新</span>，分开的每一题都要过第 4 节的测试。</li>\n<li>写成问句，不写成断言<span class=\"src\">PRD 06 Rationale</span>。</li>\n<li>结果档可核对、不重叠、不留缝，到期恰好一档成立；中间一档写明和什么比；\"小幅\"\"部分\"这种不能核对的说法不用，部分状态写成单独一档<span class=\"src\">PRD 06 梯子第 2 条</span>。标的问题的档描述业务结果；主题问题的档只写结局，不写好坏<span class=\"src\">PRD 06、D186</span>。</li>\n<li>到期日：量是它的发布日；事件是能判出结果的那天。公司的量用公司财年，别的用自然年，只开今年和明年<span class=\"src\">PRD 06 梯子第 5 条；约束性义务例外，D185</span>。</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"opening\"><span class=\"num\">4</span>开题标准</h2>\n<blockquote><p><b>一个问题值得单独追，要同时满足三件事：它的答案可能改变仓位；能在一个日期用公开材料判出落在哪一档；库里没有问题在问同一件事。</b>开多少个是判断的结果，不是目标<span class=\"src\">第 1 版 §6；评审 §1</span>。</p>\n<p>颗粒度按一个高端对冲基金分析员：只有会单独下注的大事开题，鸡毛蒜皮不开题（它的陈述留不留照第 5 节判）；同一个赌注的几种说法是一题，截止日、里程碑、财年和日历年各按本节\"同一题、续期，还是另一题\"里的那一条判<span class=\"src\">D248</span>。</p>\n<p>另有一条前提：新问题要有独立的投资判断；一个新产品、一个新数字、一个更窄的范围，单凭这些不开<span class=\"src\">D201</span>。</p></blockquote>\n\n<h3>四个测试<span class=\"src\">第 1 版 §6</span></h3>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li><b>值得追</b>写得出\"这个结果会改变哪项收入、利润、现金或信用风险、竞争位置\"吗？写不出就留作证据，不开题。\"数字很大\"不算理由。单笔交易、产品、项目，规模够不上改变公司的收入、利润、现金或信用风险的，不开题，陈述留不留照第 5 节判<span class=\"src\">D248 第 6 条：问的就是小交易、小产品</span>；这一测试的其余理由（包括竞争位置）不变。</li>\n<li><b>独立意义</b>能写出两个情景吗：其他问题的答案不变，只有这一题相反，而对业务的判断确实不同？写不出就优先作为证据<span class=\"src\">评审 §1 第 3、8 条</span>。评审里的一个判例：NVIDIA FY2027 总收入题判为数据中心收入题的证据，理由是\"该段明确说主要增长来自数据中心，未提出独立的其他业务争论\"。这是对那一段材料的判断，别的材料要逐个判。</li>\n<li><b>能核对</b>有一个日期和一份公开材料能把答案放进事先固定的档吗？没有就重写问题。到期没有材料记\"未能判定\"，不算错。</li>\n<li><b>不是重复</b>和已有问题比，只是预测值、措辞、来源、方向或同一总量的另一种口径不同，最终核对的是同一个结果吗？是就是同一题。抽取看不到库<span class=\"src\">D211</span>，这一条在抽取时只对同一篇文章里提出的几个问题判；和库里的比由匹配做。</li>\n</ol>\n<p>另外三条写法检查<span class=\"src\">评审 §1 第 1、5、6 条</span>。没过的问题不能照原样开，要重写；重写不了就不开，材料作证据。</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>原文支持：</b>主体、量或事件、时间依据都能在原文找到。原文没给日期、而规则允许研究员定检查日的，用那个日期并标明：没写日期的待决监管事项用本财年末（第 3 节 E 类）；还没签的约束性义务用文章后 18 个月以内能核对签约的那天<span class=\"src\">D185、D210</span>。不把\"拟议\"\"预计\"写成\"已签\"\"已完成\"。</li>\n<li><b>口径完整：</b>算谁、不算谁、算什么写清楚；支出、融资、担保、订单、收入不混。</li>\n<li><b>时间正确：</b>原文支持这个期间，不把几年的平均写成今年。</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>同一题、续期，还是另一题：匹配这一步的事，抽取不判库里的</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>总规则：</b>同一个结果能判掉两题，就是同一题；答案能分开走、而且分析员会分别下注的，才是另一题<span class=\"src\">D248</span>。</li>\n<li><b>同一题：</b>同一个结果能同时判两个问题。另一个估计、来源、措辞、方向<span class=\"src\">第 1 版 §6 测试 4</span>；同一总量的另一种人群或代用口径（D190 碰撞裁决提示 collision-v1：\"another population or proxy of one aggregate\"；D197 引的 role-v12 原文：\"another population or another wording of the same aggregate is evidence to the shown aggregate thesis\"）。候选并进已开问题，用它的结果档和到期日<span class=\"src\">PRD 06</span>。只有以共同量理由开题、带量的候选由代码自动判，其余是匹配和碰撞裁决的判断。</li>\n<li><b>同一个计划的几种说法</b>（担保、融资承诺、信用支持……），最后核对的是同一个数，是一题<span class=\"src\">D248 第 1 条</span>。</li>\n<li><b>同一件事的不同截止日：</b>早的那题开着时，晚的截止日是它的证据，不另开；早的到期没有结果、事情还在追，系统开下一个截止日的续期题，带上并进来的证据，不等新文章<span class=\"src\">D248 第 2 条</span>。</li>\n<li><b>同一代产品的几个里程碑：</b>一题，问能带来收入的那一步（通常是开始出货）；送样、发布作证据；会分别下注的里程碑（新药的三期数据和获批）各一题<span class=\"src\">D248 第 3 条</span>。</li>\n<li><b>公司自己的量：</b>只按财年开题；按日历年的估计是财年那题的证据，两种年份差得多时分量低一点<span class=\"src\">D248 第 4 条；PRD 06 梯子第 5 条</span>。</li>\n<li><b>身份：</b>事件和政策题里模型写的期间起点，不拿来挡合并；对象、事件、截止日照旧核对<span class=\"src\">D248 第 5 条</span>。</li>\n<li><b>续期：</b>同一个量或同一串事件的下一个期间<span class=\"src\">PRD 06 Succession、D190</span>。</li>\n<li><b>另一题：</b>同一件事里答案能分开走、分析员会分别下注的：有自己驱动的组成部分；同一笔交易或同一份附注里两个各自会变、分别盯着的数；另一件事<span class=\"src\">D190 碰撞裁决的四种；D248 第 1 条收窄了\"担保和融资\"</span>。</li>\n<li><b>约束性义务：</b>\"一笔或一个计划\"一题<span class=\"src\">D210</span>；一笔交易是不是某个已按金额开题的计划的一部分，由匹配判，是就并入<span class=\"src\">D248 第 1 条</span>。还没签的单笔交易是\"签不签\"的是非题，照旧可以开，不并进计划的金额题<span class=\"src\">D210；PRD 06；D248 未改</span>。</li>\n<li><b>共同量：</b>以共同量理由开题、带量的候选（量的类别是 <code>other</code> 的不算），由代码按\"主题＋量＋期间\"判同一题；政策阶段和别的事件保留按事件判的身份；不带这个开题理由的主题量由模型判<span class=\"src\">D197</span>。</li>\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3; a=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/identity-probe/v2/candidates-v2-next.json\\\"))[\\\"categories\\\"][\\\"T3 one price compared across two years\\\"][\\\"order\\\"]; c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); [print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",x[\\\"input_id\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",c.execute(\\\"select text from items where input_id=?\\\",(x[\\\"input_id\\\"],)).fetchone()) for x in a[0:5]]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3; a=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/identity-probe/v2/candidates-v2-next.json\\\"))[\\\"categories\\\"][\\\"T3 one price compared across two years\\\"][\\\"order\\\"]; c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); [print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",x[\\\"input_id\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",c.execute(\\\"select text from items where input_id=?\\\",(x[\\\"input_id\\\"],)).fetchone()) for x in a[10:15]]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3; a=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/identity-probe/v2/candidates-v2-next.json\\\"))[\\\"categories\\\"][\\\"T3 one price compared across two years\\\"][\\\"order\\\"]; c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); [print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",x[\\\"input_id\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",c.execute(\\\"select text from items where input_id=?\\\",(x[\\\"input_id\\\"],)).fetchone()) for x in a[5:10]]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3; a=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/identity-probe/v2/candidates-v2-next.json\\\"))[\\\"categories\\\"][\\\"T3 one price compared across two years\\\"][\\\"order\\\"]; c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); [print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",x[\\\"input_id\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",c.execute(\\\"select text from items where input_id=?\\\",(x[\\\"input_id\\\"],)).fetchone()) for x in a[15:20]]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3; a=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/identity-probe/v2/candidates-v2-next.json\\\"))[\\\"categories\\\"][\\\"T3 one price compared across two years\\\"][\\\"order\\\"]; c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); [print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",x[\\\"input_id\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",c.execute(\\\"select text from items where input_id=?\\\",(x[\\\"input_id\\\"],)).fetchone()) for x in a[0:5]]'\"","aggregated_output":"\n### gnews:0b1b72dac911085466f3a3b1 \n (\"Nvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA) investors haven't had the success they're used to in 2026. Over the past three years, the stock easily crushed the market in the first half of the year, but that's not the case in 2026. So far in 2026, Nvidia has lost to the market, as measured by the S&P 500 . Additionally, it has barely made investors any money, only rising about 2% for the year while the S&P 500 is up around 7%.\\nThat's a disappointing run to date, but I think Aug. 26 could be when Nvidia turns it around. That's when it reports earnings for the second quarter of fiscal year 2027, and it could be the catalyst this stock needs to skyrocket.\\nNvidia consistently exceeds expectations\\nNvidia is thriving from the AI data center build-out, as it supplies GPUs and other infrastructure and software to support them. Nvidia's GPUs are the gold standard in AI computing, and nearly every company in the AI realm runs at least part of its workloads on Nvidia hardware. Demand for Nvidia's products continues to ramp up. This is evidenced by AI hyperscalers continuing to raise their spending outlooks for 2026 and hinting that spending in 2027 will be even higher. That bodes well for Nvidia's future growth and reassures investors that 2026 will not be the end of the trend.\\nFor the second quarter of fiscal year 2027 (ending in late July), Wall Street analysts expect an impressive 96% growth rate. Nvidia has a strong track record of exceeding expectations, so I wouldn't be surprised if its revenue growth rate returns to the triple-digit range. That would make headlines, but what investors will really be focused on is its Q3 guidance. Right now, analysts estimate 81% revenue growth for Q3. If Nvidia projects something in the 90% range again, the stock should see a quick rise, as none of that is currently priced in.\\nNvidia trades at 21.1 times forward earnings, the same multiple as the S&P 500.\\nThis is the market communicating that if Nvidia hits expectations, it's going to price it like a market-average stock. However, investors know that Nvidia consistently exceeds expectations and is poised for further growth next year, driven by AI hyperscaler guidance.\\nI think this sets the stage for an end-of-the-year rally in Nvidia's stock, and it could all kick-start in late August. If you've missed out on Nvidia's stock so far, now is the perfect time to load up on shares, as I doubt it will stay this cheap for much longer .\",)\n\n### gnews:afd03bb57a140b78c7df9dbc \n ('NVIDIA\\'s reported $14 billion Hugging Face deal buys control of the distribution hub where nearly all open-source AI models live.\\nOpenAI\\'s custom chip scales to 1.3 GW by 2027 and Broadcom guides $21.7 billion in Q4 AI revenue, each cutting into NVIDIA\\'s dominance.\\nAMD already runs 3 million models out of the box, giving it a ready weapon if Hugging Face loses neutrality under NVIDIA ownership.\\nJust released. Our analysts combed the entire stock market and named the ten best stocks to buy right now. The report is free. Enter your email and see if any of your stocks made the cut.\\nThe world\\'s most valuable semiconductor company is reportedly preparing to spend roughly $14 billion on a business whose core product is given away for free, according to Bloomberg Intelligence. Bloomberg reports that NVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA ) is in advanced talks to acquire Hugging Face, the platform where the open-source AI community hosts models, datasets, and applications. Reports include a $1 billion employee retention component, according to Bloomberg.\\nMatt Bloxham, senior analyst at Bloomberg Intelligence, explained the strategic logic: \"Hugging Face has become the poster child for the AI boom. It is a platform for hosting open-source AI models, datasets, and apps, a kind of real collaboration space for the AI community, and that is one of the reasons why NVIDIA has been attracted to it.\" NVIDIA\\'s Q2 FY27 revenue was $96.22 billion, so the cash is available.\\nHugging Face is where developers download open models, share weights, or spin up demos. When a lab releases an open model, the file typically lands on Hugging Face first. When a developer at a bank or hospital wants to try a domain-specific model without paying per-token API fees, that is where they go.\\nDid Any of Your Stocks Make the Top 10 List?\\nIt is an uncomfortable question, and there is now an answer to it.\\n24/7 Wall St has helped investors make money for over two decades, and our top analysts just finished ranking the definitive Top 10 Stocks To Buy Now. Not the ten biggest companies. Not the ten everyone is arguing about. The ten best stocks to buy right now.\\nOpen your account and look at what you own. Some of it you bought for a reason you could still defend today. Some of it you bought years ago for a reason you can no longer remember.\\nThe report is free. Put the ten next to what you own and find out which is which.\\nFree from 24/7 Wall St. It lands in your inbox.\\nJensen Huang confirmed on the Q2 FY27 call how central open models have become to NVIDIA\\'s story, listing TML, Mistral, Quinn, Kimi, GLM, DeepSeq, Minimax and Nemotron as leading open models running on his hardware. He said, \"Nearly all open models run on NVIDIA.\" Hugging Face is where that ecosystem physically lives.\\nBloxham described the defensive rationale: \"The AI market is controlled by a small number of closed source AI platforms, the likes of OpenAI and Anthropic, and even Google, and what we are seeing is a lot of those companies are developing their own AI hardware capability, which would potentially rival NVIDIA.\"\\nBroadcom ( NASDAQ:AVGO ) said on its Q3 FY26 call that OpenAI\\'s custom accelerator, Jalapeno, will be deployed at 1.3 gigawatts in 2027, and that Google TPU supply will be in the tens of billions of dollars annually. Anthropic is partnered with both Broadcom and AMD ( NASDAQ:AMD ), with AMD saying it will deploy up to 2 GW of MI450 in Helios racks. Every hyperscaler that fills a data center with its own silicon is a customer NVIDIA loses at the margin.\\nNVIDIA\\'s real moat has always been CUDA and the software layer that made its GPUs the default target for anyone building anything. Bloxham described what NVIDIA is trying to protect: \"By buying that, they can continue to influence that part of the community and obviously start to influence more what technology those companies use, as well as selling AI hardware.\"\\nOwning the distribution point for open models means every startup, sovereign, and enterprise builder that pulls a model down is doing so from an NVIDIA property. Per NVIDIA\\'s Q2 FY27 8-K, Data Center revenue was $89.02 billion, up 117% YoY. Protecting the top of the funnel that feeds that number is worth a lot.\\nThe reported $14 billion price buys very little revenue, but it buys influence over which frameworks, model formats, and hardware targets receive first-class support on the platform where the community congregates, according to Bloomberg Intelligence.\\nAn open-source commons derives its value from being seen as neutral ground. A chip vendor owning it gives AMD, Broadcom, and every custom-silicon program a genuine reason to fund a fork or a rival hub. AMD is already investing in its own developer platform, Rackham.ai, and said: \"more than 3 million models now run out of the box on AMD.\" The moment Hugging Face is perceived as an NVIDIA property, that number becomes a marketing weapon.\\nAMD is the merchant-silicon alternative. Its Q2 FY26 data center revenue of $6.72 billion (+107% YoY) is real, but it trades at a P/E of ~172x. Broadcom sells co-designed accelerators to hyperscalers looking to move away from NVIDIA, guiding to $21.7 billion in Q4 AI semiconductor revenue.\\nNVIDIA still has the platform. It trades at a P/E of ~45x with an operating margin of ~60%, and guided Q3 revenue of ~$108 billion. Buying Hugging Face is a defensive move priced as a strategic one, and it is probably the right call. Shareholders should track whether the community accepts the new landlord.\\nIf you have cash sitting in your account right now, give this two minutes. After more than two decades of helping investors beat the market, our top analysts at 24/7 Wall St. put together a definitive report on the Top 10 Stocks To Buy Today.',)\n\n### gnews:c4d9dea79dca5997aa4f4d03 \n ('SpaceX ( SPCX +0.65% ) held its first earnings call as a publicly traded company on Aug. 4, and CEO Elon Musk used it to place the company\\'s largest capital outlay, artificial intelligence (AI) computing, in the hands of a single supplier.\\nMusk said, \"going forward, we\\'ve decided to build exclusively on Nvidia because we think the Vera Rubin architecture is the best architecture. We think it\\'s the best AI computer, and we greatly value our close cooperation and partnership on many levels with Nvidia. So, we\\'re exclusive to Nvidia.\"\\nNvidia ( NVDA +2.53% ) shares closed up 3.4% the next day. Advanced Micro Devices , the closest alternative supplier of graphics processing units (GPUs) for AI, closed down 7% after reporting its own quarterly results that same afternoon.\\nNvidia is also a shareholder, with about $21 billion in SpaceX shares at the end of June.\\nFor SpaceX shareholders, the most interesting figure is the bill. What gets locked in by building it with a single supplier?\\nImage source: The White House.\\nHow much computing capacity does Musk promise?\\nSpaceX\\'s capital expenditures were $18.4 billion in the second quarter, and $15.8 billion of that was allocated to AI computing infrastructure. The AI figure was $7.7 billion in the first quarter and $749 million a year earlier. In other words, the computing line item grew more than 20 times year over year and now absorbs 86 cents of every capital dollar.\\nCFO Bret Johnsen told analysts to expect the next two quarters to be \"very similar to the current quarter\" in terms of capital expenditures, probably about $37 billion more this year.\\nSpaceX ended June with 1.4 gigawatts of installed computing capacity, compared to 1 gigawatt in March and 0.4 gigawatts a year earlier. Musk expects the company to end 2026 with more than 2 gigawatts. And by the end of 2027, he said, the total \"may, let\\'s say, be closer to 10 gigawatts of compute than 5 gigawatts of compute.\"\\nUnder its commitment, every gigawatt built from now on will use Nvidia hardware.\\nThe 10-Q SpaceX filed on the day of the conference does not mention Nvidia, nor has any subsequent filing.\\nWhat it does show is $28 billion in noncancelable purchase commitments at the end of June, of which $22.2 billion mature in 2027, described mostly as AI infrastructure, cloud capacity, and its spectrum purchase.\\nDuring the conference, when asked how much confidence he had regarding the chips, Musk said, \"our understanding with NVIDIA is that we will receive a very significant percent of their GPUs next year.\"\\nCustomer contracts, on the other hand, specify Nvidia chips, and I would argue they say more about SpaceX\\'s tie to Nvidia than the commitment does. SpaceX\\'s cloud service agreements with Anthropic cover about 325,000 Nvidia GPUs at $1.25 billion monthly through May 2029. Its agreement with Google, of Alphabet , covers about 110,000 Nvidia GPUs at $920 million monthly from October 2026 through June 2029. Each can be terminated with 90 days\\' notice after an initial period. And if SpaceX does not deliver the committed GPUs by Sept. 30, Google could walk away after a one-month grace period or pay only for the GPUs delivered.\\nSo SpaceX has sold Nvidia capacity it has not yet finished buying, with delivery dates.\\nWhat SpaceX gives up without a second bid\\nA buyer of this size gives up two things.\\nThe first is price. Nvidia\\'s gross margin was 75% in its quarter ended July 26: on average, three-quarters of what customers pay Nvidia is gross profit.\\nThe second is the timeline. Nvidia said in its quarterly report that it is \"currently experiencing certain supply constraints,\" and Vera Rubin did not begin production shipments until the quarter that started on July 27. SpaceX\\'s 2-gigawatt and 10-gigawatt targets depend on how much a single supplier ships of a product with limited supply.\\nTerafab, the chip plant that SpaceX is planning with partners, is its hedge against shortages, but the prospectus says there are still no definitive agreements.\\nOf course, management\\'s answer is that profitability arrives quickly. Johnsen said current cloud economics provide SpaceX with \"less than a one-year payback\" on new capital allocated to computing, and the company signed contracts for another $6.7 billion in cloud service revenue during the first weeks of the third quarter. If that holds, paying more for the best computer could be the right decision.\\nBut the stock arguably already assumes it will hold. SpaceX\\'s market value sits near $1.9 trillion, with shares around $142 at the time of writing, more than 60 times the revenue a full year would produce at the second-quarter run rate. That price leaves no room for the bill to be larger or arrive later than planned, and SpaceX has committed to building it all on a single supplier\\'s hardware.\\nBy Daniel Sparks – Sep 4, 2026 at 5:43PM EST\\nMusk expects the company to end 2026 with more than 2 gigawatts of computing capacity and to get closer to 10 gigawatts by the end of 2027.',)\n\n### gnews:db5c4d64cdbdd661f0e35836 \n ('22.4M Views · 394 Posts\\n· 17x EBITDA implies zero growth after 2027 — absurd.\\n· Inference share rising; the bigger market, expanding.\\n· AMD at 53x vs. NVIDIA at 17x — mispriced.\\n· AI winter favors the default: NVIDIA\\'s proven stack.\\n· 30%+ margin of safety with 45% earnings growth.\\nOpening: A Question That\\'s Been Bugging Me for Three Months\\nHonestly, I\\'ve been wrestling with one question since April — what the hell is going on with NVIDIA?\\nThe Philadelphia Semiconductor Index is up 71% this year. AMD has more than doubled. Micron is up over 200%. And the undisputed king of the AI era ? Up 10%. Ten percent. You could\\'ve bought a random semiconductor ETF and easily outperformed it.\\nAt first, I assumed something was wrong with the fundamentals. Pulled up the financials — nothing. Next fiscal year consensus expects 42% revenue growth , EPS growth north of 45%. Then I looked at valuation — 17x EBITDA , versus a five-year average of 36x. Cut in half. My first reaction was: I must have made a calculation error.\\nBut I didn\\'t. That\\'s exactly how the market is pricing it.\\nSo today, I want to work through this carefully: has NVIDIA truly \"aged out,\" or is the market making a collective mistake?\\nWhat Is the Market Afraid Of?\\nThree layers of bear logic — none entirely unreasonable.\\nGabelli Funds\\' John Belton put it bluntly: investors want \"the strongest supply-demand imbalances and untapped growth,\" and \"NVIDIA currently meets neither criteria.\"\\nI understand this logic. I disagree with the conclusion.\\nWhy I Think the Market Is Overly Pessimistic\\nLet me reframe what the current valuation actually implies.\\nMorningstar\\'s Brian Colello did an interesting reverse-engineering exercise: a stock price of $212 implies that NVIDIA will have virtually zero growth after 2027 . Zero. A company that dominates AI inference market share, whose downstream cloud customers are still aggressively expanding capex — and it just stops growing in two years?\\nThat\\'s not conservatism. That\\'s pricing in a catastrophe.\\nBut what\\'s reality? Alphabet just raised its 2026 capex guidance this week and said it would increase further next year. Microsoft and Amazon show no signs of decelerating their capital spending. AI application deployment is slower than expected, but the direction hasn\\'t reversed. You can reasonably argue that growth rates will decelerate from 90% to 40%. But pricing a decline from 40% straight to 0% ? The gap between those two assumptions — that\\'s where the opportunity lives.\\nColello\\'s fair value estimate is $280 , more than 30% above the current price. Personally, I think even that\\'s conservative — if NVIDIA truly delivers 45%+ EPS growth for the next two years, 17x EBITDA will look absurd in hindsight .\\nA Signal Most People Missed: Inference Market Share Is Rising\\nI want to highlight this separately because most people haven\\'t noticed it.\\nAccording to The Information, NVIDIA\\'s share in the inference chip market — the chips used to run models rather than train them — is actually going up . Not flat. Not slightly declining. Rising.\\nWhy does this matter? Because inference is the endgame of AI commercialization . Training is a one-time event (albeit iterative), but inference is continuous — happening every second of every day. Long-term, the inference market will dwarf training in scale. If NVIDIA is expanding its share in this larger pie, then the market\\'s \"share has peaked → growth stalls\" narrative breaks at the very first link in the chain .\\nI think this might be the single most underappreciated fact about NVIDIA right now.\\nOn AMD: I Admit It\\'s Impressive, But the Valuation Makes Me Nervous\\nI need to be honest — $Advanced Micro Devices (AMD.US)$ \\'s execution this year has been genuinely impressive. Landing the 6GW Instinct GPU agreement with OpenAI , Oracle ordering 50,000 MI450 chips, ROCm 7.0 finally achieving meaningful compatibility breakthroughs — these are all legitimate \"zero-to-one\" milestones , and a stock doubling on the back of that is a rational market response.\\nBut 53x forward P/E ? For a company with projected 2027 revenue of just $78 billion?\\nLet me run a simple thought experiment: if the market believes \"the AI pie is large enough to feed both NVIDIA and AMD,\" then that thesis should be more bullish for NVIDIA — it has the larger share, the deeper ecosystem, the stronger customer stickiness. Yet the market gives AMD 53x and NVIDIA 17x. What this effectively says is: AMD\\'s best days are just beginning; NVIDIA\\'s best days are already over.\\nIs it possible this judgment is correct? Sure. But it requires one precondition — that NVIDIA\\'s moat is substantively breached within two years. And I don\\'t currently see evidence that this precondition is materializing. AMD won big contracts, yes, but NVIDIA\\'s contracts are bigger. AMD has system-level products now, yes, but NVIDIA has been building its system-level ecosystem for over a decade .\\nSo my personal take is this: AMD is worth owning, but chasing it at current valuations requires extremely strong conviction. NVIDIA is the opposite — you don\\'t need much conviction at all. You just need to believe \"it\\'s not dead.\"\\nA Counterintuitive Take: NVIDIA Might Be Safest in an AI Winter\\nThis is something I\\'ve been thinking about a lot recently, and I want to put it out there for discussion.\\nSuppose the worst-case scenario materializes — AI investment hype fades, cloud providers slash capex, and a genuine \" AI winter \" arrives. Who gets hurt the most?\\nIntuitively, you\\'d say NVIDIA, because it\\'s the biggest. But think carefully — the answer might be the exact opposite .\\nWhat\\'s the first budget to get cut in a downturn? The experimental, unproven bets . Meta\\'s custom chip project, Microsoft\\'s in-house accelerators, various startups\\' vaporware — in boom times, people are willing to burn cash trying these things. But in a contraction, the CFO\\'s first instinct is: \"Kill the custom project. Go back to NVIDIA\\'s proven solution. At least we know it works.\"\\nThis is NVIDIA\\'s countercyclical resilience . Its products have undergone the most rigorous market validation. Its CUDA ecosystem has the largest developer base. It is the \"default option.\" In good times, everyone wants to escape the default. In bad times, everyone runs back to it .\\nNVIDIA might be the most \"boring\" position during an AI boom, but the most \"reassuring\" one during an AI winter.\\n– Oracle has already admitted that renting out NVIDIA GPUs barely breaks even. If more cloud providers reach the same conclusion — \"we bought the chips but can\\'t make money\" — capex growth could hit a wall suddenly . This is the biggest systemic risk.\\n– If AMD\\'s Helios ships successfully and meets performance targets , it will be the first time NVIDIA faces direct competition at the full-system level — not just individual GPUs. This threatens NVIDIA\\'s solution-level pricing premium.\\n– Ongoing tightening of export controls limits NVIDIA\\'s total addressable market. China was once an enormous growth vector; it\\'s now essentially locked out.\\n– 17x might not be the floor. If the market truly completes the \"growth stock → value stock\" identity reclassification, valuations could compress further to 12-14x before bottoming. The mean reversion timeline might be longer than I expect.\\nIf I had to pick one semiconductor stock to hold for two years without touching it at current prices, I\\'d pick $NVIDIA (NVDA.US)$ over AMD $Advanced Micro Devices (AMD.US)$ or $Micron Technology (MU.US)$ . The reason is simple: I don\\'t need to bet that it wins the future. I only need to bet that \"the market is too pessimistic about it. \" $280 fair value vs. $206 current price — that\\'s a 30%+ margin of safety , layered on top of 45% annual earnings growth. Even if the multiple never re-rates, earnings growth alone will likely push the stock higher within two years.\\nAMD and Micron, while offering more upside optionality, have already priced in a lot of good news . If any single element disappoints — even just one product delay, one lost customer — the drawdown will be painful.\\nThis ultimately comes down to a preference question : do you like paying for \"possibility,\" or paying for \"certainty that\\'s being undervalued\"?\\nI choose the latter. But I completely understand those who choose the former.',)\n\n### gnews:f1af0b706051f98c7b8b3394 \n ('Shares of leading designer of graphics chips Nvidia (NASDAQ:NVDA) jumped 8.3% in the afternoon session after the company reported second-quarter financial results that topped Wall Street expectations and projected 70% revenue growth for the next year (fiscal 2028).\\nAccording to a company press release and earnings commentary, Nvidia reported revenue of $96.22 billion for the second quarter, up 106% year-over-year, alongside adjusted earnings per share of $2.22. Both figures surpassed Wall Street consensus estimates, which called for revenue of $92.37 billion and adjusted earnings of $2.09 per share. Growth was driven by unprecedented momentum in Data Center sales, which increased 18% sequentially to reach $89.0 billion. The underlying profitability was also incredibly strong, as adjusted operating income surged 124% year-over-year to $63.96 billion and operating margin expanded to 66.2% from 60.8% a year ago.\\nCEO Jensen Huang emphasized that rapid adoption of AI models is fueling massive infrastructure investments, noting accelerated growth not just from traditional hyperscalers, but from enterprise, sovereign, and AI-native start-up customers rapidly deploying Nvidia\\'s full-stack solutions. Furthermore, the networking segment posted record sequential growth powered by Spectrum-X Ethernet and InfiniBand, while the company\\'s new Vera CPU is expected to see revenue more than double next year.\\nLooking ahead, management guided for third-quarter revenue of $108 billion at the midpoint, comfortably above analysts\\' expectations of $104.6 billion. The chipmaker also projected 70% revenue growth for fiscal year 2028, reinforcing investor confidence in the sustained demand for artificial intelligence hardware.\\nCrucially, CFO Colette Kress explained that customer forecasts actually point to potential growth rates near double the guided 70%, but severe supply chain bottlenecks—ranging from memory availability to data center power and construction—are capping the company\\'s near-term output. To secure long-term demand and alleviate these constraints, Nvidia highlighted new strategic revenue-sharing structures with \"NeoCloud\" partners to facilitate infrastructure financing and establish massive recurring revenue streams.\\nNvidia\\'s shares are not very volatile and have only had 7 moves greater than 5% over the last year. In that context, today\\'s move indicates the market considers this news meaningful, although it might not be something that would fundamentally change its perception of the business.\\nThe previous big move we wrote about was 15 days ago when the stock gained 3% on the news that a wave of upbeat earnings reports and bullish forecasts from key industry players signaled robust and sustained demand for artificial intelligence technology.\\nThe rally was sparked by strong quarterly results from several AI-related firms, reinforcing investor confidence in the sector. Super Micro Computer, a seller of servers and other AI equipment, saw its shares jump after its earnings per share came in 84% higher than expected and it provided a revenue forecast that topped estimates.\\nSimilarly, CoreWeave, which provides cloud computing for AI systems, rallied after its sales forecast also exceeded expectations. This positive sentiment echoed globally, with Asian markets gaining as traders bought into AI- and semiconductor-related shares.\\nThe optimism is further supported by fundamental data, such as a reported 155% year-over-year surge in South Korea\\'s semiconductor exports for early August.AI racks consume GPUs, CPUs, high-bandwidth memory, and storage.\\nWhen a large server OEM and a major AI cloud operator both lift the path of future shipments, investors typically reprice the chip makers that supply those systems which helps explain the gains in Nvidia, AMD, and Intel.\\nNvidia is up 21.6% since the beginning of the year, and at $229.73 per share, it is trading close to its 52-week high of $235.74 from May 2026. Investors who bought $1,000 worth of Nvidia\\'s shares 5 years ago would now be looking at an investment worth $10,149.\\nONE MORE THING: The $21 AI Application Stock Wall Street Forgot. While Wall Street obsesses over who\\'s building AI, one company is already using it to print money. And nobody\\'s paying attention.\\nAI chip stocks trade at ridiculous valuations. This company processes a trillion consumer signals monthly using AI and trades at a third of the price. The gap won\\'t last. The institutions will figure it out. You need to see this first. Read the FREE Report Before They Notice .',)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3; a=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/identity-probe/v2/candidates-v2-next.json\\\"))[\\\"categories\\\"][\\\"T3 one price compared across two years\\\"][\\\"order\\\"]; c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); [print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",x[\\\"input_id\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",c.execute(\\\"select text from items where input_id=?\\\",(x[\\\"input_id\\\"],)).fetchone()) for x in a[10:15]]'\"","aggregated_output":"\n### gnews:8697580fd93d9a51eefd9876 \n (\"It has done it again. Nvidia ( NVDA +2.53% ) blew past Wall Street estimates in its second-quarter (Q2) earnings, sending the stock back toward its all-time high share price of $225. The chipmaker now has a market cap approaching $5.5 trillion, making it the largest company in the world.\\nWhere Nvidia truly shone was its guidance for the next fiscal year, which has astounded investors. In fact, it could put the company on the doorstep of becoming the first business in world history to generate $1 trillion in annual revenue, up from well under $100 billion just a few years ago.\\nBut does that make the stock a buy today? Let's find out.\\nFor context, let's give some numbers around Nvidia's current financial performance. Over the last 12 months, it has generated $303 billion in revenue. Last quarter, which ended in August, saw revenue grow 106% to $106 billion. If this type of growth continues for the next two quarters of this fiscal year (which ends in January of 2027), then Nvidia is likely knocking on the door of generating over $400 billion in sales in a 12-month period.\\nWhat was even more impressive was the fact that Nvidia is already projecting 70% revenue growth next fiscal year, which is named fiscal year 2028. This is due to the insatiable demand from the cloud providers for artificial intelligence ( AI) computer chip systems, neoclouds like Space Exploration Technologies , and Nvidia raising prices. Add 70% growth to the $400 billion in fiscal year 2027 revenue, and Nvidia may generate $700 billion to $800 billion in revenue next fiscal year.\\nThat is up from just $27 billion in revenue in fiscal year 2023, only a few years ago.\\nThe math behind $1 trillion in revenue\\nIf Nvidia can grow its sales by 70% next fiscal year to $700 billion, it will only need around 40% revenue growth in fiscal year 2029 to eclipse $1 trillion in sales. This feels highly achievable if AI infrastructure investments continue to grow at today's pace.\\nBut will Nvidia be the first stock to $1 trillion? That is a harder question to answer because of Amazon ( AMZN +0.56% ) . Amazon is already at $775 billion in revenue, meaning it will only need two years of 15% revenue growth to eclipse $1 trillion. Its sales grew 20% year over year last quarter, mainly due to AI demand. What this means is that if Nvidia keeps growing revenue at this pace, Amazon will likely do the same and reach $1 trillion in revenue first.\\nEither way, both companies -- but especially Nvidia -- are growing rapidly today because of AI.\\nIs Nvidia stock a buy?\\nUnlike Amazon, whose capital-intensive e-commerce and cloud operations require significant capital spending , Nvidia can offload capital spending to its hardware providers, allowing it to generate fantastic profit margins . Over the last 12 months, it has posted an operating margin of 65%, implying $650 billion in operating earnings in fiscal year 2029 if this margin is maintained and revenue grows to $1 trillion.\\nThat would be up from $197 billion over the last 12 months, making Nvidia by far the most profitable business in the world. The final question is: How durable would this $1 trillion in revenue and $650 billion in earnings be? If spending on the AI boom slows -- which may happen within the next 10 years -- Nvidia's revenue could be significantly lower in the near future.\\nThis makes the stock very difficult to value today, especially given its $5.5 trillion market cap. That looks cheap compared to $650 billion in earnings a few years from now, but who's to say what the average earnings power for Nvidia will be every year for the next decade?\\nBy Brett Schafer – Aug 31, 2026 at 4:15AM EST\",)\n\n### gnews:a1332af414b479a05316413f \n ('Apple Just Reclaimed the World\\'s Most Valuable Company Title From Nvidia. Here Is What the Tech Rotation Means for Enterprise Strategy.\\nApple has overtaken Nvidia to become the world\\'s most valuable company, valued at $4.91 trillion, due to a shift in tech investments towards its ecosystem. This change underscores a longer-term preference for stable ecosystems over AI infrastructure in enterprise strategies.\\nThis story was produced through MarketScale . See how Software & Technology teams put it to work with Executive Thought Leadership .\\nKey facts, context, and what it means.\\nApple is valued at $4.91 trillion, surpassing Nvidia.\\nInvestment is shifting from AI infrastructure to durable technology ecosystems.\\nEnterprise strategies may need to focus more on stable technology ecosystems.\\nTurn your Software & Technology expertise into content.\\nThe B2B signal behind the market rotation\\nFor nearly a year, Nvidia owned the top of the market. On Friday, July 17, 2026, Apple took it back.\\nApple reclaimed its title as the world\\'s most valuable publicly traded company this morning, surpassing Nvidia by market capitalization as a technology rotation sent semiconductor stocks lower and Apple shares touched new highs. Apple\\'s market capitalization reached $4.91 trillion, while Nvidia slipped to approximately $4.83 trillion following a 3.5% decline in its share price (CNBC, July 17, 2026; Wall Street Journal via MacTech, July 17, 2026).\\nApple last held the title in April 2025. Nvidia had held it since June 2025, when it surpassed Microsoft, and in October 2025 became the first company in history to cross the $5 trillion market capitalization threshold (Forbes, July 17, 2026).\\nApple\\'s stock has risen approximately 57.8% over the past year, gaining 23% in 2026 alone with a 15.7% gain just in July (Wall Street Journal via MacTech, July 17, 2026; Schaeffer\\'s Investment Research, July 17, 2026).\\nThe rotation reflects a meaningful change in how investors are valuing AI exposure. Nvidia\\'s ascent was built on the thesis that the companies building AI infrastructure, specifically the GPU chips that power model training and inference, would capture the largest share of AI economic value. That thesis drove Nvidia from a $1 trillion company in mid-2023 to the first $5 trillion company in history in under three years.\\nWhat changed on Friday is that investors began pricing a different thesis: that the companies best positioned to monetize AI at scale through distribution, services, and consumer relationships may generate more durable earnings than the infrastructure layer alone. The Philadelphia SE Semiconductor index has fallen almost 19% from its all-time highs, and chip stocks are heading for their worst weekly performance in more than a year (BNN Bloomberg via Reuters, July 17, 2026).\\n\"Apple was seen as a laggard in the AI race because it wasn\\'t spending to develop models, but now sentiment has changed,\" said Toni Meadows, head of investment at BRI Wealth Management, in comments to Reuters (Reuters via BNN Bloomberg, July 17, 2026).\\nThe WSJ framed Apple\\'s position plainly: Apple has shown a steadier upward trajectory, driven by investor confidence that its dominant consumer position can solve most problems. It may have lagged behind in AI, but that has not prompted iPhone users to switch to Android devices (Wall Street Journal via MacTech, July 17, 2026).\\nA key factor behind the sentiment shift is Apple\\'s accelerating AI roadmap. The company is preparing for what 9to5Mac described as a major leadership transition, alongside the launch of a significantly updated Siri AI expected this fall, representing Apple\\'s most substantial intelligence platform update in years (9to5Mac, July 17, 2026).\\nApple is also advancing its hardware roadmap on multiple fronts. Bloomberg reported that Apple is preparing an OLED iPad Mini alongside iPad Air and low-end iPad updates planned for 2027, expanding the premium display tier across its tablet lineup. Apple has already begun selling refurbished iPad Mini units with the A17 Pro chip at a discount through its official refurbished store, according to MacRumors (Bloomberg, July 16, 2026; MacRumors, July 16, 2026).\\nThe company\\'s approach to AI differs structurally from its competitors. Rather than investing heavily in model development, Apple has pursued on-device intelligence, privacy-first architecture, and ecosystem integration, distributing AI capabilities through the hardware and services layer it already controls rather than racing to build frontier models from scratch.\\nThe Apple-Nvidia market cap swap is not just a financial story. It is a signal about where enterprise technology investment is heading in the second half of 2026.\\nThe first phase of enterprise AI investment, from 2023 through early 2026, was dominated by infrastructure: GPU procurement, data center buildout, model training, and cloud capacity expansion. That phase rewarded Nvidia, hyperscalers, and the companies building the physical and software stack that AI runs on.\\nThe rotation now underway reflects growing confidence that the second phase, monetization through distribution, devices, and services, is beginning to materialize. Apple\\'s ecosystem of approximately 2.2 billion active devices represents one of the largest and most loyal distribution networks in the history of consumer technology.\\nFor B2B technology leaders, the rotation carries a practical planning signal. Enterprise hardware refresh cycles, software procurement decisions, and device management strategies are all influenced by where Apple takes its AI platform over the next 12 to 18 months. The new Siri AI this fall, the OLED iPad Mini in 2027, and the broader Apple Intelligence rollout across Mac, iPad, and iPhone represent a sustained enterprise touchpoint expansion that IT and procurement teams should be tracking now, not after the product launches.\\nThe $5 trillion threshold is next. Apple\\'s stock touched $4.9 trillion on Friday and the company\\'s trajectory points toward joining Nvidia as the only companies in history to cross that mark (9to5Mac, July 17, 2026).\\nEvery story in MarketScale Software & Technology starts with a company putting its solutions engineers, product teams, and customer engineers on the record. Buyers are already reading this topic. The only question is whose experts they find.\\nBuyers ask AI engines who to consider, and published expert answers are what those engines cite.\\nMarketScale gives Software & Technology B2B marketing teams a full content studio: record, produce, and distribute your own channel. No agency, no crew, no guessing.\\nBefore they reach out, Software & Technology buyers ask AI engines which vendors to trust. Explore how your experts, customers, and partners can become useful content for buyers and AI search.\\nThis article was produced through MarketScale. The same platform turns your solutions engineers, product teams, and customer engineers into the articles, video, and social content Software & Technology buyers are searching for. Start a free trial and see it with your own people. For qualifying companies, no credit card, no demo required.\\nUber deployed ChatGPT Work to more than 30,000 employees seven weeks after cutting about 3,300 jobs (10% of corporate staff). The company framed layoffs as organizational restructuring, while the rollout expands a pilot that embedded AI engineers with teams to automate pieces of their work.\\n01 Uber expanded ChatGPT Work from a 1,000-person pilot to over 30,000 employees across operations, finance, marketing, and product teams.\\n02 During the pilot, 80% of early adopters were non-engineers, and AI pods of 30 engineers paired with teams to automate work such as analyzing operational data, localizing training materials, and building dashboards.\\n03 Uber’s rollout shows AI deployments can go beyond licenses: curated integrations, embedded technical support, an internal skills library, and plans to measure adoption and outcomes.\\nTwo critical unauthenticated remote code execution flaws in PHP (CVE-2026-109618 and CVE-2026-109619) with CVSS 9.2 scores affected 34.4 million vulnerable services as of October 23, with no patches available for PHP 8.1 and older. Exploitation attempts reached millions within hours of disclosure, though confirmed compromises remain unproven, while attackers tested a Mirai-derived botnet variant against production servers.\\nUNICOM Engineering chief technologist Austin Hypes compares rear door, single-phase, two-phase and immersion cooling for AI racks on Flawless Execution. He says two-phase cold plates use dielectric refrigerant and can avoid chillers and evaporative towers in hot, dry sites, and immersion can keep servers up ten to 15 minutes and sometimes an hour or more after a cooling loss. Schneider Electric engineers cite estimates that single-phase holds about 55% of the 2026 liquid cooling market.\\n01 A Motivair by Schneider Electric engineer puts single-phase cold plates at an estimated 55% of the 2026 liquid cooling market and expects them to dominate for five to 10 years.\\nStories like this one run on content MarketScale captures from real practitioners. See how your team\\'s expertise becomes coverage in Software & Technology and beyond.',)\n\n### gnews:73b5d87b6d484d8e54c69370 \n ('Korean fried chicken with the chairmen of SK Group and LG . Yellowtail sashimi at Nobu with Larry Ellison and Elon Musk. Mapo tofu and whiskey with TSMC founder Morris Chang.\\nPeople are so curious about where Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang eats, there\\'s a blog that tracks his sumptuous dinners out around the world. But the real tea, said Huang during the chipmaker\\'s blowout second-quarter earnings call on Wednesday, is who he dines with.\\n\"One of the funnest things to do is just to go figure out where I go for dinner, and who I have dinner with,\" said Huang during the quarterly call that saw Nvidia\\'s stock jump more than 4% in after-hours trading. \"Their stock price doubles the next day.\"\\nHuang was responding to a request from an analyst at Goldman Sachs to rank the most acute constraints on Nvidia\\'s ability to meet customer demand, against the backdrop of the blockbuster 70% revenue growth the company projected for next fiscal year. The figure was significantly higher than analysts\\' 44% estimate, but Huang and Nvidia CFO Colette Kress specified that there were still heavy supply constraints holding back the $5 trillion chipmaker. The analyst asked Huang to rank items like data center power, shell availability, memory pricing, and wafer foundry access that might be limiting growth.\\n\"There\\'s something funny I could say, but I\\'m going to just not,\" quipped Huang. \"I think the answer is our entire supply chain is challenged, and everybody is really running flat out and more capacity is coming online all the time, which is one of the advantages.\"\\nNvidia currently has supply for 70% of the demand from current customers, said Huang during the call, adding that demand is \"much higher than that.\" He said the company doesn\\'t like to disappoint customers but it will take the help of the entire supply chain to get more compute capacity available.\\nThe tech giant jolted investors on Wednesday with a blockbuster second quarter earnings report, posting $96.2 billion in revenue, well above analysts\\' $92.2 billion consensus estimate. Nvidia also projected that it would grow revenue 70% next year, meaning revenue could soar more than $100 billion above where analysts were modeling. The stock rallied more than 4% on the preliminary forecast.\\nHuang didn\\'t name a restaurant, dinner companion or supplier, for that matter, but the Nvidia CEO famously eats dinner out while he travels around the globe and photographers have caught him tasting local dishes, tipping servers, and taking photos with crowds outside of restaurants and night markets where he eats.\\nIn 2024, Oracle founder Ellison said he and SpaceX CEO Musk had taken Huang out to dinner at Nobu Palo Alto and \"begged\" Huang for more GPUs.\\n\"I would describe the dinner as Oracle—me and Elon begging Jensen for GPUs,\" Ellison recalled in 2024. \"Please take our money. Please take our money. By the way, I got dinner. No, no, take more of it. We need you to take more of our money please.\"\\nIn June, the \"Jensen Eats\" blog reported he stopped over in Korea and visited a barbecue restaurant with a name that translates to \" Hey brother, it\\'s me! .\" He ate with SK Group Chairman Chey Tae-won, LG Group Chairman Koo Kwang-mo, and Naver founder Lee Hae-jin. According to tech founder Dan Liu, who writes the blog, the crew dined on grilled meat wrapped in perilla leaves, a dish called ssam. Liu wrote that because LG\\'s Koo is the youngest, he grilled pork for the group.\\nIn the run-up to the group outing , LG rose by 30% on reports that Koo would meet with Huang, and LG affiliates followed suit with increases for its listed units ranging from 17% to 29%.\\nThe halo effect even extends to where Huang eats. He had chicken and beers with Samsung Electronics chairman Jay Y Lee and Hyundai Motor executive chair Chung Euisun last October and shares of poultry processing company Cherrybro rose, along with fried chicken company Kyochon F&B. Co., and fried-chicken robot company Neuromeka Co., Bloomberg reported .\\nAs for meeting customer demand, Huang said Nvidia is trying to be as transparent as possible as more capacity comes online in dribs and drabs every day. He did not, however, disclose where he\\'s eating dinner tonight.',)\n\n### gnews:f0ded626a98986d022fd2c30 \n (\"Nvidia ( NVDA -4.40% ) was a relatively obscure chipmaker specializing in gaming technology before artificial intelligence (AI) exploded a few years ago, sending the stock to the No. 1 spot as the world's most valuable company. It held on to that title for most of 2025 as Nvidia enjoyed outstanding growth, while the next-most-valuable company, Apple , struggled to gain traction in AI.\\nHowever, Apple stock is back in the market's favor, gaining 27% over the past three months, while Nvidia stock has dropped 8%. Apple is now back in the world's most-valuable-company slot.\\nAs the market hears more updates from the largest tech companies, is there more bad news on the way for Nvidia?\\nAI chip stocks have been plummeting recently as the market prices in worries about AI spending and how long the cycle can last. However, while companies like Alphabet and Amazon continue to spend lavishly on upgrading their AI platforms, with both companies planning to spend around $200 billion in 2026 alone, the updates are actually a good sign for Nvidia.\\nNvidia works with both Amazon and Alphabet, as well as nearly every major AI company. Both clients, for example, make some of their own AI chips, but they also contract with Nvidia to offer their cloud clients the best available AI development platform. Nvidia is the gold standard , and while Amazon's chips offer budget options and Alphabet's Tensor Processing Units (TPUs) offer specialized task efficiency, Nvidia offers the most powerful capabilities. As these companies invest, a large chunk goes to Nvidia.\\nNvidia's CEO Jensen Huang noted that total AI spend is expected to be $1 trillion next year and $3 trillion to $4 trillion by the end of the decade. He thinks that Nvidia will generate $1 trillion in revenue from 2025 through 2027 from the Blackwell and Rubin lines alone, and every time a hyperscaler announces greater investments in AI infrastructure, investors should envision some of that landing on Nvidia's doorstep.\\nAlthough Nvidia should enjoy continued growth if AI spending stays strong, the market is worried that this can't continue. A separate but connected piece for Nvidia is that revenue growth is accelerating right now, but as soon as that slows down, the stock won't be worth quite as much. On a price-to-sales basis, Nvidia stock trades at 19 times trailing-12-month sales.\\nNvidia may continue to face pressure as the market weighs the future of AI, and investors should temper their expectations in the near term.\\nBy Jennifer Saibil – Aug 1, 2026 at 5:25AM EST\",)\n\n### gnews:c2fdbe0959518bba7d3baa10 \n ('Why So Many Value ETFs Are Loaded with Big Tech\\nAre Nvidia’s Growing Circular Financing Deals a Concern?\\nCybersecurity Stocks Riding the AI (Danger) Boom\\nMarkets Brief: Value ETFs Making a Big Tech Bet, Nvidia’s Dealmaking, and the Cybersecurity Stock Outlook\\nThe ripples from the AI stock boom are even showing up in value-stock ETFs.\\niShares Russell 1000 Value ETF\\n(ILCV)\\nThis article mentions funds that have an issuer-initiated rating and/or track a Morningstar Index. For full disclosure information, please refer to the specific funds, which are demarcated with a * symbol, listed below.\\nStocks are heading into September with the Morningstar US Total Market Index up a respectable 14% for the year. After a volatile start to 2026, we’re seeing yet another run driven by technology stocks, particularly the names seen benefiting the most from the artificial intelligence buildout.\\nAs we have been documenting , market concentration within tech has only increased this year, and not decreased as many strategists expected. This week’s Markets Brief looks at how it’s getting harder for investors to diversify away from tech. Even value funds may not be a haven. Also on tap is a deeper dive into Nvidia’s NVDA financing deals, which have some investors nervous, as well as a look at why cybersecurity stocks are turning into AI winners.\\nInvestors turning to value-stock strategies often try to counterbalance the growth side of a portfolio heavy in volatile tech stocks. But investors in many value-stock ETFs may be making much bigger bets on tech than they realize. “We are seeing more and more stocks that people think of as large growth and tech being included in value indices and ETFs,” explains Morningstar associate manager research analyst Brian Paoli.\\nOne example of this trend is the $84 billion iShares Russell 1000 Value ETF IWD , the fourth-largest ETF in the Morningstar large value category. These days, it has about 20% of its portfolio in technology stocks (using Morningstar sector designations), with Apple AAPL as its second-largest position and Microsoft MSFT ranking third. Meanwhile, the ETF’s largest position is a roughly 6% weighting in Amazon AMZN , which is classified as a consumer cyclical rather than a tech stock. In other words, more than a quarter of the iShares Russell 1000 Value ETF is in tech-related investments.\\nIt wasn’t always this way. The amount of technology stocks in the iShares strategy has risen sharply over the past year and a half. At the end of 2024, tech was 11% of the fund.\\nAt the same time, technology stocks have played a greater role in driving performance. In 2024, financials, including JPMorgan JPM and Berkshire Hathaway BRK.A , were the ETF’s top two contributors, followed by Walmart WMT and GE Aerospace GE . So far in 2026, tech stocks have been the catalyst. Out of the fund’s 23.8% year-to-date return, they have contributed 11.5 percentage points, with Amazon adding another 0.84 points. The top seven contributors to the fund’s returns in 2026 have been tech stocks, along with Amazon. (Apple clocks in at number 10.)\\nThe iShares Russell 1000 Value ETF is far from alone. The $50 billion iShares S&P 500 Value ETF IVE clocks in at nearly 21% tech stocks, and the iShares Morningstar Value ETF\\nat nearly 24%. But these don’t even crack the top 40 in terms of tech exposure among large value ETFs with more than $100 million in assets. The largest value-stock ETF, the $256 billion Vanguard Morningstar Value ETF, is at the low end, with 13% tech.\\nWhy is Big Tech appearing in value strategies even as Apple and Microsoft have been growing earnings at an annual rate of around 20%? Paoli says the answer lies in the intersection of stock characterization methodologies and market dynamics. Value and growth designations are relative, not based on absolute numbers. Index providers (including Morningstar) evaluate stocks via different metrics and then rank them to determine the growth/value line. With some approaches, stocks with a blend of growth and value attributes end up in both value and growth indexes.\\nMeanwhile, the intense rally in semiconductor stocks has lifted both valuations and market caps at a time when their earnings growth has exploded. In addition, a large, fast-growing IPO like SpaceX SPCX can add to the ranks of stocks at the growth end of the spectrum. Together these factors have pushed relatively slower-growth and lower-valuation Big Tech names, such as Apple and Microsoft, to the value side.\\n“AI has become large enough to influence not only stock prices but also style classifications, sector composition, factor exposures, and benchmark concentration,” writes Scott Froidl, co-head of manager research at LPL Financial. “Technology now plays an increasingly important role in both growth and value indexes. The traditional boundaries separating investment styles are beginning to blur.”\\nWhen is a surprise not a surprise? When Nvidia blows away earnings forecasts. But a bigger question for many investors is the company’s web of financing arrangements with clients and other financial commitments totaling hundreds of billions of dollars. These deals were a big focus during the company’s conference call. “We recognize the scale of this support, and we know some will call this circular financing. We see it differently,” said Colette Kress, Nvidia’s chief financial officer.\\nBrian Colello, who follows Nvidia for Morningstar, has argued that these financing arrangements are a smart strategy for the giant chipmaker. Here are four of his key takeaways from the company’s conference call:\\nNvidia’s supply chain commitments spiked in the second quarter, rising to $279 billion versus $119 billion a quarter ago, as the company procured memory chips ahead of its Vera Rubin and Vera Rubin Ultra rack-scale product rollouts in the quarters ahead. We’re not concerned with this higher level of commitments given the firm’s glowing near-term revenue forecast. The company also summarized its variety of leases, cloud service commitments, and financing guarantees (mainly its recent deal with OpenAI), reporting maximum exposure of $165 billion for deals done thus far. For context, this is less than 40% of the free cash flow we expect Nvidia to earn in fiscal 2028 from its AI hardware sales. Even if most of these deals fall through, it would make an AI bust feel a bit more painful for the company, but we think Nvidia can weather such a storm if one were to arise.\\nRegarding the OpenAI deal, which is by far the biggest one, our takeaway is that OpenAI and Anthropic have simply outgrown the VC industry. In any normal scenario, VC would fund the needs of a startup. However, because both have grown so large and are in much more capital-intensive businesses, they require additional funding. This is where Nvidia has stepped in, both with its own support (the $105 billion for OpenAI) and networking to arrange financing from others—namely, the $500 billion partnerships with Apollo, KKR, etc.\\nNvidia is stressing that its AI gear is “fungible and durable.” If its main customer can no longer afford its GPUs, the firm can rent them out to others or deploy them for its own purposes. Given that we’re in the early innings of agentic and physical AI, we think this capacity will get soaked up, even in the unlikely chance that the main customer can’t use it.\\nNvidia considers its AI gear an investable asset and this financing akin to automobile financing. These GPUs may have 5-10-year lives—a little less than a car, but a bit more durable than past tech like PCs and smartphones.\\nCybersecurity stocks were an early target of the “AI loser” trade. But over the last few months, they’ve turned into winners. CrowdStrike’s earnings report last week showed why. Morningstar equity analyst Malik Ahmed Khan says the company’s strong top-line reacceleration was “driven by increased spending on cyber solutions as enterprises beef up defenses against increasingly capable AI-driven cyber-attacks. In this arms-race-type scenario, cyber vendors are seeing clear business wins.”\\nKhan thinks this reflects a broader trend. Here’s what he says:\\nCyber budget expansion due to AI is real. It is showing up in actual results and forward-looking metrics.\\nThis is not a “quarter story.” We expect this to be a multi-year tailwind for the sector.\\nWe accelerated cyber software spending in 2027, above our forecast from our landscape.\\nMost of the spending is accruing to platform vendors, with customers clearly seeking to buy more from less. This disproportionately benefits CrowdStrike CRWD and Palo Alto Networks PANW .\\nThe two undervalued names in our coverage are Okta OKTA (identity) and Zscaler ZS (network). We see upside to both names. Based on recent trends and commentary from other vendors, we expect Zscaler’s upcoming report to be strong.\\nZscaler and Palo Alto will report quarterly results next week and provide annual guidance, which we can test against our thesis.\\nTom Lauricella is Senior Editorial Director, Global Markets, for Morningstar. Email Tom at tom.lauricella@morningstar.com.',)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3; a=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/identity-probe/v2/candidates-v2-next.json\\\"))[\\\"categories\\\"][\\\"T3 one price compared across two years\\\"][\\\"order\\\"]; c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); [print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",x[\\\"input_id\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",c.execute(\\\"select text from items where input_id=?\\\",(x[\\\"input_id\\\"],)).fetchone()) for x in a[5:10]]'\"","aggregated_output":"\n### gnews:58fb7b4d430994417016dc48 \n ('Nvidia\\'s hyper growth has been driven largely by tech\\'s hyperscalers, which have purchased the company\\'s graphics processing units in bulk.\\nInvestors are eager to see if Nvidia can rapidly diversify its business by providing financing options to a wider swath of customers.\\nJensen Huang, chief executive officer of Nvidia Corp., speaks next to a BlueField-4 STX Storage tray, from left, Vera CPU compute tray, and Spectrum-X Ethernet Photonics Switch System while holding a Spectrum-X Ethernet Photonics chip during a keynote address at the Nvidia GTC conference in San Jose, California, US, on Monday, March 16, 2026.\\nNvidia has been the profit engine of the artificial intelligence boom, filling up data centers with its graphics processing units, which are also powering the biggest AI models.\\nBut if there\\'s one persistent concern among investors regarding Nvidia\\'s ability to build on its $5 trillion market cap, it\\'s customer concentration. Nvidia counts on the group known as the hyperscalers — Amazon , Google and Microsoft — for an outsized amount of revenue, as those companies buy GPUs in bulk for their own workloads, and sell access to them through their giant cloud businesses.\\nAdd to that Meta and SpaceX , which have also been massive buyers as they build their own models, while also starting to rent out some of their Nvidia capacity to other companies that need it right away.\\nIn its last earnings report in May, Nvidia changed the way it reports financials to split out hyperscalers from the rest of the pack, which it now groups as AI clouds, industrial and enterprise, or ACIE. Nvidia doesn\\'t specify which companies fall into the former group, but CEO Jensen Huang has defined the group pretty clearly.\\n\"The easiest go-to-market, of course, is the hyperscaler, because there are only five or six of them,\" Huang said on the May earnings call. \"The rest of them, the rest of the industry, represents 250,000 companies around the world.\"\\nAs Wall Street gears up for Nvidia\\'s fiscal second-quarter earnings report after the bell on Wednesday, the ACIE portion of the customer base is coming under the microscope. In the first quarter, the two segments were almost equal, with Nvidia reporting $37.9 billion in hyperscaler sales and nearly $37.5 billion in ACIE revenue. Nvidia highlighted that its ACIE revenue grew at 31% from the prior period, topping 12% growth from hyperscalers.\\nSkepticism has started seeping into the stock. The shares fell 2.9% on Monday, dropping for a seventh straight day, the longest losing streak since 2022. They\\'re down 7.5% over that stretch.\\nNvidia stakeholders want the company to become less reliant on a small subset of customers, and they also worry about the ability of those companies to further bolster their spending. Amazon and Alphabet turned cash flow negative in the second quarter, and Meta\\'s cash generation dwindled by more than 90% from a year earlier. Both of Elon Musk\\'s public companies — SpaceX and Tesla — reported negative free cash flow as they pursue AI expansions.\\n\"This story underneath the surface is investors\\' concern about just how sustainable the run has been for Nvidia, and feeling like the hyperscalers just can\\'t give much more,\" Gene Munster, managing partner at Deepwater Asset Management, said in an interview. \"They want to start to see the other segment starting to kick in.\"\\nNvidia in May provided nine restated quarters of history on its website, showing hyperscalers making up about 55% of data center revenue in the past year. Hyperscaler revenue soared 115% from a year earlier in the most recent quarter, topping the 74% growth from ACIE customers.\\nAnalysts expect the trend to flip going forward. The ACIE side of the business is expected to show 149% annual growth over the past year to $43 billion in the second quarter, while hyperscaler revenue is likely to show growth of 83% to $43.6 billion, according to StreetAccount. One potential big driver for the hyperscaler business is SpaceX, which aims to rapidly build out Nvidia-based data centers over the next year, Munster said.\\nIn total, analysts see Nvidia\\'s revenue almost doubling from a year earlier to $92.2 billion, according to LSEG. Data center continues to account for a bigger chunk of sales, with that division expected to reach $86.3 billion, based on StreetAccount estimates. That would be 94% of the total, up from 92% in the first quarter.\\nFor the full year, analysts expect 83% revenue growth to $396 billion, before slowing to 44% expansion next year, according to LSEG.\\nIn its effort to diversify, Nvidia is being proactive rather than waiting for new customers to arrive. Recognizing that very few companies have the cash or balance sheet to support purchases of its rack-scale systems, Nvidia is working with Wall Street to turn GPUs into an asset class.\\nEarlier this month, Nvidia unveiled a program with six leading financial firms that could pull together up to $500 billion in financing from investors who view chips as an investable asset, like real estate. Such a project, Huang said, could enable more companies to borrow money for GPU purchases at lower rates, because the chips will generate returns.\\nNvidia said in the announcement that it had signed a memorandum of understanding with the investment firms. Few specifics have been presented.\\n\"There\\'s been a lot of headlines and big numbers and not a lot of details on how this stuff is going to work yet,\" Stacy Rasgon, an analyst at Bernstein, told CNBC last week. \"Color that they could give on that would be helpful to put some investors\\' minds at ease.\"\\nAnother crucial number in Nvidia\\'s earnings report will be sales of Vera Rubin systems, which have just recently started to ramp up. Earlier this year, Huang told attendees at the company\\'s GTC conference that he expects $1 trillion in sales through 2027 from current-generation Blackwell and Vera Rubin.\\nQuestions about the long tail of the customer base could ease if Vera Rubin looks like an even bigger success than projected.\\n\"We expect Nvidia to report strong results and guidance with ramping Rubin GPU shipments representing the key drivers of upside,\" KeyBanc analysts, who recommend buying the stock, wrote in a note over the weekend.',)\n\n### gnews:d30249a09f7f187ba29fbbde \n ('Every day, businesses and governments around the world are becoming increasingly reliant on America’s frontier models. Startup CEOs already can’t imagine running their companies without AI, and it won’t be long until the same is true for every other organization in the world.\\nAt the same time, it’s become abundantly clear that access to frontier models is at the mercy of Anthropic, OpenAI, and the United States government. Fable 5 was temporarily banned by the USG, and GPT 5.6 and Astra were similarly delayed. Both models have cyber, bio, and other safety safeguards that, though well-intentioned, often prevent good users from completing harmless tasks. Given recent security incidents and general worries about increasingly powerful AI, it is extremely likely that frontier model usage will only become more restricted from here. In fact, we believe it’s plausible OpenAI/Anthropic will eventually stop offering API access entirely for their most capable models.\\nOpen source seems like the obvious solution to all these dependency concerns, but it’s far from a silver bullet. First, all the “open source” licenses are starting to become increasingly restrictive. As just one example, any “model as a service business” making over $20M a year must negotiate a separate agreement with Moonshot to serve Kimi K3. Second, just because a lab open sources their models today doesn’t guarantee they’ll continue doing so in the future. Imagine if in 2028, you’re stuck with 2027 level intelligence for some extremely high-value use case because no relevant model is open source and everything has to stay on-prem for security reasons. It’s kind of like having your Fable request downgraded to Opus except 100x worse.\\nThe only way to fully address these concerns is to pretrain your own model that runs on your own GPUs. Until now, this has largely been a question for companies: do the potential benefits justify the enormous investment required? Soon, however, this same calculation will confront every major nation state.\\nToday, we’ll do a deep dive on South Korea’s sovereign AI efforts. Beyond being home to two of the most important companies in the AI supply chain, Korea also has a long tradition of technological self-reliance (as any foreigner that’s had to use Naver Maps knows) and is currently the clear leader in sovereign AI.\\nFrom there, we’ll discuss the two major implications for investors: why Nvidia is sovereign AI’s largest supporter and why Korea’s ambitions may not be aligned with Samsung and Hynix shareholders.\\nKorean Squid Games - National AI Tournament: attempting to build a domestic frontier model\\nIn June 2025, the Korean government announced “독자 AI 파운데이션 모델”, or the “Independent AI Foundation Model” project. As the name suggests, the goal is to develop a model that Korean organizations can train, modify, and operate without depending on a foreign AI lab.\\nPerhaps the most interesting thing about the project is its structure. Rather than selecting a single national champion upfront, the government is hosting a tournament. All participants are provided subsidies for the three pillars of AI—compute, data, and researchers—and evaluated every 6 months. At each stage, losers are eliminated and have their resources reallocated towards the winners.\\nThe competition began with 15 consortiums . Ten passed the initial document review, and five were selected in August 2025: Naver Cloud, LG AI Research, SK Telecom, NC AI, and Upstage. Most readers are likely entirely unaware of any of these companies’ AI efforts, but some of them are surprisingly credible. SKT, LG, and Naver all pre-trained LLMs pre-ChatGPT for example.\\nThe exact subsidies varied by team. For the first round, the government rented ~3000 H100 equivalents from SKT and Naver and distributed them to the other 3 contestants. They also spent ~$45M USD on data. Most of this was paid to Korean companies for things like books, news articles, and video broadcasts, which—along with some Korean government records—were shared among all the contestants. However, they also gave each company ~$2M to buy data themselves. Lastly for researchers, the government offered each company ~$1.4M to try recruiting overseas talent, but only Upstage took them up on the offer.\\nSurviving teams will be given additional resources as the tournament progresses, but the total government budget of ~$350M is still a rounding error compared to US labs. However, as we’ll explain later in this article, this is just a small portion of Korea’s planned AI investment. Additionally, their results so far show that training a decent model from scratch may be cheaper than most think.\\nThe original plan was to go from 5 teams to 4 to 3 and then finally 2, with each round lasting roughly 6 months. The two winners would be selected at the end of 2026, and the government would give them additional resources to scale up their models throughout 2027.\\nHowever, there was some unexpected drama at the end of round 1!\\nThe 5 teams were judged 40% based on benchmark scores, 35% on expert review, and 25% on user testing. NC finished last, which means the other 4 teams should’ve moved on, but the government decided to disqualify Naver as well. This is because they used vision and audio encoders from Alibaba’s Qwen model family.\\nIn Naver’s defense, these are separate neural networks whose outputs are then fed into the main LLM, and the Korean government was originally unclear about what contestants could vs couldn’t use from open source. It was only after the submissions were made that the government decided foreign architectures were allowed, but even auxiliary components must be trained and developed after initializing the weights.\\nNaver maintained that they’d already developed their own encoders and could replace the Qwen pieces at any time, but the government was predictably stringent and refused to reinstate them.\\nNow short 1 team, the Korean government decided to host a supplemental competition to find a new 4th contestant and ultimately selected Motif Technologies, a neolab that spun out of Moreh, a Korean AI infra software company—in February 2025.\\nAll 4 companies open sourced their newest models in July and the results for the most recent round were announced on August 18th. But before we spoil the results, let’s take a look at the models.\\nSo how good are their models?\\nHere’s an overview of the 4 models:\\nAnd here’s how they compare on the benchmarks:\\nAs you can see, the two startups (Motif and Upstage) significantly outperformed teams backed by bona fide chaebols. The fact that their models are less than half the size is even more impressive.\\nOn Artificial Analysis’ Intelligence Index, Motif 3 is 10 points ahead of Upstage and by far the best Korean model. What’s even more notable, however, is the fact that it’s comfortably ahead of both Inkling and Nemotron 3 Ultra—the two best American open source models today.\\nMotif is a sub 30 person startup that released their first pre-trained model, which was only 2.6B total parameters, last June. They’ve raised just $17M and have access to a mere 768 B200s (< 2MW).\\nIn contrast, Thinking Machines is the hottest neolab around, raised $2B at $12B post for their seed, and signed a 1GW+ compute deal with Nvidia which was accompanied by additional funding. As for Nvidia itself, the resources at its disposal hardly need mentioning.\\nPut differently, a tiny Korean startup you’ve likely never heard of trained—from scratch and on a shoestring budge—the best non-Chinese open source model in the world .\\nThe point here is not to diss Thinking Machines or Nvidia. Training open source models is neither of their core businesses. Instead, we want to highlight that training a near-SOTA open source model from scratch is likely less resource intensive than most think. There’s no substitute for focus, and the total compute cost to train Motif 3, including experimentation, was just ~$15M USD at today’s prices. This is obviously well within budget for every major nation state.\\nQuestionable judgement from the Korean government\\nWe’ve previously written in-depth about the many issues with benchmarks, but they still tend to be directionally correct. A 10+ point gap on something like the Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index has almost always corresponded to a step change improvement in model capability (e.g. GPT 4o to o1 or Opus 4.8 to Fable 5).\\nWith this in mind, we were shocked to see that Motif was the one company eliminated in the latest round of competition. Maybe you could argue that the model is benchmaxxed and thus didn’t deserve to be first, but finishing dead last is baffling.\\nRemember that competitors were scored on three categories: 40% benchmarks, 35% expert review, and 25% user testing. Although Motif scored the best on benchmarks, they ranked last for the other two.\\nThe exact methodology for these last two sections is frustratingly opaque. For user testing, we have no idea what tasks people actually used the model for or what criteria they considered to judge the models. Additionally, none of the testing was blind, so it’s possible users had some bias towards the more famous companies.\\nThe expert review section is even more questionable. Essentially, 10 external experts graded all four models on things like “development strategy”, “future plans”, and “ecosystem impact”. As for what these words actually mean, your guess is as good as ours, but it seems clear that some categories disproportionately benefit large companies like LG and SKT. We also heard that the expert reviewers were concerned Motif’s technology could be transferred overseas via foreign investors. This is quite puzzling, as one of the requirements of the competition is that all of the technical components must be open sourced.\\nWe think it’s a real shame Motif will no longer get any government support now that they’ve been eliminated from the competition. The whole point of a sovereign AI program is to rally your domestic AI talent to build a high quality model. Motif demonstrated that they’re capable of exactly this, and the government should be utilizing its reach and resources to help them with things like “future plans” and “ecosystem impact”—not punish them for it.\\nNow, Motif may be forced to relocate out of Korea in order to acquire the capital and compute required to continue their research . This is perhaps the most ironic possible result for the surprise standout of your national AI tournament. Hopefully other countries can do better as they pursue their own sovereign AI efforts.\\nWith that being said, South Korea’s ultimate AI champion will be very well resourced. In July, they announced a monster $919B investment to build 8.4GWs by 2029 and 18.4GWs by 2035. For the first phase, SK Group, GS Group, and Naver will be building 5GW, 2.4GW, and 1GW respectively. SK Group is additionally responsible for the remaining 10GWs for phase 2.\\nWe’ve already identified 3 active sites for phase 1 totaling 4.4GWs. For full details including the exact location, MW ramp, and power source, see our Datacenter Model .\\nAlthough these timelines are aggressive, we believe they are achievable. The regulatory environment is favorable, and President Lee Jae-myung is explicitly framing AI infra as a legacy-defining project akin to Korea’s broadband push in the 1990s. Throughout the 2000s and 2010s, Korea had uniquely cheap and fast internet, which is what enabled their local search, e-commerce, and esports industries.\\nTo be clear, not all of this capacity is intended to support Korea’s sovereign AI efforts, and much of it will likely be sold to Anthropic or OpenAI. Importantly, however, Korea will have the optionality to dramatically scale up their sovereign AI efforts if necessary, and this domestic compute ramp is something more countries outside of the US and China will likely copy.\\nWhy Nvidia needs open source and sovereign AI\\nJensen recently made his Twitter debut with a manifesto on the importance of open source AI. It was later co-signed by basically every relevant AI company except Anthropic. Here’s an excerpt:\\n“Our AI leadership will be judged not by one frontier AI model, but by whether the United States builds a strong, open ecosystem that diffuses into every sector. This is essential for creating opportunities for innovation and prosperity across the country. It requires expanding access to AI, encouraging competition, robust application layers, and giving Americans greater control over the technology they rely on. Open weight models—AI models that anyone can download, inspect, modify, and run on their own infrastructure—are an important part of that foundation because they make advanced AI more accessible, adaptable, and widely available.”\\nMuch of the discourse around open source AI tends to wax poetic about the democratization of intelligence and decentralization of technological power. However, look under the hood and it’s often just people talking their book.\\nNo one needs open source AI more than Nvidia. As we previously explained to Tokenomics Model subscribers, selling frontier tokens at API prices is the highest ROI use case of incremental compute. Anthropic plus OpenAI are therefore set to take a larger and larger percentage of net new GWs in the future, and Nvidia is currently on track for a world where they only have 2 real customers. Sure maybe the number’s 7 if you include the hyperscalers, but that’s still obviously unacceptable for the world’s largest company. This is doubly true when all 7 customers except SpaceX are actively trying to build their own XPUs to cannibalize Nvidia’s margins.\\nOne of the most famous competitive strategies in tech is to “ commoditize your complements ”. The general idea is that as something becomes cheaper and more abundant, demand for everything that goes well with it increases. This explains why companies will often “give away” things for “free”. Classic examples include 1) Google offering Chrome for free and open-sourcing Android, which increased access to the web and made search advertising more valuable and 2) Microsoft retaining the right to license MS-DOS to IBM’s competitors, which helped turn the PC into a commodity hardware platform.\\nIn Nvidia’s case, AI models and applications are the complements to its GPUs. The long-term health of their business depends on a vibrant, diverse AI ecosystem that exists outside of just Anthropic and OpenAI.\\nTheir recently announced $500B MOU with the world’s largest capital allocators is just one example of the clever financial engineering Nvidia can do to make this vision a reality. By making “Nvidia AI Factory Compute” an “investable asset class,” the SSIs and Thinking Machines of the world have a way to leverage capital from pension funds, insurance companies, and private credit firms to buy enormous amounts of compute.\\nSovereign AI is another huge opportunity for Nvidia to diversify their customer base. SK Group has already committed to building 2 GWs of Rubin as part of their 5GW buildout, and Naver has signed on for another 200MW. We expect Jensen to continue announcing similar deals with additional countries.\\nFrom a sovereign AI perspective, Korea’s investment in government- and corporate-owned data centers and GPU clusters makes complete sense. A sovereign model ultimately requires sovereign infrastructure: domestic control over where the model is trained, how it is deployed, and who can access the underlying compute.\\nKorea’s challenge is that it still lacks a scaled domestic provider of leading-edge accelerators. Rebellions and FuriosaAI are making some progress, but they remain objectively behind Nvidia and AMD in deployment scale, software maturity, and commercial availability. Hyperscaler-developed alternatives such as Google’s TPUs and AWS’s Trainium are not domestically controlled either.\\nThe buildout of data centers essentially comes down to two things: capital and compute. Capital is the easier problem in our view. The Korean government has been prioritizing AI investment over the past few years, while Naver, SK Telecom, Samsung, SK Hynix, and other leading domestic tech companies are committing significant resources to data centers, AI infrastructure, and model development. Compute on the other hand, is another story. Korea can finance land, power, cooling, and buildings, but it cannot manufacture a globally competitive accelerator ecosystem just like the majority of countries in the world—or guarantee access to the latest platforms—simply by spending more money.\\nIf Korea’s objective is merely to establish a domestic AI ecosystem, a conventional supplier relationship with Nvidia may be sufficient. If it wants to become one of the world’s leading sovereign AI players, however, it will need something deeper: greater roadmap visibility, close technical integration, reliable access to new compute platforms, and support for developing the surrounding domestic ecosystem.\\nThis is why Nvidia’s expanded relationships with Samsung and SK Hynix matter in this context. Samsung reportedly plans to build an Nvidia-powered AI factory using more than 50,000 GPUs, while SK Telecom’s announced 2GW DSX AI factory is expected to deploy Vera Rubin systems powered by SK Hynix HBM4. While the public announcement does not disclose preferential GPU allocation or guaranteed volumes, deeper strategic alignment should strengthen Korea’s ability to plan and deploy leading-edge compute—something far more difficult than simply constructing the data centers that house it.\\nAgainst this backdrop, we believe Nvidia’s plans extend beyond just acquiring a large sovereign compute customer. As we previously explained to Memory Model subscribers, we believe Korea’s infrastructure buildout also gives Nvidia an opportunity to deepen its relationship with SK Hynix, one of its most important memory suppliers and a key affiliate within the broader SK Group. We believe Nvidia has likely secured a long-term agreement for SOCAMM, along with favorable HBM pricing and large volume commitments for 2027, although the final negotiation result could vary from our current estimate.',)\n\n### gnews:86bbde98330831dcfcbd4b8f \n (\"Welcome to the Free edition of How They Make Money .\\nOver 300,000 subscribers turn to us for business and investment insights.\\nIn case you missed it:\\n💰 Wall Street’s Top Stocks in Q2\\nNVIDIA keeps outrunning the law of large numbers\\nThe company just crossed $96 billion in quarterly revenue, with growth accelerating above 100%. Management now expects revenue to grow another 70% next year , adding more than $200 billion in annual revenue. At this scale, that should sound almost implausible. Yet even that outlook is supply-constrained.\\nBut the story is also getting more complicated. NVIDIA is helping finance the infrastructure its customers need, while some of those same customers are developing competing chips of their own.\\nSo the debate now comes down to two questions:\\nHow long can the AI infrastructure boom keep compounding?\\nHow much of it can NVIDIA continue to capture?\\nToday at a glance:\\nNVIDIA’s Q2 FY27\\n1. NVIDIA Q2 FY27\\nNVIDIA’s fiscal year ends in January, so the July quarter was Q2 FY27.\\nData Center revenue remains off the charts, as illustrated below.\\nIncome statement:\\nRevenue accelerated 106% Y/Y to $96.2 billion ($4.1 billion beat).\\nData Center +117% Y/Y to $89.0 billion.\\nEdge Computing +27% Y/Y to $7.2 billion.\\nGross margin was 75% (+3pp Y/Y).\\nOperating margin was 66% (+5pp Y/Y).\\nNon-GAAP EPS was $2.22 ($0.13 beat).\\nCash flow:\\nOperating cash flow +57% Y/Y to $24.1 billion.\\nFree cash flow +59% Y/Y to $21.3 billion.\\nBalance sheet:\\nCash and marketable securities: $99.4 billion.\\nDebt: $33.4 billion.\\nQ3 FY27 Guidance:\\nRevenue +12% Q/Q and +89% Y/Y to $108.0 billion ($3.4 billion beat).\\nGross margin 74% (-1pp Q/Q).\\nGuidance assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China.\\nSo, what to make of all this?\\n🚀 Growth accelerated again: Revenue growth jumped from 85% in Q1 to 106% in Q2, while Data Center accelerated from 92% to 117%. NVIDIA added nearly $15 billion of revenue sequentially, and Q3 guidance calls for another $12 billion.\\n☁️ Demand is broadening: Hyperscale revenue reached $48.7 billion, while AI Clouds, Industrial, and Enterprise grew even faster to $40.3 billion. NVIDIA is increasingly benefiting from neoclouds, enterprises, and sovereign AI alongside Big Tech.\\n🔄 Rubin is arriving without a digestion pause: Blackwell Ultra is still ramping, yet Rubin shipments have already started. So far, each new architecture is layering onto the previous one rather than creating an air pocket.\\n📉 Margins are finally bending: Gross margin is expected to fall from 75% in Q2 to 74% in Q3 and roughly 71%-72% in Q4, largely because of higher memory costs. The pressure appears supply-driven rather than a sign of weaker demand or pricing.\\n🧱 NVIDIA is locking up supply: Supply and capacity commitments jumped from $119 billion to $279 billion in three months, largely to secure memory. That's a huge vote of confidence in future demand—and a much larger commitment if the cycle eventually slows.\\n🔮 The outlook remains extraordinary: NVIDIA expects roughly 70% revenue growth in FY28, far above prior Wall Street expectations. If that holds, the AI infrastructure cycle is still expanding rapidly despite NVIDIA already operating at enormous scale.\\nBig picture: Growth accelerated, demand broadened, and Rubin is arriving before Blackwell has slowed. The main new wrinkle is that sustaining this pace is becoming more expensive, with memory costs pressuring margins and supply commitments rising rapidly.\\n2. Business highlights\\nNVIDIA is capturing more revenue from every AI factory generation.\\nManagement estimates its revenue opportunity per gigawatt keeps increasing:\\n~$18 billion per gigawatt with Hopper.\\n~$25 billion per gigawatt with Blackwell.\\nAnd now ~ $40 billion per gigawatt with Vera Rubin .\\nRubin combines GPUs, CPUs, networking, and software to deliver 30x higher throughput per megawatt and 35x lower token costs than Grace Blackwell Ultra.\\nProduction shipments have already started, with purchase orders from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud, and system OEM.\\nNVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs and partnered with major financial institutions to help raise more than $500 billion of third-party capital for AI infrastructure.\\nIt is also using credit support and take-or-pay commitments to help AI labs and neoclouds finance capacity. Critics call this circular financing. NVIDIA argues it is simply removing a capital bottleneck for customers whose demand is growing faster than their balance sheets.\\nEither way, NVIDIA is moving beyond selling infrastructure to helping make that infrastructure possible. There may also be a strategic benefit: financing infrastructure today can help lock in NVIDIA deployments before competing silicon reaches scale.\\nAccording to The Information, NVIDIA has agreed to acquire Hugging Face for $12.9 billion , nearly triple its 2023 valuation.\\nHugging Face is one of the main hubs for developers to discover, share, and deploy open AI models, often dubbed the “GitHub of AI.” With only about $150 million in annual revenue, NVIDIA is clearly buying strategic positioning rather than near-term profits.\\nThe logic ties directly to Jensen’s argument on the call: NVIDIA benefits whenever AI models proliferate. Open models are particularly attractive because startups and enterprises generally don’t build custom chips and overwhelmingly rely on existing compute infrastructure.\\nOwning Hugging Face would move NVIDIA one layer closer to developers and tighten the link between open-model adoption and its broader computing platform. The risk is that NVIDIA ownership could weaken the neutrality that helped make Hugging Face so valuable in the first place.\\n3. Key quotes from the earnings call\\nCheck out the earnings call transcript on Fiscal.ai here .\\nCEO Jensen Huang:\\nOn custom chips:\\n“Whereas many of these XPUs are inference-specific chips for one cloud or one service, NVIDIA is a platform, an entire AI factory platform that spans the entire AI life cycle that you can use in any cloud. It’s in every cloud. You can run anywhere. [...] I have 100% confidence that our technology will continue to be extraordinary for them and that the economics of using our technology, whether it’s from data processing to training, to post-training, to agentic processing, our technology’s going to be extraordinary for them.”\\nThis is Jensen’s answer to OpenAI’s Jalapeño and the broader custom-silicon threat. His argument isn’t that customers won’t build their own chips. It’s that those chips tend to optimize specific workloads, while NVIDIA’s advantage is a fungible platform spanning training, inference, networking, CPUs, and multiple clouds. The question is whether that breadth remains valuable enough to justify NVIDIA’s premium economics.\\nOn what happens when AI becomes agentic:\\n“When the world goes to agentic, fully agentic systems, you are going to have agents running all the time, working with other agents running all the time. [...] I think the most important thing that matters for the industry is that, one, AI is now doing productive and useful work. Two, AI is generating profitable tokens. Three, if we had more compute, we could generate more profitable tokens, which results in more profit for all of the services. This is the exact phase where we are at, which is the reason why everybody is leaning in.”\\nToday’s AI is still largely triggered by humans. Jensen believes the next phase involves millions of agents operating continuously and interacting with other agents. If that happens, inference demand stops being directly tied to human usage, and the amount of compute required could increase dramatically.\\n4. What to watch next\\nNVDA is up roughly 20% YTD, still outperforming the S&P 500.\\nEven after the post-earnings jump, the stock trades at only about 20x forward earnings , well below the multiple it commanded earlier in the AI boom and below the rest of US Big Tech.\\nThe latest 13F filings for Q2 2026 showed that hedge funds are not accumulating NVDA as much as they used to. The stock remains one of the most widely held names, although many funds are still underexposed relative to its 8% weight in the S&P 500.\\nAt that valuation, the debate is less about whether NVIDIA looks cheap today and more about how durable these extraordinary earnings can be .\\nIn semiconductors, a low P/E can sometimes signal peak earnings rather than a bargain. The multiple may be compressed precisely because the market is questioning whether today’s profitability is sustainable. So far, that skepticism has been repeatedly proven wrong.\\nHere’s what I’m watching:\\nCustom silicon: OpenAI just published the first results for Jalapeño , its custom inference chip. The company says it delivered 1.5x-1.9x more throughput per watt and materially lower latency than the NVIDIA systems tested across several models. OpenAI still plans to use NVIDIA broadly, but Jalapeño shows that NVIDIA’s largest customers have a growing incentive to move specialized inference workloads onto their own silicon.\\nCircular financing: NVIDIA says AI labs receiving some form of balance-sheet support could account for roughly one-quarter of its business next year . Financing customers does not make the underlying demand fake, but it increases NVIDIA’s exposure if AI labs eventually struggle to monetize the infrastructure they are building.\\nMargins and supply: Memory scarcity is expected to push gross margin down before price increases help it recover next year. The question is whether NVIDIA can continue securing sufficient supply to meet extraordinary demand without sacrificing too much of its economics.\\n📉 The bear case is that AI infrastructure spending eventually outruns the profits it can generate, while custom silicon takes a growing share of inference.\\n📈 The bull case is that agentic AI keeps expanding compute demand faster than efficiency gains and competition can reduce it.\\nSo far, NVIDIA is still winning that race.\\nThat’s it for today!\\nHappy investing!\\nThanks to Fiscal.ai for being our official data partner. Create your own charts and pull key metrics from 50,000+ companies directly on Fiscal.ai . Save 15% with this link .\\nDisclosure: I own AAPL, AMD, AMZN, GOOG, META, MSFT, and NVDA in App Economy Portfolio . I share my ratings (BUY, SELL, or HOLD) with App Economy Portfolio members.\\nThe jump in supply commitments is where the bull and bear cases almost meet. If demand keeps compounding, locking scarce memory and capacity early is brilliant. If monetization lags the buildout, those commitments become a very different kind of operating leverage. At this scale I think the risk has shifted from “is AI demand real?” toward “how much future demand is already being financed and pulled into the present?”\\nThe tape's reaction is the story to me. $NVDA grew 106% and gave back the entire post-earnings move in a day, and $MRVL beat with above-consensus guidance and fell 10.3%. When the two best reports in the group get sold, expectations are the constraint, and expectations reset on time, not on numbers. Watching 227 on NVDA as the line where that changes.\",)\n\n### gnews:59a612d6bcf31d162a9a3a8c \n ('Investing.com -- NVIDIA\\'s deal to mobilize over $500 billion in third-party capital with Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs, and KKR reframes how AI factories are built and financed. By turning GPU compute into a standardized, investable asset class, NVIDIA addresses severe liquidity bottlenecks.\\nHowever, the financial rewards and structural advantages flow to specific winners across the AI and financial ecosystems.\\n1. NVIDIA: Offloading Debt to Safeguard Free Cash Flow\\nNVIDIA emerges as the primary strategic winner because the framework shifts lending risk entirely off its balance sheet.\\nUnder prior arrangements, NVIDIA acted simultaneously as a hardware supplier and debt guarantor—including an initial ~$100 billion OpenAI commitment backed by an estimated $250 billion guarantee. That structure raised intense \"circularity\" concerns that NVIDIA was effectively funding its own demand. BofA analyst Vivek Arya reiterates a Buy rating ($320 price target), plain-spokenly noting: \"The burden sits with the consortium, not NVDA\\'s balance sheet. NVDA guarantees asset quality, not the debt, turning the bear\\'s depreciation worry into the enabling feature.\"\\nBofA estimates NVIDIA\\'s prior vendor-financing exposure at roughly 15% of its ~$470 billion in projected free cash flow (FCF) over 2026 and 2027. Shifting that load to private capital frees up NVIDIA\\'s massive FCF for aggressive stock buybacks. Consensus models project NVDA buybacks at ~$73 billion in 2026 and ~$106 billion in 2027—only 36% to 37% of FCF compared to NVIDIA\\'s pledge to return over 50%.\\nWells Fargo analyst Aaron Rakers (Overweight rating, $315 price target) highlights that NVIDIA is orchestrating something far bigger than chip sales: \"We see this as yet another example of how NVDA is playing a much bigger game than just a supplier in AI infra build-outs,\" citing NVIDIA\\'s press framing that it is \"helping create a new class of productive, investable infrastructure: AI Factories.\" Because CUDA software continually extends GPU lifespan and keeps compute fungible across operators, rental yields remain elevated and depreciation curves stay benign. With BofA estimating NVIDIA holds 65% to 70% of a projected $1.7 trillion CY2030 AI systems TAM, this $500 billion financing pool serves as an early enabler of a massive long-term expansion.\\n2. Neoclouds & Power Operators: Lowering the Cost of Capital\\nNeocloud operators CoreWeave (NASDAQ: CRWV) and Nebius (NASDAQ: NBIS) represent the most direct public-market beneficiaries. Both saw immediate stock gains following the announcement—CoreWeave rose 1.6% and Nebius jumped 2.6%.\\nAs non-investment-grade borrowers, neoclouds face high borrowing costs when trying to buy tens of thousands of expensive GPUs. Bloomberg Intelligence analyst Vasu Kasibhotla notes that private capital backing \"can ease financing constraints for CoreWeave, Nebius and other neocloud and power-site operators.\" Kasibhotla adds that \"for CoreWeave, cheaper and deeper credit can ease funding pressure from its heavy capital-spending plan. For Nebius, greater capital availability would strengthen the funding base for expansion.\"\\nAccess to deeper, cheaper credit enables these operators to secure scarce power sites and expand data center capacity faster without diluting equity.\\nThe six private equity and asset management giants—Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs, and KKR—gain access to a lucrative, scalable yield platform.\\nInstitutional investors have long searched for large-scale, yield-generating real assets tied to digital transformation. By structuring AI data centers as an asset-backed credit class with usage-linked revenue-sharing mechanisms, these mega-firms can deploy institutional capital into long-duration, high-yield technology real estate.\\nIf the $500 billion capital pool successfully accelerates physical data center construction, component suppliers further down the supply chain stand to benefit. While analysts caution that this linkage remains directional speculation—unsupported by new order data or company guidance—unbroken data center expansion directly drives hardware bill-of-materials demand.\\nMemory Manufacturers (Micron, Sandisk): Unconstrained data center construction maintains tight supply dynamics for High-Bandwidth Memory (HBM) and enterprise SSDs, supporting elevated average selling prices.\\nOptical Interconnects (GLW, COHR, CRDO, AAOI, LITE): As AI clusters expand to hundreds of thousands of chips, copper wiring hits physical distance and speed limits. Accelerating data center builds fuels demand for high-speed fiber and photonic components:\\nMOUs Are Not Deployed Capital: Signed memorandums of understanding do not equal deployed dollars. Third-party capital still requires creditworthy end-customers paying real money for compute usage.\\nDemand Pull-Forward: Vasu Kasibhotla warns that the central danger may be that financing simply \"pulls accelerator demand forward, rather than signaling broad weakness in prime AI infrastructure,\" while raising questions about backlog quality and who bears risk if actual usage trails expectations.\\nPhysical Constraints Persist: Financing GPUs does not remove physical hurdles like power availability or local regulation, highlighted by cancelled projects like the UK NScale deal.\\nAdded Opacity: Complex financing structures can add opacity to AI buildout economics, potentially pressuring trading multiples if debt risk becomes unclear.\\nAll eyes now turn to NVIDIA\\'s Q2 FY2027 earnings call on August 26, which analysts at BofA and Wells Fargo view as the next critical catalyst. The market expects NVIDIA to clarify its precise role in these financing platforms, its remaining vendor-financing exposure, and its go-forward buyback trajectory.',)\n\n### gnews:7dfab0010a8ac15786f05387 \n ('Record growth continued: NVIDIA reported fiscal Q2 revenue of $96 billion, more than double the prior year, with data center revenue reaching $89 billion. The company forecast $108 billion in third-quarter revenue and expects approximately 70% fiscal 2028 growth, constrained by supply.\\nAI infrastructure demand is broadening: NVIDIA\\'s Vera Rubin platform began production shipments and is expected to represent about 20% of third-quarter data center revenue. AWS also plans to deploy an additional 2 million NVIDIA GPUs, while enterprise, sovereign AI and NeoCloud demand is growing rapidly.\\nMargins and China remain challenges: Gross margins are expected to decline to 71%–72% in the fourth quarter because of rising memory costs before stabilizing in fiscal 2028. NVIDIA\\'s outlook assumes no China data center compute revenue amid geopolitical uncertainty, while the company returned a record $26 billion to shareholders.\\nLooking Beyond NVIDIA? These 3 AI ETFs Are Beating the Market\\nNVIDIA (NASDAQ:NVDA) reported record second-quarter results for fiscal 2027, with revenue more than doubling from a year earlier to $96 billion, as demand for artificial intelligence infrastructure continued to expand across hyperscalers, cloud providers, enterprises and sovereign customers.\\nChief Financial Officer Colette Kress said the company expects revenue to grow by approximately 70% in fiscal 2028, though she characterized that outlook as supply-constrained. NVIDIA forecast third-quarter revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%.\\n→ Quantum Computing Is Raising the Stakes for Cybersecurity: 5 Stocks to Watch\\nOpenAI\\'s Jalapeño Chip Could Change the AI Hardware Race\\n\"The surge in AI demand is driving a global infrastructure build-out,\" Kress said, citing demand from hyperscalers, AI laboratories, AI-native businesses, enterprise customers and sovereign AI initiatives.\\nData center revenue rose 18% sequentially to $89 billion in the second quarter. Revenue from hyperscale customers was $49 billion, up 13% from the prior quarter, driven by demand for Blackwell products. Revenue in NVIDIA\\'s ACIE category, which includes NeoCloud, industrial and enterprise customers, rose 25% sequentially and 138% year over year to $40 billion.\\n→ Palantir\\'s Kool-Aid Moment: The Math Behind Karp\\'s Forecast\\n5 Stocks to Buy in September Before Wall Street Catches On\\nKress said NVIDIA Compute was \"fully utilized across every cloud we serve.\" She also said NeoCloud partners using NVIDIA\\'s DSX reference designs are expected to exit the year with 8 gigawatts of installed capacity, compared with roughly 3 gigawatts at the end of 2025.\\nNVIDIA announced an expanded partnership with Amazon Web Services under which AWS plans to deploy an additional 2 million NVIDIA GPUs beginning in the current quarter through the second quarter of fiscal 2029. The deployment will include Vera CPUs, some integrated with Rubin products and some deployed as standalone processors, according to Kress.\\nThe company began production shipments of its Vera Rubin platform earlier in August and said it had received purchase orders from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud provider and system original equipment manufacturer. Kress said NVIDIA expects Vera Rubin to be its fastest product ramp to date and expects the platform to account for about 20% of data center revenue in the third quarter.\\nNVIDIA\\'s networking revenue also reached a record, increasing 18% sequentially. Spectrum-X Ethernet revenue grew 2.6 times from a year earlier, Kress said. The company also said it is in full production of its Vera CPU and expects CPU revenue to more than double in fiscal 2028, based on customer demand and its improving supply outlook.\\nCEO Jensen Huang said the company\\'s outlook reflects both hyperscale demand and growth in markets outside the largest cloud operators. He said NVIDIA\\'s ACIE segment, including enterprises, NeoClouds and sovereign AI customers, represents about half of its business and is growing at an annual rate of 100%.\\nHuang also highlighted the company\\'s expanding revenue opportunity per gigawatt of data center capacity. NVIDIA estimated that opportunity at roughly $18 billion during the Hopper generation, $25 billion with Blackwell and $40 billion with Vera Rubin, including CPUs, GPUs, networking, systems and software.\\n\"Our demand is much higher than\" the 70% growth outlook, Huang said, adding that the company is working with suppliers to expand capacity. He said NVIDIA\\'s supply chain is broadly constrained and that the company has supply sufficient to support approximately 70% growth, while demand exceeds that level.\\nKress said NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI laboratories and has partnered with Apollo Global Management, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield Asset Management, Goldman Sachs and KKR on financing platforms expected to raise more than $500 billion in third-party capital.\\nThe company also described a revenue-sharing arrangement with NeoCloud providers. NVIDIA can provide take-or-pay commitments on part of a facility\\'s capacity to support project financing, then share in revenue generated above a defined minimum level. Kress said the model could create a recurring, usage-linked revenue stream in addition to hardware sales over the medium to long term.\\nNVIDIA said it secured land, power and shell capacity through SB Energy for a Portsmouth campus expected to support 4.25 gigawatts of AI factory capacity, with the initial deployment intended for OpenAI. OpenAI\\'s existing and planned commitments represent approximately 12 gigawatts of NVIDIA Compute through 2030, according to Kress.\\nGAAP and non-GAAP gross margin were both 75% in the second quarter, largely unchanged sequentially. Operating expenses increased 10% on a GAAP basis and 11% on a non-GAAP basis from the prior quarter, driven primarily by compute infrastructure and employee compensation costs.\\nFor the third quarter, NVIDIA forecast GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points. Kress said gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in the fourth quarter before settling at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price increases take effect.\\nThe company cited \"extreme pricing conditions\" in memory, saying memory cost increases have surpassed its earlier expectations. Kress said tighter memory supply is partly a result of the same AI infrastructure build-out that is supporting NVIDIA\\'s demand.\\nInventory rose to $32 billion as NVIDIA prepared for the Vera Rubin launch. Days sales outstanding increased to 60 days, reflecting extended payment terms for certain large investment-grade customer purchases scheduled for shipment across multiple quarters.\\nNVIDIA said it shipped less than 1% of total data center revenue in Hopper 200 products to China-based customers during the second quarter under U.S. government licenses. The company said those shipments reduced corporate gross margins and that its forward outlook includes no China data center compute revenue because of geopolitical uncertainty.\\nDuring the quarter, NVIDIA returned a record $26 billion to shareholders, including $20 billion in share repurchases and $6 billion through its quarterly dividend of $0.25 per share. The company said it had returned 60% of free cash flow year to date, above its plan to return at least 50% of free cash flow.\\nNVIDIA Corporation, founded in 1993 and headquartered in Santa Clara, California, is a global technology company that designs and develops graphics processing units (GPUs) and system-on-chip (SoC) technologies. Co-founded by Jensen Huang, who serves as president and chief executive officer, along with Chris Malachowsky and Curtis Priem, NVIDIA has grown from a graphics-focused chipmaker into a broad provider of accelerated computing hardware and software for multiple industries.\\nThe company\\'s product portfolio spans discrete GPUs for gaming and professional visualization (marketed under the GeForce and NVIDIA RTX lines), high-performance data center accelerators used for AI training and inference (including widely adopted platforms such as the A100 and H100 series), and Tegra SoCs for automotive and edge applications.\\nThis instant news alert was generated by narrative science technology and financial data from MarketBeat in order to provide readers with the fastest reporting and unbiased coverage. Please send any questions or comments about this story to contact@marketbeat.com.',)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3; a=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/identity-probe/v2/candidates-v2-next.json\\\"))[\\\"categories\\\"][\\\"T3 one price compared across two years\\\"][\\\"order\\\"]; c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); [print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",x[\\\"input_id\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",c.execute(\\\"select text from items where input_id=?\\\",(x[\\\"input_id\\\"],)).fetchone()) for x in a[15:20]]'\"","aggregated_output":"\n### gnews:4f85affb9aaa7d877e4539c2 \n ('Nvidia will not ship a single new gaming GPU in 2026. Not a refresh, not a Super variant, not a budget card. It’s the first calendar year without a new GeForce launch since the original GeForce 256 shipped in October 1999, according to industry reporting tracking Nvidia’s release cadence – and a July 2026 Tech Insider analysis flagged 2026 as the year that could snap Nvidia’s roughly 30-year streak of annual GPU launches. The RTX 50 series, which launched across 2025, remains the current lineup with no successor in sight until at least late 2027, the earliest window cited by leaker Kopite7kimi and reported by Notebookcheck in January 2026. Nvidia’s own product reporting as of March 2026 still listed RTX 50 Series ownership as its current GeForce generation with no RTX 60 Series announced at that point, and that remains the case as of this October 2026 update.\\nWhy Nvidia Skipped Gaming GPUs Entirely in 2026\\nWhat Gamers Actually Get in 2026: DLSS 4.5, Not New Silicon\\nThe RTX 5090 SE Rumor That Nvidia Never Confirmed\\nNvidia’s 2026 Gaming GPU Roadmap vs Prior Generations\\nHistorical Context: Has This Happened Before?\\nCompetitive Landscape: RTX 50 vs Radeon RX 9000 in Mid-2026\\nWhat This Means for GeForce NOW and Cloud Gaming\\nShould You Buy a GPU Now or Wait?\\n5 Predictions for the GPU Market Through 2027\\nIs Nvidia really not releasing any new gaming GPUs in 2026?\\nWhen will the RTX 60 series release?\\nWhat is the RTX 5090 SE and is it real?\\nWhat is RTX Spark used for?\\nShould I buy an RTX 5090 or RTX 5080 now, or wait for the next generation?\\nIs AMD launching new gaming GPUs in 2026 either?\\nDoes DLSS 4.5 make older RTX cards feel new again?\\nHas Nvidia ever skipped a full year of gaming GPU launches before?\\nThat gap has turned “rtx 60 series release date” into one of the more searched GPU queries of August 2026, pulling roughly 880 monthly searches in the US alone – the same month a Tech Insider report made the core claim explicit: no new gaming GPU is arriving in 2026 at all. Gamers who assumed a new architecture would land on the usual two-year cycle are instead watching Nvidia pour its engineering resources into an entirely different product: the RTX Spark, an AI PC superchip announced with Microsoft that has nothing to do with gaming frame rates. This piece breaks down what’s actually happening, why it’s happening now, and what it means for anyone shopping for a graphics card before 2027.\\nNvidia’s gaming GPU release pattern has held for nearly three decades: a new architecture roughly every 18 to 24 months, usually accompanied by a mid-cycle Super or Ti refresh. The RTX 40 series launched in 2022, the RTX 50 series followed in January 2025, and under the historical cadence a Super refresh or an early RTX 60 tease should have landed sometime in 2026. Neither happened – and it’s getting later, not earlier. As recently as September 2025, Tom’s Hardware had projected that Rubin-based consumer cards could still arrive as soon as late 2026 or early 2027, but that window has since slipped hard: Tom’s Hardware and NotebookCheck reported in February 2026 that Nvidia’s internal mass-production target for the next generation had already slipped from the end of 2027 into 2028, and The FPS Review followed in March 2026 with its own reporting that mass production may not begin until 2028 rather than late 2027, with no confirmed launch month attached to any of these estimates.\\nThe reporting from Digital Foundry and other outlets covering Computex 2026 in early June points to a schedule slip rather than a cancellation. Nvidia’s next true gaming architecture, code-named Rubin, isn’t expected before the second half of 2027 at the earliest – a 2H 2027 window that Club386 and TechSpot’s January 2026 reporting pegged as no earlier than roughly June 2027 – with some leakers pushing that timeline into 2028, a date now echoed by both Tech Insider in July 2026 and Tom’s Hardware in February 2026. A separate rumor round-up from Digital Foundry and GamesRadar in June 2026 described the slip as at least a 12-month gap measured against where a hypothetical RTX 50 Super refresh would have landed, though even that round-up stopped short of naming a firm date, price, or spec sheet. PCGuide’s own tracking, first published in August 2025 – when the site still expected RTX 60 no earlier than 2026, before later reporting pushed that estimate back – and updated again in August 2026, now frames the arrival window as 2027-2028, and TechSpot’s January 2026 roundup of leaker reporting landed on that same late-2027-to-2028 consensus. By September 2026, the 2028 date had hardened further still: Tom’s Hardware reported that month that Rubin-based RTX 60 GPUs might not launch before 2028, and TweakTown’s own September 2026 reporting found multiple independent leakers converging on that same 2028 window. The reason isn’t a design failure or manufacturing hiccup at TSMC. It’s that Nvidia’s engineering and wafer allocation are being funneled into data center AI accelerators and, more recently, into a new category of AI-first PC hardware that competes for the same 3nm and advanced packaging capacity that would otherwise go to a gaming die.\\nWafer capacity at TSMC’s leading nodes is the real constraint. Every Blackwell-class die Nvidia builds for a gaming card is a die it isn’t building for a data center customer paying substantially more per unit. With AI infrastructure demand still outstripping supply through 2026, Nvidia’s own incentives point toward filling data center and now consumer AI-PC orders first, leaving gaming as the segment that waits.\\nInstead of a new GeForce card, Nvidia used its Computex 2026 keynote to introduce RTX Spark, a dual-die “superchip” built for Windows AI PCs and announced jointly with Microsoft. According to Nvidia’s official announcement from late May 2026, RTX Spark pairs a 20-core Grace CPU (Arm-based) with a Blackwell-class RTX GPU carrying 6,144 CUDA cores and fifth-generation Tensor Cores with FP4 precision support. The two dies communicate over Nvidia’s NVLink-C2C chip-to-chip interconnect rather than a traditional PCIe bus, and the package ships with 128GB of LPDDR5x memory delivering roughly 300GB/s of bandwidth. Nvidia confirmed RTX Spark’s worldwide launch date as October 16, 2026, opening preorders that same month with the product listed directly alongside the GeForce RTX 5090 and RTX 5080 on Nvidia’s own site – and still with no RTX 60 Series pricing or SKU anywhere in that October lineup.\\nNone of that spec sheet is aimed at gaming benchmarks. RTX Spark is built to run local AI agents, large language model inference, and Copilot-style workloads directly on a desktop or laptop without round-tripping to the cloud. Nvidia’s framing, according to its own press materials, is “reinventing Windows PCs for the age of AI” – a strategic pivot that treats the PC as an AI inference endpoint first and a gaming rig second. Coverage from Tom’s Hardware’s Computex day-zero wrap-up described RTX Spark as an “assault on laptop and desktop markets,” positioning it against Intel’s incoming Xeon 6-based AI PC chips and Qualcomm’s Snapdragon X lineup, not against AMD’s Radeon gaming cards.\\nThe bigger picture: this is the same Blackwell architecture family that powers Nvidia’s data center GPUs. The company also confirmed at GTC 2026 that an RTX PRO 4500 Blackwell Server Edition GPU is headed to Amazon EC2, according to Nvidia’s GTC 2026 live blog . Blackwell silicon is being sliced up for servers, AI PCs, and workstations – everywhere except a new gaming card.\\nThe one gaming-relevant update Nvidia shipped this year is software, not hardware. Nvidia had already scheduled DLSS 4.5 Multi Frame Generation for RTX 50 Series owners on March 31, 2026, and by June 2026 the company counted more than 1,000 RTX-enabled games and apps across its ecosystem, including 007: First Light , which Nvidia scheduled for a May 27, 2026 release with DLSS 4.5 support built in. DLSS 4.5 Ray Reconstruction itself then rolled out in August 2026 to existing RTX GPUs, introducing a second-generation transformer model that unifies denoising and Super Resolution into a single AI network instead of running them as separate passes. We covered the technical rollout in detail in our DLSS 4.5 Ray Reconstruction breakdown , but the strategic read is simple: Nvidia is extending the useful life of RTX 40 and RTX 50 series cards through driver and AI-model updates – and by October 2026, Nvidia’s own GeForce coverage had already moved on to previewing DLSS 5, still without attaching any release date to an RTX 60 Series card.\\nThat’s a deliberate move. If there’s no new gaming GPU to sell, keeping current-generation cards competitive through software is the only lever left. It also buys goodwill with the installed base of RTX 40 and RTX 50 owners who would otherwise feel stranded on hardware that’s a full generation removed from Nvidia’s marketing spotlight for the entirety of 2026.\\nSpeculation filled part of the vacuum. Leaks pointed to a cut-down RTX 5090 SE built on the same GB202 silicon as the retail RTX 5090, but scaled back to 110 streaming multiprocessors (roughly 14,080 CUDA cores) against the full 170 SM count on the standard 5090, paired with 32GB of GDDR7 on a 384-bit bus and a 500W TGP. As of mid-July 2026, Nvidia had made no official announcement, and at least one outlet, Club386, published a direct denial that any RTX 5090 SE was in the pipeline. Treat this one as unconfirmed until Nvidia says otherwise; it illustrates the appetite for any scrap of new-hardware news during a year when the actual roadmap has gone quiet.\\nNvidia’s gap year should, in theory, be AMD’s moment. It hasn’t quite worked out that way. AMD’s next consumer architecture, RDNA 5 (sometimes referred to under the UDNA branding as the company unifies its gaming and compute lines), is also tracking to mid-2027 or later rather than a 2026 launch. Computex 2026 hardware showcases, including an ASrock prototype Phantom Gaming card, were still built around the existing RDNA 4 flagship, the RX 9070 XT, dressed up with new cooling and a 12V-2×6 power connector rather than new silicon underneath.\\nAMD did make one real move this year: the global launch of the Radeon RX 9070 GRE, a trimmed-VRAM variant that we covered in our RX 9070 GRE global launch report . That’s a market-expansion play, not a new-generation launch, and it underscores that both major GPU vendors are managing the current generation for another year rather than racing to replace it.\\nIntel, meanwhile, is chasing a different opportunity. Leaks point to a budget Panther Lake-based chip with eight CPU cores and four Xe3 graphics cores aimed squarely at gaming handheld manufacturers looking for a cheaper alternative to flagship silicon – a segment Intel already has a foothold in through cards like the Arc B580, which we compared directly against Nvidia and AMD’s budget options in our Arc B580 vs RTX 5060 vs RX 9060 XT comparison .\\nRTX 40 series (Ada Lovelace)\\nRTX 50 series (Blackwell)\\nRTX 60 series (Rubin, expected)\\nThe gap between RTX 50 and the still-unnamed next generation is already tracking to be the longest in the RTX era, and GamesRadar reported in September 2026 that it could stretch into a 20-year record gap between GPU generations if Rubin keeps sliding toward 2028 as leakers now suggest. As of September 2026, that’s no longer just a projection: iTechGuides confirmed that month that Nvidia’s own public product information still lists the RTX 50 series as its confirmed consumer generation, with no RTX 60 model announced, priced, or released, and none of the reporting tracking the launch – from Tom’s Hardware to NotebookCheck to Digital Foundry – has moved beyond rumor and delay coverage to anything resembling a confirmed product.\\nA generation-length pause in new gaming GPU launches has ripple effects well beyond enthusiast forums. With no successor pressuring prices downward, RTX 50 series cards have held value far longer than any recent generation. We documented this directly in our RTX 4090 vs RTX 5080 used-market analysis , where used RTX 4090 pricing actually climbed relative to the newer RTX 5080 rather than depreciating on the usual curve, because buyers have no reason to wait for a replacement that isn’t coming.\\nThat pricing dynamic is compounding with a separate problem: the 2026 DRAM and memory shortage that’s already pushed component and device prices up across the board. We covered how that shortage forced Apple to raise iPad and Mac prices by as much as 29% in our DRAM shortage report , and GDDR7 memory used in GPUs isn’t insulated from the same supply crunch. With no new architecture to reset pricing expectations and memory costs rising independently, RTX 40 and RTX 50 series cards are proving unusually sticky at current price points well into their third year on shelves.\\nNone of this is happening by accident. Nvidia’s data center segment has dwarfed gaming revenue for several years running, and every wafer allocated to a GeForce die is a wafer not sold to a hyperscaler at data-center margins. The RTX Spark launch with Microsoft extends that same math into the consumer PC market: an AI PC superchip carries software and platform strategy value (tying Windows Copilot features to Nvidia silicon) that a traditional gaming refresh doesn’t. From a purely financial standpoint, 2026 wafer capacity spent on RTX Spark and data center Blackwell parts likely generates more revenue per wafer than the same capacity spent on a GeForce refresh aimed at a gaming market that isn’t demanding one as urgently as AI buyers are.\\nNvidia has stretched a generation before – the RTX 20 to RTX 30 gap ran close to two years, and RTX 30 to RTX 40 also landed near the 24-month mark – but a full calendar year with zero new gaming GPU SKUs of any kind, not even a Super refresh or a rebadge, has no precedent since the GeForce 256 debuted in 1999 and kicked off the branded GPU category. Even during the 2020-2022 chip shortage, when Ampere cards were nearly impossible to buy at MSRP, Nvidia still launched new SKUs; it just couldn’t supply them in volume. 2026 is different: supply isn’t the blocker, allocation is.\\nCurrent high-end, holding price per our RTX 5080 vs RX 9070 XT comparison\\nStill AMD’s flagship gaming card, no RDNA 5 in 2026\\nBudget tier, no successor confirmed for 2026\\nEvery major GPU vendor is effectively frozen on its current generation through the rest of 2026. That’s an unusual moment of competitive stasis across the entire discrete GPU market simultaneously, not just at Nvidia.\\nNvidia’s cloud gaming arm hasn’t slowed down even as the desktop GPU roadmap stalled. GeForce NOW continued its RTX 5080-tier rollout through 2026, and we detailed the pricing and server upgrade path in our GeForce NOW RTX 5080 rollout coverage . That’s a meaningful signal: Nvidia can keep upgrading the silicon that powers its subscription streaming service on its own schedule, decoupled from the retail GPU cadence that gamers buying physical cards are stuck waiting on. If you want RTX 5080-class rendering today without buying a card that won’t be replaced until 2027 at the earliest, cloud gaming is arguably the more current option right now.\\nFor anyone shopping for a graphics card as of this October 2026 update, the calculus has shifted from the usual “wait for the next generation” advice. With Rubin-based RTX 60 series cards not expected before late 2027 – and possibly 2028 given how leaker timelines have already slipped once this year – waiting an entire generation cycle for a card that doesn’t exist yet is a much bigger ask than the usual few-month wait before a refresh. Current RTX 50 and RX 9000 series cards are the real current generation for at least another 12 to 18 months, not a soon-to-be-outdated stopgap – and Nvidia’s own October 2026 lineup still lists the RTX 5090 and RTX 5080 as its flagship GeForce cards, now sold directly alongside the newly launched RTX Spark AI PC superchip whose worldwide preorders opened that same month.\\nThe one caveat is the unconfirmed RTX 5090 SE rumor. If Nvidia does release a cut-down high-VRAM variant later in 2026, it would land as a mid-cycle SKU rather than a new architecture, similar in spirit to a Super refresh. It’s worth watching, but it shouldn’t be the reason to delay a purchase given Nvidia hasn’t confirmed the product exists.\\nRubin slips to early 2028 for mainstream desktop cards. Data center Rubin parts will likely arrive first in late 2027, with consumer GeForce variants trailing by two to four months, mirroring the Blackwell rollout pattern.\\nDLSS updates become Nvidia’s primary 2027 gaming announcement cadence. Expect at least one more DLSS point release (DLSS 4.6 or similar) before any new silicon, continuing the software-first strategy that extended RTX 40/50 series relevance through 2026.\\nAMD narrows the RDNA 5 gap rather than beating Nvidia to market. With both vendors tracking mid-to-late 2027, AMD’s real opportunity is matching Nvidia’s launch window rather than exploiting a first-mover advantage it hasn’t seized in 2026.\\nUsed RTX 40 series pricing stays elevated through at least Q2 2027. As long as no successor architecture ships, secondhand Ada Lovelace cards will keep holding value abnormally well compared to historical depreciation curves.\\nAI PC chips like RTX Spark expand into more OEM laptops before any new gaming die ships. Expect Nvidia to prioritize additional Grace-Blackwell AI PC partnerships with OEMs beyond Microsoft before allocating meaningful wafer capacity back to gaming-only silicon.\\nCorrect. As of August 2026, Nvidia has not announced any new gaming GPU architecture, Super refresh, or new SKU beyond variants of the existing RTX 50 series. The RTX Spark superchip announced at Computex 2026 is an AI PC product, not a gaming card.\\nNo official date exists – iTechGuides confirmed in August 2026, citing Nvidia’s own public product information, that no RTX 60 launch date has been set. Industry leakers and outlets like Digital Foundry point to Nvidia’s next gaming architecture, Rubin, arriving no earlier than the second half of 2027, with some estimates extending into 2028. Tom’s Guide’s January 2026 coverage put a possible announcement as early as January 2027, with Q1 2027 floated as a launch window, though most other outlets still treat late 2027 as the realistic floor. PCGuide and NotebookCheck’s roadmap tracking, updated as recently as August 2026, splits the two use cases apart entirely: Rubin is expected to ship into data centers across a 2026-2028 window, while the gaming-oriented variant that would eventually become RTX 60 trails behind into 2027 or later. By September 2026, that later end of the range looked increasingly likely – Tom’s Hardware and TweakTown both reported that month that multiple leakers now expect Rubin-based RTX 60 GPUs no earlier than 2028. Nvidia’s own disclosures back that reading up: as of March 2026 the company’s product pages still listed RTX 50 Series as the current GeForce generation with no RTX 60 Series announced, and by October 2026 – even as Nvidia’s GeForce coverage shifted to the newly launched RTX Spark and a preview of DLSS 5 – still no RTX 60 Series release date had been given.\\nThe RTX 5090 SE is an unconfirmed rumored variant using cut-down GB202 silicon (110 SM, roughly 14,080 CUDA cores) with 32GB of GDDR7. Nvidia has not confirmed the product, and at least one outlet has published a direct denial that it’s launching.\\nRTX Spark is a superchip combining a 20-core Grace CPU with a Blackwell-class RTX GPU, built for running local AI agents and inference workloads on Windows PCs. It’s announced through a Microsoft partnership and isn’t marketed as a gaming product.\\nWith no new gaming architecture expected before late 2027 at the earliest, current RTX 50 series cards remain the practical top-tier choice for at least the next 12 to 18 months. Waiting for RTX 60 series in 2026 is not a realistic strategy.\\nNo. AMD’s RDNA 5 architecture is also tracking to mid-2027 or later. AMD’s only 2026 moves have been SKU expansions like the RX 9070 GRE, not a new generation.\\nDLSS 4.5 Ray Reconstruction, which rolled out in August 2026, brings a unified transformer model for denoising and upscaling to all existing RTX GPUs. It’s a meaningful image-quality and performance upgrade, but it’s a software update, not new hardware.\\nNot since the GeForce brand launched in 1999. Even during the 2020-2022 chip shortage, when cards were scarce, Nvidia still launched new SKUs; supply was the constraint, not the roadmap. 2026 marks the first time allocation, not supply, has paused new launches entirely.\\nFor more GPU and AI chip coverage, visit our AI chips and GPU pricing hub .\\nElias Virtanen is the Cybersecurity Analyst at Tech Insider, bringing hands-on expertise from his background in penetration testing and security consulting. He previously worked as a security researcher at F-Secure in Helsinki, where he focused on threat intelligence and vulnerability disclosure. Elias covers ransomware trends, zero-trust architecture, and the evolving regulatory landscape including NIS2 and the EU Cyber Resilience Act. He holds a CISSP certification and an MSc in Information Security from Aalto University.',)\n\n### gnews:0fdddf6a7cdc3904b78a8653 \n (\"Nvidia in Talks to Backstop $250 Billion in OpenAI Data Center Financing\\nNvidia is in talks to guarantee roughly $250 billion in lease and construction financing for a 10-gigawatt OpenAI data center campus in southern Ohio, first reported by the Wall Street Journal [1]\\nThe total project cost exceeds $500 billion including chips, with Nvidia separately weighing a $350 billion deal to finance OpenAI's GPU purchases [2]\\nOpenAI lacks an investment-grade credit rating, so Nvidia's balance sheet would effectively stand in as collateral for lenders [3]\\nInvestor Michael Burry increased his Nvidia short position at $210.28, calling the arrangement circular: 'Around and around we go' [4]\\nThe proposed guarantee is nearly six times larger than Google's ~$44 billion in data center rent guarantees, the previous high-water mark for tech infrastructure commitments [3]\\nNvidia is in talks to provide approximately $250 billion in financial guarantees that would backstop OpenAI's lease of a massive data center campus in southern Ohio, the Wall Street Journal reported Saturday [1] . The arrangement, if finalized, would represent the largest vendor-financing commitment in technology history.\\nThe 10-gigawatt facility is being developed by SB Energy, SoftBank Group's power subsidiary, on a decommissioned uranium-enrichment site roughly 50 miles south of Columbus. The full project cost — including the AI chips installed inside the data centers — is expected to exceed $500 billion [2] . The first phase, at roughly 800 megawatts, is targeted for completion in 2028 [3] .\\nOpenAI does not carry an investment-grade credit rating, making it difficult to raise the debt needed for a project of this scale on its own terms. Under the proposed structure, Nvidia's balance sheet would effectively serve as collateral, allowing lenders to price the financing against the chipmaker's creditworthiness rather than the borrower's [3] . Discussions remain in early stages and no agreement has been signed [1] .\\nThe $250 billion guarantee covers OpenAI's lease obligations and certain construction costs for the Ohio campus, but excludes the cost of chips themselves [2] . In a parallel negotiation, Nvidia is weighing a separate deal to finance up to $350 billion in GPU purchases by OpenAI, which would bring total Nvidia financial exposure to the project to roughly $600 billion [3] .\\nThe campus will likely house next-generation Nvidia hardware rather than current systems. Nvidia's Rubin Ultra architecture, expected in the second half of 2027, delivers roughly 15 exaflops of performance versus 3.5 exaflops for current chips. The subsequent Feynman architecture, slated for 2028, features die stacking and custom HBM memory, with racks hosting up to 1,152 GPUs [2] .\\nSB Energy, which raised $500 million from OpenAI in 2025 and previously built a 900-megawatt solar park near Austin for Google, is planning an initial public offering later in 2026 at a valuation exceeding $50 billion [2] . The Ohio site's power supply is controlled by the U.S. government, with Commerce Secretary Howard Lutnick overseeing access allocation [3] .\\nThe proposed arrangement has drawn sharp criticism from investors and analysts who see a structural conflict: a chip supplier underwriting its customer's ability to buy its own products. Michael Burry, the investor famous for his 2008 housing-market short, increased his Nvidia short position on July 24 at $210.28 and posted on X: 'Around and around we go. Nvidia to guarantee $200 billion of ChatGPT's spending on $NVDA chips' [4] .\\nNvidia's AI-related financial commitments have grown rapidly. The chipmaker has committed over $40 billion to AI equity investments, including roughly $30 billion invested in OpenAI itself. Nvidia has also funded Safe Superintelligence, the startup led by former OpenAI co-founder Ilya Sutskever, which then purchases Nvidia hardware [3] .\\nTech commentator Ed Zitron characterized the deal as 'such an insane thing to do on so many levels and about as bearish as it gets, especially considering it is being built by SoftBank' [4] .\\nThe proposed $250 billion guarantee dwarfs existing precedents in tech infrastructure financing. Google has guaranteed approximately $44 billion in data center rent — the current industry high-water mark — making the Nvidia figure nearly six times larger [3] .\\nBig Tech's off-balance-sheet AI commitments have ballooned to an estimated $1.65 trillion in aggregate. Meta alone carries roughly $420 billion in such obligations, nearly triple its reported debt [3] . SoftBank is separately backing a $40 billion OpenAI loan through an eight-bank syndication [3] .\\nAI infrastructure spending industrywide is expected to exceed $700 billion in 2026, with a cumulative $3 trillion projected through 2028 on data center buildouts [3] . The central risk is that the pace of construction overshoots demand or that returns erode faster than debt can be amortized.\\nFor OpenAI, the deal represents a step toward controlling its own compute infrastructure rather than renting capacity from Microsoft, Amazon, and Oracle — its current primary cloud providers. Ownership of dedicated facilities would give OpenAI more control over hardware configurations and training schedules [3] .\\nFor Nvidia, the guarantee locks in demand for its chips years into the future, though it also concentrates financial risk around a single customer that has never turned an operating profit. Nvidia shares closed Friday at $206.84, down 0.92% [4] .\\nOther AI companies are pursuing similar infrastructure ambitions. Anthropic, Microsoft, and Google have all recently approached Commerce Secretary Lutnick about access to the Ohio site's government-controlled power supply, signaling a broader scramble for energy-rich data center locations [3] .\\nThe negotiations remain in early stages, and the terms or structure of any guarantee are subject to change. No timeline for a final agreement has been disclosed [1] . Nvidia is expected to report fiscal Q2 earnings in August, which will give investors their first opportunity to question management about the scope of the company's financing commitments.\\nThe deal's completion would also depend on regulatory and political factors, including allocation of federally controlled power at the Ohio site and a Japanese commitment to invest $33 billion in a natural gas plant to supply additional electricity [3] .\",)\n\n### gnews:61332f6e93b88db0d3214cab \n (\"Best AI Stocks After Nvidia Earnings: NVDA, AMD, Broadcom, Marvell or Arm?\\nNvidia’s strong earnings report highlights robust, ongoing growth in AI infrastructure spending, driving demand across chips, CPUs, and networking. Nvidia remains the industry standard, supported by massive scale and high visibility. Among competitors, AMD offers the clearest challenger upside with strong GPU and CPU growth, while Broadcom dominates custom silicon and networking. Marvell provides speculative upside with significant future monetization tied to Google, and Arm delivers differentiated CPU architecture exposure. Key risks include customer concentration, execution challenges, valuation, and potential slowdowns in hyperscaler capital expenditures.\\nTradingKey - Nvidia's release of earnings on Aug. 26 sparked the beginning of a new trade. On Thursday, the stock jumped by 8.7%, closing at $227.98, after positive fiscal second quarter reports by Nvidia showed $96.2 billion in total revenue, with $89.0 billion from its Data Center segment, and with management expecting Q3 revenue to come in at $108 billion. The positive read-through to Nvidia extends beyond the chip segment; the demand for chips is also driving demand for CPUs, networking equipment, custom chips, and connectivity infrastructure. With the listed beneficiaries of AI spending now clear, investors are not asking if AI spending is growing, but which companies do investors believe will benefit the most from that spending.\\nNvidia Still Sets the Standard for AI Infrastructure\\nWith another exceptionally strong quarter showing year-over-year growth for total revenue of 106% and growth for Data Center revenue of 117%, and GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins holding at 75%, Nvidia is the best overall AI infrastructure company. Management has now changed expectations and expects revenue for next year’s fiscal to grow by 70%.\\nNvidia Price Chart - Source: Tradingview\\nThe company’s strong position is further solidified with the production of Vera Rubin and growing demand for Blackwell. Nvidia’s AI factory continues to grow, increasing the business's ability to capture more of the dollars associated with each deployment.\\nSome concerns are driven by the stock price assigned to years of potentially aggressive AI spending, but the company’s mix of factors: scale, software, margins, and visibility, make Nvidia the best position to benefit in this market.\\nAMD Offers the Clearest Challenger Upside\\nAs a listed participant in both accelerator and server CPU markets, AMD is the most credible challenger. Revenue for Q2 grew by 50 percent to $11.54 billion, with Data Center revenue more than doubling to $6.72 billion. Management projects Q3 revenue at $13 billion and has set a 56 percent non-GAAP gross margin.\\nAMD has multiple levers to benefit from spending on AI. With Instinct GPUs, EPYC CPUs and the Helios rack-scale platform, AMD has multiple levers to benefit from spending on AI. AMD would not need to displace Nvidia in order to create a significant amount of upside. A minor increase in accelerator share would impact AMD's revenue significantly.\\nThe primary concern is execution. CUDA is Nvidia's largest competitive advantage, while AMD is still improving ROCm and has to execute on building complex rack-scale systems without margins eroding.\\nBroadcom Is the Strongest Custom-Silicon and Networking Play\\nBroadcom's fiscal Q2 AI semiconductor revenue grew 143% year-over-year to reach $10.8 billion versus the company's expectation. Broadcom projects $16 billion for its Q3 AI semiconductor revenue, which will represent over 200% year-over-year growth.\\nBroadcom Price Chart - Source: Tradingview\\nAI custom silicon diversification in the data center is the key to Broadcom's dominance. Broadcom continues to expand its Ethernet networking and custom AI accelerator business, and AI accelerators even offset reductions in customers' reliance on merchant GPUs. AVGO is thus, a solid opportunity to invest in hyperscalers' custom silicon, with a clean avoided single processor architecture bet.\\nThe fiscal Q3 earnings report scheduled for September 2 will be an important catalyst for Broadcom, but customer concentration remains a chief risk. With Broadcom's AI revenue trajectory looking more positive than the post-earnings market reaction, the market seems to be missing the positive forward AI revenue indication in Broadcom's Q2 fiscal report\\nMarvell Has Strong Growth, but Google Timing Adds Risk\\nMarvell reported fiscal Q2 record revenue in the amount of $2.739 billion representing a 37% increase over the prior year, with Data Center revenue growing 46% to $2.17 billion. The company guided to $3.15 billion for Q3 revenue, and increased their fiscal 2027 and fiscal 2028 revenue expectations to approximately $12 billion and $18 billion.\\nMarvell Price Chart - Source: Tradingview\\nCustom silicon, switching, optical interconnects and memory connectivity exposure remain strong, but execution and timing risk have increased over Nvidia, AMD and Broadcom. The Google partnership is viewed as the most significant new potential opportunity. However, the investing community focused on the approximate timing, and management noted that the most significant financial contributions are likely to occur in fiscal 2029. For this reason, shares of MRVL sold off post earnings despite positive revenue guidance for the future.\\nArm Is the Differentiated CPU and IP Bet\\nArm gives new opportunities for AI infrastructure. Fiscal Q1 revenue was $1.29 billion with 22% growth year on year. More than doubling data center royalty revenue. There are more than $2 billion of customer demand for Arm’s AGI CPU over fiscal years 2027 and 2028.\\nThe way Arm’s offerings integrate into AI will be interesting to follow. Whether for inference or training, new AI as agents means CPUs need to be able to control not only accelerators, but storage, networking, and services.\\nThis could be Arm’s biggest challenge yet as a company. The risk of fighting in the channel as a full silicon provider will be a challenge in addition to the high market valuation of Arm leaving little room for execution misses.\\n· Nvidia: Best overall AI infrastructure exposure, excels in scale, software moat, and earnings visibility.\\n· AMD: Best challenger, highest direct share-gain opportunity across AI GPUs and Server CPUs.\\n· Broadcom: Best custom-silicon and networking exposure, September 2 earnings will be a probe as to this thesis.\\n· Arm: Best differentiated CPU/IP exposure, strong data-center royalty growth, and valuation.\\n· Marvell: For the speculative upside, strongest AI demand, but Google monetization is weighted towards future years.\\nWhich AI Stock Looks Best After Nvidia Earnings?\\nFor quality and visibility, Nvidia is still the best option. AMD has the best challenger opportunity. For custom silicon networking, Broadcom is the best option. For a new differentiated exposure to the CPU layer, Arm is the best option. For a speculative upside, Marvell has it. Their custom-chip program may pay off, but there are future years of Google monetization.\\nWhat Should Investors Watch Next?\\nThe next major catalyst for Broadcom will be their release of September 2 earnings. After that, investors should be looking for the Vera Rubin supply, AMD Helios deployments, Marvell's custom-chip goals, and if Arm’s AGI CPUs will convert. The overall biggest concern for investors will be the slowing of hyperscaler capex or if AI infra investments will not be profitable.\\nNvidia's report makes the case that AI infrastructure spending is still growing. NVDA continues to have the strongest stock, AMD is the best share gain option besides NVDA, and Broadcom is the best way to add exposure to custom silicon and networking. Arm offers differentiated CPU architecture exposure, while Marvell offers custom silicon and connectivity exposure. There is more timing risk with Marvell than Arm. For the weekend watchlist, NVDA goes first, followed by AMD, AVGO, Arm, and Marvell for investors who like more risk.\",)\n\n### gnews:1219126db2e6631aa1264142 \n ('Nvidia has recruited Wall Street to bankroll its own customers.\\nThe US chipmaker said last week it had signed memorandums of understanding with Wall Street\\'s largest asset managers, including Apollo Global Management, Blackstone, BlackRock, Brookfield Asset Management, Goldman Sachs and KKR to raise upwards of half a trillion dollars for AI companies to borrow against, money that will buy its chips and build the servers that run them.\\nThe six firms will set up what Nvidia calls \"compute financing platforms,\" drawing on institutional money, insurance funds and private credit. Borrowers can use the proceeds for the chips as well as servers, networking equipment, buildings and power supply.\\nNvidia has the option to guarantee up to a quarter of any given deal, which lowers the interest rate its customers pay while leaving most of the credit risk with the lenders.\\nCEO Jensen Huang said he approached only these six companies and none refused.\\nKeeping that spending off their own books is precisely the point, and the fact that such a structure is needed at all tells investors a great deal about where the constraints in the AI boom now lie.\\nThe financial engineering rests on a single reclassification. Graphics processing units (GPUs) have always been treated as equipment that loses value quickly, superseded whenever a faster generation arrives.\\nNvidia raises over €21.5bn in first bond sale since 2021 as AI growth race continues\\nAnalysis: Will Big Tech\\'s colossal AI spending crush Europe\\'s data sovereignty?\\nNvidia is effectively asking lenders to treat them instead as long-lived infrastructure, closer to a toll road or a power plant, that can be borrowed against for years.\\n\"These are revenue-generating assets now,\" Huang said, describing them as productive, long-lived and transferable between customers.\\nWhy the money had to come from somewhere else\\nThe timing reflects a squeeze that has been building all year.\\nMicrosoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers whose cloud platforms host most of the world\\'s AI workloads have together guided roughly $720 billion (€624bn) to $745 billion (€646bn) of capital spending in 2026, an increase of about 77% on last year.\\nWhat analysts expect the hyperscalers to spend in 2027 alone has more than doubled in the space of a year, from a consensus of $480 billion (€416bn) in August 2025 to $1.08 trillion (€943bn) this month, a rise of about 127%, according to Bank of America.\\nThe pattern has repeated at every stage.\\nAnalysts who already considered last year\\'s investment unsustainable then watched the hyperscalers guide higher at the start of 2026, revise those figures upward again through the year, and pencil in larger sums still for next year and 2028.\\nMoody\\'s has warned that spending on this scale is eating into free cash flow and pushing tech groups into heavier borrowing. Alphabet recorded negative free cash flow of $5.9 billion (€5.1bn) in a quarter when it spent $44.9 billion (€38.9bn) on projects.\\nDebt raised through these \"compute financing platforms\" sits with the financing vehicles rather than on a hyperscaler\\'s own accounts and also has Nvidia\\'s backing, which protects credit ratings and leaves room for conventional borrowing elsewhere.\\nFor smaller operators the effect is larger still as companies such as CoreWeave and Nebius, which lack investment-grade ratings and pay dearly for credit, gain access to capital on terms previously reserved for the giants.\\nThe reaction was more ambivalent than the headline number suggests, and came weeks after a July selloff driven by doubts over whether AI spending will pay for itself.\\nEssentially, equity investors saw a bottleneck being cleared while credit investors saw something else: the cost of insuring Nvidia\\'s own debt against default rose after the news and has roughly doubled since late May.\\n\"Chips depreciate fast and lose value the moment a newer generation arrives,\" warned Nigel Green of financial advisory firm deVere Group, noting that lending against them only works if the collateral holds its value.\\nCritics also point out that Nvidia is helping finance purchases of its own products, deepening the circularity that already worries the sector.\\nWhether it proves pivotal in the direction Solomon means depends on a question nobody can yet answer: what will the value of a current GPU be in five years?',)\n\n### gnews:910a0652e8879b8859c8927d \n (\"At NVIDIA, Kshitiz Khatri Is Learning the Business Behind AI\\nAt NVIDIA this summer, Kshitiz Khatri (Class of 2027) is getting a close-up look behind the answer box in between his first and second year at The University of Virginia Darden School of Business.\\nEvery day, people type questions into generative AI tools and get answers in seconds. What most users do not see is the enormous amount of computing power those few seconds take and the business decisions around performance, energy and cost that lead to the answers appearing.\\nBefore coming to Darden, Khatri spent five years working in semiconductors. This summer, he is back in that world, but with a new perspective.\\nAs a product management intern on the NVIDIA’s accelerated computing GPU team, Khatri is working on the business questions behind the technology: how hardware performs, how much power it uses and what it costs for data centers to run the systems supporting today’s AI tools.\\nBefore the internship, Khatri said he assumed electricity would be the biggest cost involved in running a data center. This summer, he has learned that energy is only one part of a much larger picture. The bigger cost structure includes GPUs, networking, cooling, storage, memory and other components that make large-scale AI computing possible.\\nThat broader view has helped Khatri see AI not only as a technology story, but also as a business story shaped by cost, performance and infrastructure decisions.\\nWhile Khatri brought technical experience into the internship, he said his first year at Darden has helped him understand the business side of the work in a much deeper way.\\nThe clearest examples have been accounting and finance. In his role, Khatri works with complex cost models for data centers, including spreadsheets with hundreds of line items, discounted cash flow analysis, depreciation assumptions and financial reports.\\nBefore Darden, Khatri had an engineering background but little formal exposure to accounting. He had not spent much time reading income statements, balance sheets or 10-K reports. This summer, those concepts have become directly relevant to his work.\\n“Without the finance one and two core courses, I would have not been able to capture the meaning of those cost models,” he said.\\nThe case method has helped in a different way. Khatri said Darden has trained him to take on unfamiliar problems, form a point of view and contribute to a discussion. That habit has been useful in meetings and brainstorming sessions at NVIDIA, where he is often learning new concepts quickly and then applying them in real time.\\nOne example has been tokenomics, or the economics of tokens in AI systems. Khatri was asked to study the topic, review industry reports and share what he learned with teams at NVIDIA. He said the pace and structure of Darden’s case method helped prepare him for that kind of work.\\nThe internship has also helped Khatri clarify what he wants to do after Darden.\\nDuring his first year, he explored other career paths, including consulting. But returning to semiconductors this summer reminded him how much he enjoys the field and the kind of work he wants to keep doing.\\n“Coming back to semiconductors, I realized 100% that that’s what I love,” he said.\\nIf given the opportunity, Khatri hopes to continue at NVIDIA. If not, he plans to pursue product management or product marketing roles elsewhere in the semiconductor industry.\\nFor other Darden students interested in tech, Khatri’s advice is to start early.\\nHe said students often prioritize networking for consulting and investment banking, but tech recruiting also requires building relationships well before applications open. That is especially true for students interested in large technology companies, where internship recruiting can begin early in the academic year.\\nHis advice: make connections early, keep them warm and learn as much as possible about teams and roles before the process formally begins.\\nFor Khatri, the summer has brought together the technical foundation he had before Darden and the business skills he has built during his first year. At NVIDIA, he is seeing up close how AI infrastructure gets built, evaluated and improved, and how much business thinking guides the technology.\\nThe University of Virginia Darden School of Business prepares responsible global leaders through unparalleled transformational learning experiences.\\nDarden's graduate degree programs and Executive Education & Lifelong Learning programs offered by the Darden School Foundation set the stage for a lifetime of career advancement and impact.\",)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"agent_message","text":"已有问题里，2026 年和 2027 年的内存合约价都已分别覆盖；我正在逐篇核对文章究竟比较了什么，以及它是不是同一个量。"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3; a=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/identity-probe/v2/candidates-v2-next.json\\\"))[\\\"categories\\\"][\\\"T3 one price compared across two years\\\"][\\\"order\\\"]; c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); [print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",x[\\\"input_id\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",c.execute(\\\"select text from items where input_id=?\\\",(x[\\\"input_id\\\"],)).fetchone()[0]) for x in a[3:7]]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3; a=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/identity-probe/v2/candidates-v2-next.json\\\"))[\\\"categories\\\"][\\\"T3 one price compared across two years\\\"][\\\"order\\\"]; c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); [print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",x[\\\"input_id\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",c.execute(\\\"select text from items where input_id=?\\\",(x[\\\"input_id\\\"],)).fetchone()[0]) for x in a[3:7]]'\"","aggregated_output":"\n### gnews:db5c4d64cdbdd661f0e35836 \n 22.4M Views · 394 Posts\n· 17x EBITDA implies zero growth after 2027 — absurd.\n· Inference share rising; the bigger market, expanding.\n· AMD at 53x vs. NVIDIA at 17x — mispriced.\n· AI winter favors the default: NVIDIA's proven stack.\n· 30%+ margin of safety with 45% earnings growth.\nOpening: A Question That's Been Bugging Me for Three Months\nHonestly, I've been wrestling with one question since April — what the hell is going on with NVIDIA?\nThe Philadelphia Semiconductor Index is up 71% this year. AMD has more than doubled. Micron is up over 200%. And the undisputed king of the AI era ? Up 10%. Ten percent. You could've bought a random semiconductor ETF and easily outperformed it.\nAt first, I assumed something was wrong with the fundamentals. Pulled up the financials — nothing. Next fiscal year consensus expects 42% revenue growth , EPS growth north of 45%. Then I looked at valuation — 17x EBITDA , versus a five-year average of 36x. Cut in half. My first reaction was: I must have made a calculation error.\nBut I didn't. That's exactly how the market is pricing it.\nSo today, I want to work through this carefully: has NVIDIA truly \"aged out,\" or is the market making a collective mistake?\nWhat Is the Market Afraid Of?\nThree layers of bear logic — none entirely unreasonable.\nGabelli Funds' John Belton put it bluntly: investors want \"the strongest supply-demand imbalances and untapped growth,\" and \"NVIDIA currently meets neither criteria.\"\nI understand this logic. I disagree with the conclusion.\nWhy I Think the Market Is Overly Pessimistic\nLet me reframe what the current valuation actually implies.\nMorningstar's Brian Colello did an interesting reverse-engineering exercise: a stock price of $212 implies that NVIDIA will have virtually zero growth after 2027 . Zero. A company that dominates AI inference market share, whose downstream cloud customers are still aggressively expanding capex — and it just stops growing in two years?\nThat's not conservatism. That's pricing in a catastrophe.\nBut what's reality? Alphabet just raised its 2026 capex guidance this week and said it would increase further next year. Microsoft and Amazon show no signs of decelerating their capital spending. AI application deployment is slower than expected, but the direction hasn't reversed. You can reasonably argue that growth rates will decelerate from 90% to 40%. But pricing a decline from 40% straight to 0% ? The gap between those two assumptions — that's where the opportunity lives.\nColello's fair value estimate is $280 , more than 30% above the current price. Personally, I think even that's conservative — if NVIDIA truly delivers 45%+ EPS growth for the next two years, 17x EBITDA will look absurd in hindsight .\nA Signal Most People Missed: Inference Market Share Is Rising\nI want to highlight this separately because most people haven't noticed it.\nAccording to The Information, NVIDIA's share in the inference chip market — the chips used to run models rather than train them — is actually going up . Not flat. Not slightly declining. Rising.\nWhy does this matter? Because inference is the endgame of AI commercialization . Training is a one-time event (albeit iterative), but inference is continuous — happening every second of every day. Long-term, the inference market will dwarf training in scale. If NVIDIA is expanding its share in this larger pie, then the market's \"share has peaked → growth stalls\" narrative breaks at the very first link in the chain .\nI think this might be the single most underappreciated fact about NVIDIA right now.\nOn AMD: I Admit It's Impressive, But the Valuation Makes Me Nervous\nI need to be honest — $Advanced Micro Devices (AMD.US)$ 's execution this year has been genuinely impressive. Landing the 6GW Instinct GPU agreement with OpenAI , Oracle ordering 50,000 MI450 chips, ROCm 7.0 finally achieving meaningful compatibility breakthroughs — these are all legitimate \"zero-to-one\" milestones , and a stock doubling on the back of that is a rational market response.\nBut 53x forward P/E ? For a company with projected 2027 revenue of just $78 billion?\nLet me run a simple thought experiment: if the market believes \"the AI pie is large enough to feed both NVIDIA and AMD,\" then that thesis should be more bullish for NVIDIA — it has the larger share, the deeper ecosystem, the stronger customer stickiness. Yet the market gives AMD 53x and NVIDIA 17x. What this effectively says is: AMD's best days are just beginning; NVIDIA's best days are already over.\nIs it possible this judgment is correct? Sure. But it requires one precondition — that NVIDIA's moat is substantively breached within two years. And I don't currently see evidence that this precondition is materializing. AMD won big contracts, yes, but NVIDIA's contracts are bigger. AMD has system-level products now, yes, but NVIDIA has been building its system-level ecosystem for over a decade .\nSo my personal take is this: AMD is worth owning, but chasing it at current valuations requires extremely strong conviction. NVIDIA is the opposite — you don't need much conviction at all. You just need to believe \"it's not dead.\"\nA Counterintuitive Take: NVIDIA Might Be Safest in an AI Winter\nThis is something I've been thinking about a lot recently, and I want to put it out there for discussion.\nSuppose the worst-case scenario materializes — AI investment hype fades, cloud providers slash capex, and a genuine \" AI winter \" arrives. Who gets hurt the most?\nIntuitively, you'd say NVIDIA, because it's the biggest. But think carefully — the answer might be the exact opposite .\nWhat's the first budget to get cut in a downturn? The experimental, unproven bets . Meta's custom chip project, Microsoft's in-house accelerators, various startups' vaporware — in boom times, people are willing to burn cash trying these things. But in a contraction, the CFO's first instinct is: \"Kill the custom project. Go back to NVIDIA's proven solution. At least we know it works.\"\nThis is NVIDIA's countercyclical resilience . Its products have undergone the most rigorous market validation. Its CUDA ecosystem has the largest developer base. It is the \"default option.\" In good times, everyone wants to escape the default. In bad times, everyone runs back to it .\nNVIDIA might be the most \"boring\" position during an AI boom, but the most \"reassuring\" one during an AI winter.\n– Oracle has already admitted that renting out NVIDIA GPUs barely breaks even. If more cloud providers reach the same conclusion — \"we bought the chips but can't make money\" — capex growth could hit a wall suddenly . This is the biggest systemic risk.\n– If AMD's Helios ships successfully and meets performance targets , it will be the first time NVIDIA faces direct competition at the full-system level — not just individual GPUs. This threatens NVIDIA's solution-level pricing premium.\n– Ongoing tightening of export controls limits NVIDIA's total addressable market. China was once an enormous growth vector; it's now essentially locked out.\n– 17x might not be the floor. If the market truly completes the \"growth stock → value stock\" identity reclassification, valuations could compress further to 12-14x before bottoming. The mean reversion timeline might be longer than I expect.\nIf I had to pick one semiconductor stock to hold for two years without touching it at current prices, I'd pick $NVIDIA (NVDA.US)$ over AMD $Advanced Micro Devices (AMD.US)$ or $Micron Technology (MU.US)$ . The reason is simple: I don't need to bet that it wins the future. I only need to bet that \"the market is too pessimistic about it. \" $280 fair value vs. $206 current price — that's a 30%+ margin of safety , layered on top of 45% annual earnings growth. Even if the multiple never re-rates, earnings growth alone will likely push the stock higher within two years.\nAMD and Micron, while offering more upside optionality, have already priced in a lot of good news . If any single element disappoints — even just one product delay, one lost customer — the drawdown will be painful.\nThis ultimately comes down to a preference question : do you like paying for \"possibility,\" or paying for \"certainty that's being undervalued\"?\nI choose the latter. But I completely understand those who choose the former.\n\n### gnews:f1af0b706051f98c7b8b3394 \n Shares of leading designer of graphics chips Nvidia (NASDAQ:NVDA) jumped 8.3% in the afternoon session after the company reported second-quarter financial results that topped Wall Street expectations and projected 70% revenue growth for the next year (fiscal 2028).\nAccording to a company press release and earnings commentary, Nvidia reported revenue of $96.22 billion for the second quarter, up 106% year-over-year, alongside adjusted earnings per share of $2.22. Both figures surpassed Wall Street consensus estimates, which called for revenue of $92.37 billion and adjusted earnings of $2.09 per share. Growth was driven by unprecedented momentum in Data Center sales, which increased 18% sequentially to reach $89.0 billion. The underlying profitability was also incredibly strong, as adjusted operating income surged 124% year-over-year to $63.96 billion and operating margin expanded to 66.2% from 60.8% a year ago.\nCEO Jensen Huang emphasized that rapid adoption of AI models is fueling massive infrastructure investments, noting accelerated growth not just from traditional hyperscalers, but from enterprise, sovereign, and AI-native start-up customers rapidly deploying Nvidia's full-stack solutions. Furthermore, the networking segment posted record sequential growth powered by Spectrum-X Ethernet and InfiniBand, while the company's new Vera CPU is expected to see revenue more than double next year.\nLooking ahead, management guided for third-quarter revenue of $108 billion at the midpoint, comfortably above analysts' expectations of $104.6 billion. The chipmaker also projected 70% revenue growth for fiscal year 2028, reinforcing investor confidence in the sustained demand for artificial intelligence hardware.\nCrucially, CFO Colette Kress explained that customer forecasts actually point to potential growth rates near double the guided 70%, but severe supply chain bottlenecks—ranging from memory availability to data center power and construction—are capping the company's near-term output. To secure long-term demand and alleviate these constraints, Nvidia highlighted new strategic revenue-sharing structures with \"NeoCloud\" partners to facilitate infrastructure financing and establish massive recurring revenue streams.\nNvidia's shares are not very volatile and have only had 7 moves greater than 5% over the last year. In that context, today's move indicates the market considers this news meaningful, although it might not be something that would fundamentally change its perception of the business.\nThe previous big move we wrote about was 15 days ago when the stock gained 3% on the news that a wave of upbeat earnings reports and bullish forecasts from key industry players signaled robust and sustained demand for artificial intelligence technology.\nThe rally was sparked by strong quarterly results from several AI-related firms, reinforcing investor confidence in the sector. Super Micro Computer, a seller of servers and other AI equipment, saw its shares jump after its earnings per share came in 84% higher than expected and it provided a revenue forecast that topped estimates.\nSimilarly, CoreWeave, which provides cloud computing for AI systems, rallied after its sales forecast also exceeded expectations. This positive sentiment echoed globally, with Asian markets gaining as traders bought into AI- and semiconductor-related shares.\nThe optimism is further supported by fundamental data, such as a reported 155% year-over-year surge in South Korea's semiconductor exports for early August.AI racks consume GPUs, CPUs, high-bandwidth memory, and storage.\nWhen a large server OEM and a major AI cloud operator both lift the path of future shipments, investors typically reprice the chip makers that supply those systems which helps explain the gains in Nvidia, AMD, and Intel.\nNvidia is up 21.6% since the beginning of the year, and at $229.73 per share, it is trading close to its 52-week high of $235.74 from May 2026. Investors who bought $1,000 worth of Nvidia's shares 5 years ago would now be looking at an investment worth $10,149.\nONE MORE THING: The $21 AI Application Stock Wall Street Forgot. While Wall Street obsesses over who's building AI, one company is already using it to print money. And nobody's paying attention.\nAI chip stocks trade at ridiculous valuations. This company processes a trillion consumer signals monthly using AI and trades at a third of the price. The gap won't last. The institutions will figure it out. You need to see this first. Read the FREE Report Before They Notice .\n\n### gnews:58fb7b4d430994417016dc48 \n Nvidia's hyper growth has been driven largely by tech's hyperscalers, which have purchased the company's graphics processing units in bulk.\nInvestors are eager to see if Nvidia can rapidly diversify its business by providing financing options to a wider swath of customers.\nJensen Huang, chief executive officer of Nvidia Corp., speaks next to a BlueField-4 STX Storage tray, from left, Vera CPU compute tray, and Spectrum-X Ethernet Photonics Switch System while holding a Spectrum-X Ethernet Photonics chip during a keynote address at the Nvidia GTC conference in San Jose, California, US, on Monday, March 16, 2026.\nNvidia has been the profit engine of the artificial intelligence boom, filling up data centers with its graphics processing units, which are also powering the biggest AI models.\nBut if there's one persistent concern among investors regarding Nvidia's ability to build on its $5 trillion market cap, it's customer concentration. Nvidia counts on the group known as the hyperscalers — Amazon , Google and Microsoft — for an outsized amount of revenue, as those companies buy GPUs in bulk for their own workloads, and sell access to them through their giant cloud businesses.\nAdd to that Meta and SpaceX , which have also been massive buyers as they build their own models, while also starting to rent out some of their Nvidia capacity to other companies that need it right away.\nIn its last earnings report in May, Nvidia changed the way it reports financials to split out hyperscalers from the rest of the pack, which it now groups as AI clouds, industrial and enterprise, or ACIE. Nvidia doesn't specify which companies fall into the former group, but CEO Jensen Huang has defined the group pretty clearly.\n\"The easiest go-to-market, of course, is the hyperscaler, because there are only five or six of them,\" Huang said on the May earnings call. \"The rest of them, the rest of the industry, represents 250,000 companies around the world.\"\nAs Wall Street gears up for Nvidia's fiscal second-quarter earnings report after the bell on Wednesday, the ACIE portion of the customer base is coming under the microscope. In the first quarter, the two segments were almost equal, with Nvidia reporting $37.9 billion in hyperscaler sales and nearly $37.5 billion in ACIE revenue. Nvidia highlighted that its ACIE revenue grew at 31% from the prior period, topping 12% growth from hyperscalers.\nSkepticism has started seeping into the stock. The shares fell 2.9% on Monday, dropping for a seventh straight day, the longest losing streak since 2022. They're down 7.5% over that stretch.\nNvidia stakeholders want the company to become less reliant on a small subset of customers, and they also worry about the ability of those companies to further bolster their spending. Amazon and Alphabet turned cash flow negative in the second quarter, and Meta's cash generation dwindled by more than 90% from a year earlier. Both of Elon Musk's public companies — SpaceX and Tesla — reported negative free cash flow as they pursue AI expansions.\n\"This story underneath the surface is investors' concern about just how sustainable the run has been for Nvidia, and feeling like the hyperscalers just can't give much more,\" Gene Munster, managing partner at Deepwater Asset Management, said in an interview. \"They want to start to see the other segment starting to kick in.\"\nNvidia in May provided nine restated quarters of history on its website, showing hyperscalers making up about 55% of data center revenue in the past year. Hyperscaler revenue soared 115% from a year earlier in the most recent quarter, topping the 74% growth from ACIE customers.\nAnalysts expect the trend to flip going forward. The ACIE side of the business is expected to show 149% annual growth over the past year to $43 billion in the second quarter, while hyperscaler revenue is likely to show growth of 83% to $43.6 billion, according to StreetAccount. One potential big driver for the hyperscaler business is SpaceX, which aims to rapidly build out Nvidia-based data centers over the next year, Munster said.\nIn total, analysts see Nvidia's revenue almost doubling from a year earlier to $92.2 billion, according to LSEG. Data center continues to account for a bigger chunk of sales, with that division expected to reach $86.3 billion, based on StreetAccount estimates. That would be 94% of the total, up from 92% in the first quarter.\nFor the full year, analysts expect 83% revenue growth to $396 billion, before slowing to 44% expansion next year, according to LSEG.\nIn its effort to diversify, Nvidia is being proactive rather than waiting for new customers to arrive. Recognizing that very few companies have the cash or balance sheet to support purchases of its rack-scale systems, Nvidia is working with Wall Street to turn GPUs into an asset class.\nEarlier this month, Nvidia unveiled a program with six leading financial firms that could pull together up to $500 billion in financing from investors who view chips as an investable asset, like real estate. Such a project, Huang said, could enable more companies to borrow money for GPU purchases at lower rates, because the chips will generate returns.\nNvidia said in the announcement that it had signed a memorandum of understanding with the investment firms. Few specifics have been presented.\n\"There's been a lot of headlines and big numbers and not a lot of details on how this stuff is going to work yet,\" Stacy Rasgon, an analyst at Bernstein, told CNBC last week. \"Color that they could give on that would be helpful to put some investors' minds at ease.\"\nAnother crucial number in Nvidia's earnings report will be sales of Vera Rubin systems, which have just recently started to ramp up. Earlier this year, Huang told attendees at the company's GTC conference that he expects $1 trillion in sales through 2027 from current-generation Blackwell and Vera Rubin.\nQuestions about the long tail of the customer base could ease if Vera Rubin looks like an even bigger success than projected.\n\"We expect Nvidia to report strong results and guidance with ramping Rubin GPU shipments representing the key drivers of upside,\" KeyBanc analysts, who recommend buying the stock, wrote in a note over the weekend.\n\n### gnews:d30249a09f7f187ba29fbbde \n Every day, businesses and governments around the world are becoming increasingly reliant on America’s frontier models. Startup CEOs already can’t imagine running their companies without AI, and it won’t be long until the same is true for every other organization in the world.\nAt the same time, it’s become abundantly clear that access to frontier models is at the mercy of Anthropic, OpenAI, and the United States government. Fable 5 was temporarily banned by the USG, and GPT 5.6 and Astra were similarly delayed. Both models have cyber, bio, and other safety safeguards that, though well-intentioned, often prevent good users from completing harmless tasks. Given recent security incidents and general worries about increasingly powerful AI, it is extremely likely that frontier model usage will only become more restricted from here. In fact, we believe it’s plausible OpenAI/Anthropic will eventually stop offering API access entirely for their most capable models.\nOpen source seems like the obvious solution to all these dependency concerns, but it’s far from a silver bullet. First, all the “open source” licenses are starting to become increasingly restrictive. As just one example, any “model as a service business” making over $20M a year must negotiate a separate agreement with Moonshot to serve Kimi K3. Second, just because a lab open sources their models today doesn’t guarantee they’ll continue doing so in the future. Imagine if in 2028, you’re stuck with 2027 level intelligence for some extremely high-value use case because no relevant model is open source and everything has to stay on-prem for security reasons. It’s kind of like having your Fable request downgraded to Opus except 100x worse.\nThe only way to fully address these concerns is to pretrain your own model that runs on your own GPUs. Until now, this has largely been a question for companies: do the potential benefits justify the enormous investment required? Soon, however, this same calculation will confront every major nation state.\nToday, we’ll do a deep dive on South Korea’s sovereign AI efforts. Beyond being home to two of the most important companies in the AI supply chain, Korea also has a long tradition of technological self-reliance (as any foreigner that’s had to use Naver Maps knows) and is currently the clear leader in sovereign AI.\nFrom there, we’ll discuss the two major implications for investors: why Nvidia is sovereign AI’s largest supporter and why Korea’s ambitions may not be aligned with Samsung and Hynix shareholders.\nKorean Squid Games - National AI Tournament: attempting to build a domestic frontier model\nIn June 2025, the Korean government announced “독자 AI 파운데이션 모델”, or the “Independent AI Foundation Model” project. As the name suggests, the goal is to develop a model that Korean organizations can train, modify, and operate without depending on a foreign AI lab.\nPerhaps the most interesting thing about the project is its structure. Rather than selecting a single national champion upfront, the government is hosting a tournament. All participants are provided subsidies for the three pillars of AI—compute, data, and researchers—and evaluated every 6 months. At each stage, losers are eliminated and have their resources reallocated towards the winners.\nThe competition began with 15 consortiums . Ten passed the initial document review, and five were selected in August 2025: Naver Cloud, LG AI Research, SK Telecom, NC AI, and Upstage. Most readers are likely entirely unaware of any of these companies’ AI efforts, but some of them are surprisingly credible. SKT, LG, and Naver all pre-trained LLMs pre-ChatGPT for example.\nThe exact subsidies varied by team. For the first round, the government rented ~3000 H100 equivalents from SKT and Naver and distributed them to the other 3 contestants. They also spent ~$45M USD on data. Most of this was paid to Korean companies for things like books, news articles, and video broadcasts, which—along with some Korean government records—were shared among all the contestants. However, they also gave each company ~$2M to buy data themselves. Lastly for researchers, the government offered each company ~$1.4M to try recruiting overseas talent, but only Upstage took them up on the offer.\nSurviving teams will be given additional resources as the tournament progresses, but the total government budget of ~$350M is still a rounding error compared to US labs. However, as we’ll explain later in this article, this is just a small portion of Korea’s planned AI investment. Additionally, their results so far show that training a decent model from scratch may be cheaper than most think.\nThe original plan was to go from 5 teams to 4 to 3 and then finally 2, with each round lasting roughly 6 months. The two winners would be selected at the end of 2026, and the government would give them additional resources to scale up their models throughout 2027.\nHowever, there was some unexpected drama at the end of round 1!\nThe 5 teams were judged 40% based on benchmark scores, 35% on expert review, and 25% on user testing. NC finished last, which means the other 4 teams should’ve moved on, but the government decided to disqualify Naver as well. This is because they used vision and audio encoders from Alibaba’s Qwen model family.\nIn Naver’s defense, these are separate neural networks whose outputs are then fed into the main LLM, and the Korean government was originally unclear about what contestants could vs couldn’t use from open source. It was only after the submissions were made that the government decided foreign architectures were allowed, but even auxiliary components must be trained and developed after initializing the weights.\nNaver maintained that they’d already developed their own encoders and could replace the Qwen pieces at any time, but the government was predictably stringent and refused to reinstate them.\nNow short 1 team, the Korean government decided to host a supplemental competition to find a new 4th contestant and ultimately selected Motif Technologies, a neolab that spun out of Moreh, a Korean AI infra software company—in February 2025.\nAll 4 companies open sourced their newest models in July and the results for the most recent round were announced on August 18th. But before we spoil the results, let’s take a look at the models.\nSo how good are their models?\nHere’s an overview of the 4 models:\nAnd here’s how they compare on the benchmarks:\nAs you can see, the two startups (Motif and Upstage) significantly outperformed teams backed by bona fide chaebols. The fact that their models are less than half the size is even more impressive.\nOn Artificial Analysis’ Intelligence Index, Motif 3 is 10 points ahead of Upstage and by far the best Korean model. What’s even more notable, however, is the fact that it’s comfortably ahead of both Inkling and Nemotron 3 Ultra—the two best American open source models today.\nMotif is a sub 30 person startup that released their first pre-trained model, which was only 2.6B total parameters, last June. They’ve raised just $17M and have access to a mere 768 B200s (< 2MW).\nIn contrast, Thinking Machines is the hottest neolab around, raised $2B at $12B post for their seed, and signed a 1GW+ compute deal with Nvidia which was accompanied by additional funding. As for Nvidia itself, the resources at its disposal hardly need mentioning.\nPut differently, a tiny Korean startup you’ve likely never heard of trained—from scratch and on a shoestring budge—the best non-Chinese open source model in the world .\nThe point here is not to diss Thinking Machines or Nvidia. Training open source models is neither of their core businesses. Instead, we want to highlight that training a near-SOTA open source model from scratch is likely less resource intensive than most think. There’s no substitute for focus, and the total compute cost to train Motif 3, including experimentation, was just ~$15M USD at today’s prices. This is obviously well within budget for every major nation state.\nQuestionable judgement from the Korean government\nWe’ve previously written in-depth about the many issues with benchmarks, but they still tend to be directionally correct. A 10+ point gap on something like the Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index has almost always corresponded to a step change improvement in model capability (e.g. GPT 4o to o1 or Opus 4.8 to Fable 5).\nWith this in mind, we were shocked to see that Motif was the one company eliminated in the latest round of competition. Maybe you could argue that the model is benchmaxxed and thus didn’t deserve to be first, but finishing dead last is baffling.\nRemember that competitors were scored on three categories: 40% benchmarks, 35% expert review, and 25% user testing. Although Motif scored the best on benchmarks, they ranked last for the other two.\nThe exact methodology for these last two sections is frustratingly opaque. For user testing, we have no idea what tasks people actually used the model for or what criteria they considered to judge the models. Additionally, none of the testing was blind, so it’s possible users had some bias towards the more famous companies.\nThe expert review section is even more questionable. Essentially, 10 external experts graded all four models on things like “development strategy”, “future plans”, and “ecosystem impact”. As for what these words actually mean, your guess is as good as ours, but it seems clear that some categories disproportionately benefit large companies like LG and SKT. We also heard that the expert reviewers were concerned Motif’s technology could be transferred overseas via foreign investors. This is quite puzzling, as one of the requirements of the competition is that all of the technical components must be open sourced.\nWe think it’s a real shame Motif will no longer get any government support now that they’ve been eliminated from the competition. The whole point of a sovereign AI program is to rally your domestic AI talent to build a high quality model. Motif demonstrated that they’re capable of exactly this, and the government should be utilizing its reach and resources to help them with things like “future plans” and “ecosystem impact”—not punish them for it.\nNow, Motif may be forced to relocate out of Korea in order to acquire the capital and compute required to continue their research . This is perhaps the most ironic possible result for the surprise standout of your national AI tournament. Hopefully other countries can do better as they pursue their own sovereign AI efforts.\nWith that being said, South Korea’s ultimate AI champion will be very well resourced. In July, they announced a monster $919B investment to build 8.4GWs by 2029 and 18.4GWs by 2035. For the first phase, SK Group, GS Group, and Naver will be building 5GW, 2.4GW, and 1GW respectively. SK Group is additionally responsible for the remaining 10GWs for phase 2.\nWe’ve already identified 3 active sites for phase 1 totaling 4.4GWs. For full details including the exact location, MW ramp, and power source, see our Datacenter Model .\nAlthough these timelines are aggressive, we believe they are achievable. The regulatory environment is favorable, and President Lee Jae-myung is explicitly framing AI infra as a legacy-defining project akin to Korea’s broadband push in the 1990s. Throughout the 2000s and 2010s, Korea had uniquely cheap and fast internet, which is what enabled their local search, e-commerce, and esports industries.\nTo be clear, not all of this capacity is intended to support Korea’s sovereign AI efforts, and much of it will likely be sold to Anthropic or OpenAI. Importantly, however, Korea will have the optionality to dramatically scale up their sovereign AI efforts if necessary, and this domestic compute ramp is something more countries outside of the US and China will likely copy.\nWhy Nvidia needs open source and sovereign AI\nJensen recently made his Twitter debut with a manifesto on the importance of open source AI. It was later co-signed by basically every relevant AI company except Anthropic. Here’s an excerpt:\n“Our AI leadership will be judged not by one frontier AI model, but by whether the United States builds a strong, open ecosystem that diffuses into every sector. This is essential for creating opportunities for innovation and prosperity across the country. It requires expanding access to AI, encouraging competition, robust application layers, and giving Americans greater control over the technology they rely on. Open weight models—AI models that anyone can download, inspect, modify, and run on their own infrastructure—are an important part of that foundation because they make advanced AI more accessible, adaptable, and widely available.”\nMuch of the discourse around open source AI tends to wax poetic about the democratization of intelligence and decentralization of technological power. However, look under the hood and it’s often just people talking their book.\nNo one needs open source AI more than Nvidia. As we previously explained to Tokenomics Model subscribers, selling frontier tokens at API prices is the highest ROI use case of incremental compute. Anthropic plus OpenAI are therefore set to take a larger and larger percentage of net new GWs in the future, and Nvidia is currently on track for a world where they only have 2 real customers. Sure maybe the number’s 7 if you include the hyperscalers, but that’s still obviously unacceptable for the world’s largest company. This is doubly true when all 7 customers except SpaceX are actively trying to build their own XPUs to cannibalize Nvidia’s margins.\nOne of the most famous competitive strategies in tech is to “ commoditize your complements ”. The general idea is that as something becomes cheaper and more abundant, demand for everything that goes well with it increases. This explains why companies will often “give away” things for “free”. Classic examples include 1) Google offering Chrome for free and open-sourcing Android, which increased access to the web and made search advertising more valuable and 2) Microsoft retaining the right to license MS-DOS to IBM’s competitors, which helped turn the PC into a commodity hardware platform.\nIn Nvidia’s case, AI models and applications are the complements to its GPUs. The long-term health of their business depends on a vibrant, diverse AI ecosystem that exists outside of just Anthropic and OpenAI.\nTheir recently announced $500B MOU with the world’s largest capital allocators is just one example of the clever financial engineering Nvidia can do to make this vision a reality. By making “Nvidia AI Factory Compute” an “investable asset class,” the SSIs and Thinking Machines of the world have a way to leverage capital from pension funds, insurance companies, and private credit firms to buy enormous amounts of compute.\nSovereign AI is another huge opportunity for Nvidia to diversify their customer base. SK Group has already committed to building 2 GWs of Rubin as part of their 5GW buildout, and Naver has signed on for another 200MW. We expect Jensen to continue announcing similar deals with additional countries.\nFrom a sovereign AI perspective, Korea’s investment in government- and corporate-owned data centers and GPU clusters makes complete sense. A sovereign model ultimately requires sovereign infrastructure: domestic control over where the model is trained, how it is deployed, and who can access the underlying compute.\nKorea’s challenge is that it still lacks a scaled domestic provider of leading-edge accelerators. Rebellions and FuriosaAI are making some progress, but they remain objectively behind Nvidia and AMD in deployment scale, software maturity, and commercial availability. Hyperscaler-developed alternatives such as Google’s TPUs and AWS’s Trainium are not domestically controlled either.\nThe buildout of data centers essentially comes down to two things: capital and compute. Capital is the easier problem in our view. The Korean government has been prioritizing AI investment over the past few years, while Naver, SK Telecom, Samsung, SK Hynix, and other leading domestic tech companies are committing significant resources to data centers, AI infrastructure, and model development. Compute on the other hand, is another story. Korea can finance land, power, cooling, and buildings, but it cannot manufacture a globally competitive accelerator ecosystem just like the majority of countries in the world—or guarantee access to the latest platforms—simply by spending more money.\nIf Korea’s objective is merely to establish a domestic AI ecosystem, a conventional supplier relationship with Nvidia may be sufficient. If it wants to become one of the world’s leading sovereign AI players, however, it will need something deeper: greater roadmap visibility, close technical integration, reliable access to new compute platforms, and support for developing the surrounding domestic ecosystem.\nThis is why Nvidia’s expanded relationships with Samsung and SK Hynix matter in this context. Samsung reportedly plans to build an Nvidia-powered AI factory using more than 50,000 GPUs, while SK Telecom’s announced 2GW DSX AI factory is expected to deploy Vera Rubin systems powered by SK Hynix HBM4. While the public announcement does not disclose preferential GPU allocation or guaranteed volumes, deeper strategic alignment should strengthen Korea’s ability to plan and deploy leading-edge compute—something far more difficult than simply constructing the data centers that house it.\nAgainst this backdrop, we believe Nvidia’s plans extend beyond just acquiring a large sovereign compute customer. As we previously explained to Memory Model subscribers, we believe Korea’s infrastructure buildout also gives Nvidia an opportunity to deepen its relationship with SK Hynix, one of its most important memory suppliers and a key affiliate within the broader SK Group. We believe Nvidia has likely secured a long-term agreement for SOCAMM, along with favorable HBM pricing and large volume commitments for 2027, although the final negotiation result could vary from our current estimate.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3; a=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/identity-probe/v2/candidates-v2-next.json\\\"))[\\\"categories\\\"][\\\"T3 one price compared across two years\\\"][\\\"order\\\"]; c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); [print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",x[\\\"input_id\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",c.execute(\\\"select text from items where input_id=?\\\",(x[\\\"input_id\\\"],)).fetchone()[0]) for x in a[7:12]]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3; a=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/identity-probe/v2/candidates-v2-next.json\\\"))[\\\"categories\\\"][\\\"T3 one price compared across two years\\\"][\\\"order\\\"]; c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); [print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",x[\\\"input_id\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",c.execute(\\\"select text from items where input_id=?\\\",(x[\\\"input_id\\\"],)).fetchone()[0]) for x in a[7:12]]'\"","aggregated_output":"\n### gnews:86bbde98330831dcfcbd4b8f \n Welcome to the Free edition of How They Make Money .\nOver 300,000 subscribers turn to us for business and investment insights.\nIn case you missed it:\n💰 Wall Street’s Top Stocks in Q2\nNVIDIA keeps outrunning the law of large numbers\nThe company just crossed $96 billion in quarterly revenue, with growth accelerating above 100%. Management now expects revenue to grow another 70% next year , adding more than $200 billion in annual revenue. At this scale, that should sound almost implausible. Yet even that outlook is supply-constrained.\nBut the story is also getting more complicated. NVIDIA is helping finance the infrastructure its customers need, while some of those same customers are developing competing chips of their own.\nSo the debate now comes down to two questions:\nHow long can the AI infrastructure boom keep compounding?\nHow much of it can NVIDIA continue to capture?\nToday at a glance:\nNVIDIA’s Q2 FY27\n1. NVIDIA Q2 FY27\nNVIDIA’s fiscal year ends in January, so the July quarter was Q2 FY27.\nData Center revenue remains off the charts, as illustrated below.\nIncome statement:\nRevenue accelerated 106% Y/Y to $96.2 billion ($4.1 billion beat).\nData Center +117% Y/Y to $89.0 billion.\nEdge Computing +27% Y/Y to $7.2 billion.\nGross margin was 75% (+3pp Y/Y).\nOperating margin was 66% (+5pp Y/Y).\nNon-GAAP EPS was $2.22 ($0.13 beat).\nCash flow:\nOperating cash flow +57% Y/Y to $24.1 billion.\nFree cash flow +59% Y/Y to $21.3 billion.\nBalance sheet:\nCash and marketable securities: $99.4 billion.\nDebt: $33.4 billion.\nQ3 FY27 Guidance:\nRevenue +12% Q/Q and +89% Y/Y to $108.0 billion ($3.4 billion beat).\nGross margin 74% (-1pp Q/Q).\nGuidance assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China.\nSo, what to make of all this?\n🚀 Growth accelerated again: Revenue growth jumped from 85% in Q1 to 106% in Q2, while Data Center accelerated from 92% to 117%. NVIDIA added nearly $15 billion of revenue sequentially, and Q3 guidance calls for another $12 billion.\n☁️ Demand is broadening: Hyperscale revenue reached $48.7 billion, while AI Clouds, Industrial, and Enterprise grew even faster to $40.3 billion. NVIDIA is increasingly benefiting from neoclouds, enterprises, and sovereign AI alongside Big Tech.\n🔄 Rubin is arriving without a digestion pause: Blackwell Ultra is still ramping, yet Rubin shipments have already started. So far, each new architecture is layering onto the previous one rather than creating an air pocket.\n📉 Margins are finally bending: Gross margin is expected to fall from 75% in Q2 to 74% in Q3 and roughly 71%-72% in Q4, largely because of higher memory costs. The pressure appears supply-driven rather than a sign of weaker demand or pricing.\n🧱 NVIDIA is locking up supply: Supply and capacity commitments jumped from $119 billion to $279 billion in three months, largely to secure memory. That's a huge vote of confidence in future demand—and a much larger commitment if the cycle eventually slows.\n🔮 The outlook remains extraordinary: NVIDIA expects roughly 70% revenue growth in FY28, far above prior Wall Street expectations. If that holds, the AI infrastructure cycle is still expanding rapidly despite NVIDIA already operating at enormous scale.\nBig picture: Growth accelerated, demand broadened, and Rubin is arriving before Blackwell has slowed. The main new wrinkle is that sustaining this pace is becoming more expensive, with memory costs pressuring margins and supply commitments rising rapidly.\n2. Business highlights\nNVIDIA is capturing more revenue from every AI factory generation.\nManagement estimates its revenue opportunity per gigawatt keeps increasing:\n~$18 billion per gigawatt with Hopper.\n~$25 billion per gigawatt with Blackwell.\nAnd now ~ $40 billion per gigawatt with Vera Rubin .\nRubin combines GPUs, CPUs, networking, and software to deliver 30x higher throughput per megawatt and 35x lower token costs than Grace Blackwell Ultra.\nProduction shipments have already started, with purchase orders from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud, and system OEM.\nNVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs and partnered with major financial institutions to help raise more than $500 billion of third-party capital for AI infrastructure.\nIt is also using credit support and take-or-pay commitments to help AI labs and neoclouds finance capacity. Critics call this circular financing. NVIDIA argues it is simply removing a capital bottleneck for customers whose demand is growing faster than their balance sheets.\nEither way, NVIDIA is moving beyond selling infrastructure to helping make that infrastructure possible. There may also be a strategic benefit: financing infrastructure today can help lock in NVIDIA deployments before competing silicon reaches scale.\nAccording to The Information, NVIDIA has agreed to acquire Hugging Face for $12.9 billion , nearly triple its 2023 valuation.\nHugging Face is one of the main hubs for developers to discover, share, and deploy open AI models, often dubbed the “GitHub of AI.” With only about $150 million in annual revenue, NVIDIA is clearly buying strategic positioning rather than near-term profits.\nThe logic ties directly to Jensen’s argument on the call: NVIDIA benefits whenever AI models proliferate. Open models are particularly attractive because startups and enterprises generally don’t build custom chips and overwhelmingly rely on existing compute infrastructure.\nOwning Hugging Face would move NVIDIA one layer closer to developers and tighten the link between open-model adoption and its broader computing platform. The risk is that NVIDIA ownership could weaken the neutrality that helped make Hugging Face so valuable in the first place.\n3. Key quotes from the earnings call\nCheck out the earnings call transcript on Fiscal.ai here .\nCEO Jensen Huang:\nOn custom chips:\n“Whereas many of these XPUs are inference-specific chips for one cloud or one service, NVIDIA is a platform, an entire AI factory platform that spans the entire AI life cycle that you can use in any cloud. It’s in every cloud. You can run anywhere. [...] I have 100% confidence that our technology will continue to be extraordinary for them and that the economics of using our technology, whether it’s from data processing to training, to post-training, to agentic processing, our technology’s going to be extraordinary for them.”\nThis is Jensen’s answer to OpenAI’s Jalapeño and the broader custom-silicon threat. His argument isn’t that customers won’t build their own chips. It’s that those chips tend to optimize specific workloads, while NVIDIA’s advantage is a fungible platform spanning training, inference, networking, CPUs, and multiple clouds. The question is whether that breadth remains valuable enough to justify NVIDIA’s premium economics.\nOn what happens when AI becomes agentic:\n“When the world goes to agentic, fully agentic systems, you are going to have agents running all the time, working with other agents running all the time. [...] I think the most important thing that matters for the industry is that, one, AI is now doing productive and useful work. Two, AI is generating profitable tokens. Three, if we had more compute, we could generate more profitable tokens, which results in more profit for all of the services. This is the exact phase where we are at, which is the reason why everybody is leaning in.”\nToday’s AI is still largely triggered by humans. Jensen believes the next phase involves millions of agents operating continuously and interacting with other agents. If that happens, inference demand stops being directly tied to human usage, and the amount of compute required could increase dramatically.\n4. What to watch next\nNVDA is up roughly 20% YTD, still outperforming the S&P 500.\nEven after the post-earnings jump, the stock trades at only about 20x forward earnings , well below the multiple it commanded earlier in the AI boom and below the rest of US Big Tech.\nThe latest 13F filings for Q2 2026 showed that hedge funds are not accumulating NVDA as much as they used to. The stock remains one of the most widely held names, although many funds are still underexposed relative to its 8% weight in the S&P 500.\nAt that valuation, the debate is less about whether NVIDIA looks cheap today and more about how durable these extraordinary earnings can be .\nIn semiconductors, a low P/E can sometimes signal peak earnings rather than a bargain. The multiple may be compressed precisely because the market is questioning whether today’s profitability is sustainable. So far, that skepticism has been repeatedly proven wrong.\nHere’s what I’m watching:\nCustom silicon: OpenAI just published the first results for Jalapeño , its custom inference chip. The company says it delivered 1.5x-1.9x more throughput per watt and materially lower latency than the NVIDIA systems tested across several models. OpenAI still plans to use NVIDIA broadly, but Jalapeño shows that NVIDIA’s largest customers have a growing incentive to move specialized inference workloads onto their own silicon.\nCircular financing: NVIDIA says AI labs receiving some form of balance-sheet support could account for roughly one-quarter of its business next year . Financing customers does not make the underlying demand fake, but it increases NVIDIA’s exposure if AI labs eventually struggle to monetize the infrastructure they are building.\nMargins and supply: Memory scarcity is expected to push gross margin down before price increases help it recover next year. The question is whether NVIDIA can continue securing sufficient supply to meet extraordinary demand without sacrificing too much of its economics.\n📉 The bear case is that AI infrastructure spending eventually outruns the profits it can generate, while custom silicon takes a growing share of inference.\n📈 The bull case is that agentic AI keeps expanding compute demand faster than efficiency gains and competition can reduce it.\nSo far, NVIDIA is still winning that race.\nThat’s it for today!\nHappy investing!\nThanks to Fiscal.ai for being our official data partner. Create your own charts and pull key metrics from 50,000+ companies directly on Fiscal.ai . Save 15% with this link .\nDisclosure: I own AAPL, AMD, AMZN, GOOG, META, MSFT, and NVDA in App Economy Portfolio . I share my ratings (BUY, SELL, or HOLD) with App Economy Portfolio members.\nThe jump in supply commitments is where the bull and bear cases almost meet. If demand keeps compounding, locking scarce memory and capacity early is brilliant. If monetization lags the buildout, those commitments become a very different kind of operating leverage. At this scale I think the risk has shifted from “is AI demand real?” toward “how much future demand is already being financed and pulled into the present?”\nThe tape's reaction is the story to me. $NVDA grew 106% and gave back the entire post-earnings move in a day, and $MRVL beat with above-consensus guidance and fell 10.3%. When the two best reports in the group get sold, expectations are the constraint, and expectations reset on time, not on numbers. Watching 227 on NVDA as the line where that changes.\n\n### gnews:59a612d6bcf31d162a9a3a8c \n Investing.com -- NVIDIA's deal to mobilize over $500 billion in third-party capital with Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs, and KKR reframes how AI factories are built and financed. By turning GPU compute into a standardized, investable asset class, NVIDIA addresses severe liquidity bottlenecks.\nHowever, the financial rewards and structural advantages flow to specific winners across the AI and financial ecosystems.\n1. NVIDIA: Offloading Debt to Safeguard Free Cash Flow\nNVIDIA emerges as the primary strategic winner because the framework shifts lending risk entirely off its balance sheet.\nUnder prior arrangements, NVIDIA acted simultaneously as a hardware supplier and debt guarantor—including an initial ~$100 billion OpenAI commitment backed by an estimated $250 billion guarantee. That structure raised intense \"circularity\" concerns that NVIDIA was effectively funding its own demand. BofA analyst Vivek Arya reiterates a Buy rating ($320 price target), plain-spokenly noting: \"The burden sits with the consortium, not NVDA's balance sheet. NVDA guarantees asset quality, not the debt, turning the bear's depreciation worry into the enabling feature.\"\nBofA estimates NVIDIA's prior vendor-financing exposure at roughly 15% of its ~$470 billion in projected free cash flow (FCF) over 2026 and 2027. Shifting that load to private capital frees up NVIDIA's massive FCF for aggressive stock buybacks. Consensus models project NVDA buybacks at ~$73 billion in 2026 and ~$106 billion in 2027—only 36% to 37% of FCF compared to NVIDIA's pledge to return over 50%.\nWells Fargo analyst Aaron Rakers (Overweight rating, $315 price target) highlights that NVIDIA is orchestrating something far bigger than chip sales: \"We see this as yet another example of how NVDA is playing a much bigger game than just a supplier in AI infra build-outs,\" citing NVIDIA's press framing that it is \"helping create a new class of productive, investable infrastructure: AI Factories.\" Because CUDA software continually extends GPU lifespan and keeps compute fungible across operators, rental yields remain elevated and depreciation curves stay benign. With BofA estimating NVIDIA holds 65% to 70% of a projected $1.7 trillion CY2030 AI systems TAM, this $500 billion financing pool serves as an early enabler of a massive long-term expansion.\n2. Neoclouds & Power Operators: Lowering the Cost of Capital\nNeocloud operators CoreWeave (NASDAQ: CRWV) and Nebius (NASDAQ: NBIS) represent the most direct public-market beneficiaries. Both saw immediate stock gains following the announcement—CoreWeave rose 1.6% and Nebius jumped 2.6%.\nAs non-investment-grade borrowers, neoclouds face high borrowing costs when trying to buy tens of thousands of expensive GPUs. Bloomberg Intelligence analyst Vasu Kasibhotla notes that private capital backing \"can ease financing constraints for CoreWeave, Nebius and other neocloud and power-site operators.\" Kasibhotla adds that \"for CoreWeave, cheaper and deeper credit can ease funding pressure from its heavy capital-spending plan. For Nebius, greater capital availability would strengthen the funding base for expansion.\"\nAccess to deeper, cheaper credit enables these operators to secure scarce power sites and expand data center capacity faster without diluting equity.\nThe six private equity and asset management giants—Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs, and KKR—gain access to a lucrative, scalable yield platform.\nInstitutional investors have long searched for large-scale, yield-generating real assets tied to digital transformation. By structuring AI data centers as an asset-backed credit class with usage-linked revenue-sharing mechanisms, these mega-firms can deploy institutional capital into long-duration, high-yield technology real estate.\nIf the $500 billion capital pool successfully accelerates physical data center construction, component suppliers further down the supply chain stand to benefit. While analysts caution that this linkage remains directional speculation—unsupported by new order data or company guidance—unbroken data center expansion directly drives hardware bill-of-materials demand.\nMemory Manufacturers (Micron, Sandisk): Unconstrained data center construction maintains tight supply dynamics for High-Bandwidth Memory (HBM) and enterprise SSDs, supporting elevated average selling prices.\nOptical Interconnects (GLW, COHR, CRDO, AAOI, LITE): As AI clusters expand to hundreds of thousands of chips, copper wiring hits physical distance and speed limits. Accelerating data center builds fuels demand for high-speed fiber and photonic components:\nMOUs Are Not Deployed Capital: Signed memorandums of understanding do not equal deployed dollars. Third-party capital still requires creditworthy end-customers paying real money for compute usage.\nDemand Pull-Forward: Vasu Kasibhotla warns that the central danger may be that financing simply \"pulls accelerator demand forward, rather than signaling broad weakness in prime AI infrastructure,\" while raising questions about backlog quality and who bears risk if actual usage trails expectations.\nPhysical Constraints Persist: Financing GPUs does not remove physical hurdles like power availability or local regulation, highlighted by cancelled projects like the UK NScale deal.\nAdded Opacity: Complex financing structures can add opacity to AI buildout economics, potentially pressuring trading multiples if debt risk becomes unclear.\nAll eyes now turn to NVIDIA's Q2 FY2027 earnings call on August 26, which analysts at BofA and Wells Fargo view as the next critical catalyst. The market expects NVIDIA to clarify its precise role in these financing platforms, its remaining vendor-financing exposure, and its go-forward buyback trajectory.\n\n### gnews:7dfab0010a8ac15786f05387 \n Record growth continued: NVIDIA reported fiscal Q2 revenue of $96 billion, more than double the prior year, with data center revenue reaching $89 billion. The company forecast $108 billion in third-quarter revenue and expects approximately 70% fiscal 2028 growth, constrained by supply.\nAI infrastructure demand is broadening: NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform began production shipments and is expected to represent about 20% of third-quarter data center revenue. AWS also plans to deploy an additional 2 million NVIDIA GPUs, while enterprise, sovereign AI and NeoCloud demand is growing rapidly.\nMargins and China remain challenges: Gross margins are expected to decline to 71%–72% in the fourth quarter because of rising memory costs before stabilizing in fiscal 2028. NVIDIA's outlook assumes no China data center compute revenue amid geopolitical uncertainty, while the company returned a record $26 billion to shareholders.\nLooking Beyond NVIDIA? These 3 AI ETFs Are Beating the Market\nNVIDIA (NASDAQ:NVDA) reported record second-quarter results for fiscal 2027, with revenue more than doubling from a year earlier to $96 billion, as demand for artificial intelligence infrastructure continued to expand across hyperscalers, cloud providers, enterprises and sovereign customers.\nChief Financial Officer Colette Kress said the company expects revenue to grow by approximately 70% in fiscal 2028, though she characterized that outlook as supply-constrained. NVIDIA forecast third-quarter revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%.\n→ Quantum Computing Is Raising the Stakes for Cybersecurity: 5 Stocks to Watch\nOpenAI's Jalapeño Chip Could Change the AI Hardware Race\n\"The surge in AI demand is driving a global infrastructure build-out,\" Kress said, citing demand from hyperscalers, AI laboratories, AI-native businesses, enterprise customers and sovereign AI initiatives.\nData center revenue rose 18% sequentially to $89 billion in the second quarter. Revenue from hyperscale customers was $49 billion, up 13% from the prior quarter, driven by demand for Blackwell products. Revenue in NVIDIA's ACIE category, which includes NeoCloud, industrial and enterprise customers, rose 25% sequentially and 138% year over year to $40 billion.\n→ Palantir's Kool-Aid Moment: The Math Behind Karp's Forecast\n5 Stocks to Buy in September Before Wall Street Catches On\nKress said NVIDIA Compute was \"fully utilized across every cloud we serve.\" She also said NeoCloud partners using NVIDIA's DSX reference designs are expected to exit the year with 8 gigawatts of installed capacity, compared with roughly 3 gigawatts at the end of 2025.\nNVIDIA announced an expanded partnership with Amazon Web Services under which AWS plans to deploy an additional 2 million NVIDIA GPUs beginning in the current quarter through the second quarter of fiscal 2029. The deployment will include Vera CPUs, some integrated with Rubin products and some deployed as standalone processors, according to Kress.\nThe company began production shipments of its Vera Rubin platform earlier in August and said it had received purchase orders from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud provider and system original equipment manufacturer. Kress said NVIDIA expects Vera Rubin to be its fastest product ramp to date and expects the platform to account for about 20% of data center revenue in the third quarter.\nNVIDIA's networking revenue also reached a record, increasing 18% sequentially. Spectrum-X Ethernet revenue grew 2.6 times from a year earlier, Kress said. The company also said it is in full production of its Vera CPU and expects CPU revenue to more than double in fiscal 2028, based on customer demand and its improving supply outlook.\nCEO Jensen Huang said the company's outlook reflects both hyperscale demand and growth in markets outside the largest cloud operators. He said NVIDIA's ACIE segment, including enterprises, NeoClouds and sovereign AI customers, represents about half of its business and is growing at an annual rate of 100%.\nHuang also highlighted the company's expanding revenue opportunity per gigawatt of data center capacity. NVIDIA estimated that opportunity at roughly $18 billion during the Hopper generation, $25 billion with Blackwell and $40 billion with Vera Rubin, including CPUs, GPUs, networking, systems and software.\n\"Our demand is much higher than\" the 70% growth outlook, Huang said, adding that the company is working with suppliers to expand capacity. He said NVIDIA's supply chain is broadly constrained and that the company has supply sufficient to support approximately 70% growth, while demand exceeds that level.\nKress said NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI laboratories and has partnered with Apollo Global Management, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield Asset Management, Goldman Sachs and KKR on financing platforms expected to raise more than $500 billion in third-party capital.\nThe company also described a revenue-sharing arrangement with NeoCloud providers. NVIDIA can provide take-or-pay commitments on part of a facility's capacity to support project financing, then share in revenue generated above a defined minimum level. Kress said the model could create a recurring, usage-linked revenue stream in addition to hardware sales over the medium to long term.\nNVIDIA said it secured land, power and shell capacity through SB Energy for a Portsmouth campus expected to support 4.25 gigawatts of AI factory capacity, with the initial deployment intended for OpenAI. OpenAI's existing and planned commitments represent approximately 12 gigawatts of NVIDIA Compute through 2030, according to Kress.\nGAAP and non-GAAP gross margin were both 75% in the second quarter, largely unchanged sequentially. Operating expenses increased 10% on a GAAP basis and 11% on a non-GAAP basis from the prior quarter, driven primarily by compute infrastructure and employee compensation costs.\nFor the third quarter, NVIDIA forecast GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points. Kress said gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in the fourth quarter before settling at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price increases take effect.\nThe company cited \"extreme pricing conditions\" in memory, saying memory cost increases have surpassed its earlier expectations. Kress said tighter memory supply is partly a result of the same AI infrastructure build-out that is supporting NVIDIA's demand.\nInventory rose to $32 billion as NVIDIA prepared for the Vera Rubin launch. Days sales outstanding increased to 60 days, reflecting extended payment terms for certain large investment-grade customer purchases scheduled for shipment across multiple quarters.\nNVIDIA said it shipped less than 1% of total data center revenue in Hopper 200 products to China-based customers during the second quarter under U.S. government licenses. The company said those shipments reduced corporate gross margins and that its forward outlook includes no China data center compute revenue because of geopolitical uncertainty.\nDuring the quarter, NVIDIA returned a record $26 billion to shareholders, including $20 billion in share repurchases and $6 billion through its quarterly dividend of $0.25 per share. The company said it had returned 60% of free cash flow year to date, above its plan to return at least 50% of free cash flow.\nNVIDIA Corporation, founded in 1993 and headquartered in Santa Clara, California, is a global technology company that designs and develops graphics processing units (GPUs) and system-on-chip (SoC) technologies. Co-founded by Jensen Huang, who serves as president and chief executive officer, along with Chris Malachowsky and Curtis Priem, NVIDIA has grown from a graphics-focused chipmaker into a broad provider of accelerated computing hardware and software for multiple industries.\nThe company's product portfolio spans discrete GPUs for gaming and professional visualization (marketed under the GeForce and NVIDIA RTX lines), high-performance data center accelerators used for AI training and inference (including widely adopted platforms such as the A100 and H100 series), and Tegra SoCs for automotive and edge applications.\nThis instant news alert was generated by narrative science technology and financial data from MarketBeat in order to provide readers with the fastest reporting and unbiased coverage. Please send any questions or comments about this story to contact@marketbeat.com.\n\n### gnews:8697580fd93d9a51eefd9876 \n It has done it again. Nvidia ( NVDA +2.53% ) blew past Wall Street estimates in its second-quarter (Q2) earnings, sending the stock back toward its all-time high share price of $225. The chipmaker now has a market cap approaching $5.5 trillion, making it the largest company in the world.\nWhere Nvidia truly shone was its guidance for the next fiscal year, which has astounded investors. In fact, it could put the company on the doorstep of becoming the first business in world history to generate $1 trillion in annual revenue, up from well under $100 billion just a few years ago.\nBut does that make the stock a buy today? Let's find out.\nFor context, let's give some numbers around Nvidia's current financial performance. Over the last 12 months, it has generated $303 billion in revenue. Last quarter, which ended in August, saw revenue grow 106% to $106 billion. If this type of growth continues for the next two quarters of this fiscal year (which ends in January of 2027), then Nvidia is likely knocking on the door of generating over $400 billion in sales in a 12-month period.\nWhat was even more impressive was the fact that Nvidia is already projecting 70% revenue growth next fiscal year, which is named fiscal year 2028. This is due to the insatiable demand from the cloud providers for artificial intelligence ( AI) computer chip systems, neoclouds like Space Exploration Technologies , and Nvidia raising prices. Add 70% growth to the $400 billion in fiscal year 2027 revenue, and Nvidia may generate $700 billion to $800 billion in revenue next fiscal year.\nThat is up from just $27 billion in revenue in fiscal year 2023, only a few years ago.\nThe math behind $1 trillion in revenue\nIf Nvidia can grow its sales by 70% next fiscal year to $700 billion, it will only need around 40% revenue growth in fiscal year 2029 to eclipse $1 trillion in sales. This feels highly achievable if AI infrastructure investments continue to grow at today's pace.\nBut will Nvidia be the first stock to $1 trillion? That is a harder question to answer because of Amazon ( AMZN +0.56% ) . Amazon is already at $775 billion in revenue, meaning it will only need two years of 15% revenue growth to eclipse $1 trillion. Its sales grew 20% year over year last quarter, mainly due to AI demand. What this means is that if Nvidia keeps growing revenue at this pace, Amazon will likely do the same and reach $1 trillion in revenue first.\nEither way, both companies -- but especially Nvidia -- are growing rapidly today because of AI.\nIs Nvidia stock a buy?\nUnlike Amazon, whose capital-intensive e-commerce and cloud operations require significant capital spending , Nvidia can offload capital spending to its hardware providers, allowing it to generate fantastic profit margins . Over the last 12 months, it has posted an operating margin of 65%, implying $650 billion in operating earnings in fiscal year 2029 if this margin is maintained and revenue grows to $1 trillion.\nThat would be up from $197 billion over the last 12 months, making Nvidia by far the most profitable business in the world. The final question is: How durable would this $1 trillion in revenue and $650 billion in earnings be? If spending on the AI boom slows -- which may happen within the next 10 years -- Nvidia's revenue could be significantly lower in the near future.\nThis makes the stock very difficult to value today, especially given its $5.5 trillion market cap. That looks cheap compared to $650 billion in earnings a few years from now, but who's to say what the average earnings power for Nvidia will be every year for the next decade?\nBy Brett Schafer – Aug 31, 2026 at 4:15AM EST\n\n### gnews:a1332af414b479a05316413f \n Apple Just Reclaimed the World's Most Valuable Company Title From Nvidia. Here Is What the Tech Rotation Means for Enterprise Strategy.\nApple has overtaken Nvidia to become the world's most valuable company, valued at $4.91 trillion, due to a shift in tech investments towards its ecosystem. This change underscores a longer-term preference for stable ecosystems over AI infrastructure in enterprise strategies.\nThis story was produced through MarketScale . See how Software & Technology teams put it to work with Executive Thought Leadership .\nKey facts, context, and what it means.\nApple is valued at $4.91 trillion, surpassing Nvidia.\nInvestment is shifting from AI infrastructure to durable technology ecosystems.\nEnterprise strategies may need to focus more on stable technology ecosystems.\nTurn your Software & Technology expertise into content.\nThe B2B signal behind the market rotation\nFor nearly a year, Nvidia owned the top of the market. On Friday, July 17, 2026, Apple took it back.\nApple reclaimed its title as the world's most valuable publicly traded company this morning, surpassing Nvidia by market capitalization as a technology rotation sent semiconductor stocks lower and Apple shares touched new highs. Apple's market capitalization reached $4.91 trillion, while Nvidia slipped to approximately $4.83 trillion following a 3.5% decline in its share price (CNBC, July 17, 2026; Wall Street Journal via MacTech, July 17, 2026).\nApple last held the title in April 2025. Nvidia had held it since June 2025, when it surpassed Microsoft, and in October 2025 became the first company in history to cross the $5 trillion market capitalization threshold (Forbes, July 17, 2026).\nApple's stock has risen approximately 57.8% over the past year, gaining 23% in 2026 alone with a 15.7% gain just in July (Wall Street Journal via MacTech, July 17, 2026; Schaeffer's Investment Research, July 17, 2026).\nThe rotation reflects a meaningful change in how investors are valuing AI exposure. Nvidia's ascent was built on the thesis that the companies building AI infrastructure, specifically the GPU chips that power model training and inference, would capture the largest share of AI economic value. That thesis drove Nvidia from a $1 trillion company in mid-2023 to the first $5 trillion company in history in under three years.\nWhat changed on Friday is that investors began pricing a different thesis: that the companies best positioned to monetize AI at scale through distribution, services, and consumer relationships may generate more durable earnings than the infrastructure layer alone. The Philadelphia SE Semiconductor index has fallen almost 19% from its all-time highs, and chip stocks are heading for their worst weekly performance in more than a year (BNN Bloomberg via Reuters, July 17, 2026).\n\"Apple was seen as a laggard in the AI race because it wasn't spending to develop models, but now sentiment has changed,\" said Toni Meadows, head of investment at BRI Wealth Management, in comments to Reuters (Reuters via BNN Bloomberg, July 17, 2026).\nThe WSJ framed Apple's position plainly: Apple has shown a steadier upward trajectory, driven by investor confidence that its dominant consumer position can solve most problems. It may have lagged behind in AI, but that has not prompted iPhone users to switch to Android devices (Wall Street Journal via MacTech, July 17, 2026).\nA key factor behind the sentiment shift is Apple's accelerating AI roadmap. The company is preparing for what 9to5Mac described as a major leadership transition, alongside the launch of a significantly updated Siri AI expected this fall, representing Apple's most substantial intelligence platform update in years (9to5Mac, July 17, 2026).\nApple is also advancing its hardware roadmap on multiple fronts. Bloomberg reported that Apple is preparing an OLED iPad Mini alongside iPad Air and low-end iPad updates planned for 2027, expanding the premium display tier across its tablet lineup. Apple has already begun selling refurbished iPad Mini units with the A17 Pro chip at a discount through its official refurbished store, according to MacRumors (Bloomberg, July 16, 2026; MacRumors, July 16, 2026).\nThe company's approach to AI differs structurally from its competitors. Rather than investing heavily in model development, Apple has pursued on-device intelligence, privacy-first architecture, and ecosystem integration, distributing AI capabilities through the hardware and services layer it already controls rather than racing to build frontier models from scratch.\nThe Apple-Nvidia market cap swap is not just a financial story. It is a signal about where enterprise technology investment is heading in the second half of 2026.\nThe first phase of enterprise AI investment, from 2023 through early 2026, was dominated by infrastructure: GPU procurement, data center buildout, model training, and cloud capacity expansion. That phase rewarded Nvidia, hyperscalers, and the companies building the physical and software stack that AI runs on.\nThe rotation now underway reflects growing confidence that the second phase, monetization through distribution, devices, and services, is beginning to materialize. Apple's ecosystem of approximately 2.2 billion active devices represents one of the largest and most loyal distribution networks in the history of consumer technology.\nFor B2B technology leaders, the rotation carries a practical planning signal. Enterprise hardware refresh cycles, software procurement decisions, and device management strategies are all influenced by where Apple takes its AI platform over the next 12 to 18 months. The new Siri AI this fall, the OLED iPad Mini in 2027, and the broader Apple Intelligence rollout across Mac, iPad, and iPhone represent a sustained enterprise touchpoint expansion that IT and procurement teams should be tracking now, not after the product launches.\nThe $5 trillion threshold is next. Apple's stock touched $4.9 trillion on Friday and the company's trajectory points toward joining Nvidia as the only companies in history to cross that mark (9to5Mac, July 17, 2026).\nEvery story in MarketScale Software & Technology starts with a company putting its solutions engineers, product teams, and customer engineers on the record. Buyers are already reading this topic. The only question is whose experts they find.\nBuyers ask AI engines who to consider, and published expert answers are what those engines cite.\nMarketScale gives Software & Technology B2B marketing teams a full content studio: record, produce, and distribute your own channel. No agency, no crew, no guessing.\nBefore they reach out, Software & Technology buyers ask AI engines which vendors to trust. Explore how your experts, customers, and partners can become useful content for buyers and AI search.\nThis article was produced through MarketScale. The same platform turns your solutions engineers, product teams, and customer engineers into the articles, video, and social content Software & Technology buyers are searching for. Start a free trial and see it with your own people. For qualifying companies, no credit card, no demo required.\nUber deployed ChatGPT Work to more than 30,000 employees seven weeks after cutting about 3,300 jobs (10% of corporate staff). The company framed layoffs as organizational restructuring, while the rollout expands a pilot that embedded AI engineers with teams to automate pieces of their work.\n01 Uber expanded ChatGPT Work from a 1,000-person pilot to over 30,000 employees across operations, finance, marketing, and product teams.\n02 During the pilot, 80% of early adopters were non-engineers, and AI pods of 30 engineers paired with teams to automate work such as analyzing operational data, localizing training materials, and building dashboards.\n03 Uber’s rollout shows AI deployments can go beyond licenses: curated integrations, embedded technical support, an internal skills library, and plans to measure adoption and outcomes.\nTwo critical unauthenticated remote code execution flaws in PHP (CVE-2026-109618 and CVE-2026-109619) with CVSS 9.2 scores affected 34.4 million vulnerable services as of October 23, with no patches available for PHP 8.1 and older. Exploitation attempts reached millions within hours of disclosure, though confirmed compromises remain unproven, while attackers tested a Mirai-derived botnet variant against production servers.\nUNICOM Engineering chief technologist Austin Hypes compares rear door, single-phase, two-phase and immersion cooling for AI racks on Flawless Execution. He says two-phase cold plates use dielectric refrigerant and can avoid chillers and evaporative towers in hot, dry sites, and immersion can keep servers up ten to 15 minutes and sometimes an hour or more after a cooling loss. Schneider Electric engineers cite estimates that single-phase holds about 55% of the 2026 liquid cooling market.\n01 A Motivair by Schneider Electric engineer puts single-phase cold plates at an estimated 55% of the 2026 liquid cooling market and expects them to dominate for five to 10 years.\nStories like this one run on content MarketScale captures from real practitioners. See how your team's expertise becomes coverage in Software & Technology and beyond.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3; a=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/identity-probe/v2/candidates-v2-next.json\\\"))[\\\"categories\\\"][\\\"T3 one price compared across two years\\\"][\\\"order\\\"]; c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); [print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",x[\\\"input_id\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",c.execute(\\\"select text from items where input_id=?\\\",(x[\\\"input_id\\\"],)).fetchone()[0]) for x in a[12:15]]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3; a=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/identity-probe/v2/candidates-v2-next.json\\\"))[\\\"categories\\\"][\\\"T3 one price compared across two years\\\"][\\\"order\\\"]; c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); [print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",x[\\\"input_id\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",c.execute(\\\"select text from items where input_id=?\\\",(x[\\\"input_id\\\"],)).fetchone()[0]) for x in a[12:15]]'\"","aggregated_output":"\n### gnews:73b5d87b6d484d8e54c69370 \n Korean fried chicken with the chairmen of SK Group and LG . Yellowtail sashimi at Nobu with Larry Ellison and Elon Musk. Mapo tofu and whiskey with TSMC founder Morris Chang.\nPeople are so curious about where Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang eats, there's a blog that tracks his sumptuous dinners out around the world. But the real tea, said Huang during the chipmaker's blowout second-quarter earnings call on Wednesday, is who he dines with.\n\"One of the funnest things to do is just to go figure out where I go for dinner, and who I have dinner with,\" said Huang during the quarterly call that saw Nvidia's stock jump more than 4% in after-hours trading. \"Their stock price doubles the next day.\"\nHuang was responding to a request from an analyst at Goldman Sachs to rank the most acute constraints on Nvidia's ability to meet customer demand, against the backdrop of the blockbuster 70% revenue growth the company projected for next fiscal year. The figure was significantly higher than analysts' 44% estimate, but Huang and Nvidia CFO Colette Kress specified that there were still heavy supply constraints holding back the $5 trillion chipmaker. The analyst asked Huang to rank items like data center power, shell availability, memory pricing, and wafer foundry access that might be limiting growth.\n\"There's something funny I could say, but I'm going to just not,\" quipped Huang. \"I think the answer is our entire supply chain is challenged, and everybody is really running flat out and more capacity is coming online all the time, which is one of the advantages.\"\nNvidia currently has supply for 70% of the demand from current customers, said Huang during the call, adding that demand is \"much higher than that.\" He said the company doesn't like to disappoint customers but it will take the help of the entire supply chain to get more compute capacity available.\nThe tech giant jolted investors on Wednesday with a blockbuster second quarter earnings report, posting $96.2 billion in revenue, well above analysts' $92.2 billion consensus estimate. Nvidia also projected that it would grow revenue 70% next year, meaning revenue could soar more than $100 billion above where analysts were modeling. The stock rallied more than 4% on the preliminary forecast.\nHuang didn't name a restaurant, dinner companion or supplier, for that matter, but the Nvidia CEO famously eats dinner out while he travels around the globe and photographers have caught him tasting local dishes, tipping servers, and taking photos with crowds outside of restaurants and night markets where he eats.\nIn 2024, Oracle founder Ellison said he and SpaceX CEO Musk had taken Huang out to dinner at Nobu Palo Alto and \"begged\" Huang for more GPUs.\n\"I would describe the dinner as Oracle—me and Elon begging Jensen for GPUs,\" Ellison recalled in 2024. \"Please take our money. Please take our money. By the way, I got dinner. No, no, take more of it. We need you to take more of our money please.\"\nIn June, the \"Jensen Eats\" blog reported he stopped over in Korea and visited a barbecue restaurant with a name that translates to \" Hey brother, it's me! .\" He ate with SK Group Chairman Chey Tae-won, LG Group Chairman Koo Kwang-mo, and Naver founder Lee Hae-jin. According to tech founder Dan Liu, who writes the blog, the crew dined on grilled meat wrapped in perilla leaves, a dish called ssam. Liu wrote that because LG's Koo is the youngest, he grilled pork for the group.\nIn the run-up to the group outing , LG rose by 30% on reports that Koo would meet with Huang, and LG affiliates followed suit with increases for its listed units ranging from 17% to 29%.\nThe halo effect even extends to where Huang eats. He had chicken and beers with Samsung Electronics chairman Jay Y Lee and Hyundai Motor executive chair Chung Euisun last October and shares of poultry processing company Cherrybro rose, along with fried chicken company Kyochon F&B. Co., and fried-chicken robot company Neuromeka Co., Bloomberg reported .\nAs for meeting customer demand, Huang said Nvidia is trying to be as transparent as possible as more capacity comes online in dribs and drabs every day. He did not, however, disclose where he's eating dinner tonight.\n\n### gnews:f0ded626a98986d022fd2c30 \n Nvidia ( NVDA -4.40% ) was a relatively obscure chipmaker specializing in gaming technology before artificial intelligence (AI) exploded a few years ago, sending the stock to the No. 1 spot as the world's most valuable company. It held on to that title for most of 2025 as Nvidia enjoyed outstanding growth, while the next-most-valuable company, Apple , struggled to gain traction in AI.\nHowever, Apple stock is back in the market's favor, gaining 27% over the past three months, while Nvidia stock has dropped 8%. Apple is now back in the world's most-valuable-company slot.\nAs the market hears more updates from the largest tech companies, is there more bad news on the way for Nvidia?\nAI chip stocks have been plummeting recently as the market prices in worries about AI spending and how long the cycle can last. However, while companies like Alphabet and Amazon continue to spend lavishly on upgrading their AI platforms, with both companies planning to spend around $200 billion in 2026 alone, the updates are actually a good sign for Nvidia.\nNvidia works with both Amazon and Alphabet, as well as nearly every major AI company. Both clients, for example, make some of their own AI chips, but they also contract with Nvidia to offer their cloud clients the best available AI development platform. Nvidia is the gold standard , and while Amazon's chips offer budget options and Alphabet's Tensor Processing Units (TPUs) offer specialized task efficiency, Nvidia offers the most powerful capabilities. As these companies invest, a large chunk goes to Nvidia.\nNvidia's CEO Jensen Huang noted that total AI spend is expected to be $1 trillion next year and $3 trillion to $4 trillion by the end of the decade. He thinks that Nvidia will generate $1 trillion in revenue from 2025 through 2027 from the Blackwell and Rubin lines alone, and every time a hyperscaler announces greater investments in AI infrastructure, investors should envision some of that landing on Nvidia's doorstep.\nAlthough Nvidia should enjoy continued growth if AI spending stays strong, the market is worried that this can't continue. A separate but connected piece for Nvidia is that revenue growth is accelerating right now, but as soon as that slows down, the stock won't be worth quite as much. On a price-to-sales basis, Nvidia stock trades at 19 times trailing-12-month sales.\nNvidia may continue to face pressure as the market weighs the future of AI, and investors should temper their expectations in the near term.\nBy Jennifer Saibil – Aug 1, 2026 at 5:25AM EST\n\n### gnews:c2fdbe0959518bba7d3baa10 \n Why So Many Value ETFs Are Loaded with Big Tech\nAre Nvidia’s Growing Circular Financing Deals a Concern?\nCybersecurity Stocks Riding the AI (Danger) Boom\nMarkets Brief: Value ETFs Making a Big Tech Bet, Nvidia’s Dealmaking, and the Cybersecurity Stock Outlook\nThe ripples from the AI stock boom are even showing up in value-stock ETFs.\niShares Russell 1000 Value ETF\n(ILCV)\nThis article mentions funds that have an issuer-initiated rating and/or track a Morningstar Index. For full disclosure information, please refer to the specific funds, which are demarcated with a * symbol, listed below.\nStocks are heading into September with the Morningstar US Total Market Index up a respectable 14% for the year. After a volatile start to 2026, we’re seeing yet another run driven by technology stocks, particularly the names seen benefiting the most from the artificial intelligence buildout.\nAs we have been documenting , market concentration within tech has only increased this year, and not decreased as many strategists expected. This week’s Markets Brief looks at how it’s getting harder for investors to diversify away from tech. Even value funds may not be a haven. Also on tap is a deeper dive into Nvidia’s NVDA financing deals, which have some investors nervous, as well as a look at why cybersecurity stocks are turning into AI winners.\nInvestors turning to value-stock strategies often try to counterbalance the growth side of a portfolio heavy in volatile tech stocks. But investors in many value-stock ETFs may be making much bigger bets on tech than they realize. “We are seeing more and more stocks that people think of as large growth and tech being included in value indices and ETFs,” explains Morningstar associate manager research analyst Brian Paoli.\nOne example of this trend is the $84 billion iShares Russell 1000 Value ETF IWD , the fourth-largest ETF in the Morningstar large value category. These days, it has about 20% of its portfolio in technology stocks (using Morningstar sector designations), with Apple AAPL as its second-largest position and Microsoft MSFT ranking third. Meanwhile, the ETF’s largest position is a roughly 6% weighting in Amazon AMZN , which is classified as a consumer cyclical rather than a tech stock. In other words, more than a quarter of the iShares Russell 1000 Value ETF is in tech-related investments.\nIt wasn’t always this way. The amount of technology stocks in the iShares strategy has risen sharply over the past year and a half. At the end of 2024, tech was 11% of the fund.\nAt the same time, technology stocks have played a greater role in driving performance. In 2024, financials, including JPMorgan JPM and Berkshire Hathaway BRK.A , were the ETF’s top two contributors, followed by Walmart WMT and GE Aerospace GE . So far in 2026, tech stocks have been the catalyst. Out of the fund’s 23.8% year-to-date return, they have contributed 11.5 percentage points, with Amazon adding another 0.84 points. The top seven contributors to the fund’s returns in 2026 have been tech stocks, along with Amazon. (Apple clocks in at number 10.)\nThe iShares Russell 1000 Value ETF is far from alone. The $50 billion iShares S&P 500 Value ETF IVE clocks in at nearly 21% tech stocks, and the iShares Morningstar Value ETF\nat nearly 24%. But these don’t even crack the top 40 in terms of tech exposure among large value ETFs with more than $100 million in assets. The largest value-stock ETF, the $256 billion Vanguard Morningstar Value ETF, is at the low end, with 13% tech.\nWhy is Big Tech appearing in value strategies even as Apple and Microsoft have been growing earnings at an annual rate of around 20%? Paoli says the answer lies in the intersection of stock characterization methodologies and market dynamics. Value and growth designations are relative, not based on absolute numbers. Index providers (including Morningstar) evaluate stocks via different metrics and then rank them to determine the growth/value line. With some approaches, stocks with a blend of growth and value attributes end up in both value and growth indexes.\nMeanwhile, the intense rally in semiconductor stocks has lifted both valuations and market caps at a time when their earnings growth has exploded. In addition, a large, fast-growing IPO like SpaceX SPCX can add to the ranks of stocks at the growth end of the spectrum. Together these factors have pushed relatively slower-growth and lower-valuation Big Tech names, such as Apple and Microsoft, to the value side.\n“AI has become large enough to influence not only stock prices but also style classifications, sector composition, factor exposures, and benchmark concentration,” writes Scott Froidl, co-head of manager research at LPL Financial. “Technology now plays an increasingly important role in both growth and value indexes. The traditional boundaries separating investment styles are beginning to blur.”\nWhen is a surprise not a surprise? When Nvidia blows away earnings forecasts. But a bigger question for many investors is the company’s web of financing arrangements with clients and other financial commitments totaling hundreds of billions of dollars. These deals were a big focus during the company’s conference call. “We recognize the scale of this support, and we know some will call this circular financing. We see it differently,” said Colette Kress, Nvidia’s chief financial officer.\nBrian Colello, who follows Nvidia for Morningstar, has argued that these financing arrangements are a smart strategy for the giant chipmaker. Here are four of his key takeaways from the company’s conference call:\nNvidia’s supply chain commitments spiked in the second quarter, rising to $279 billion versus $119 billion a quarter ago, as the company procured memory chips ahead of its Vera Rubin and Vera Rubin Ultra rack-scale product rollouts in the quarters ahead. We’re not concerned with this higher level of commitments given the firm’s glowing near-term revenue forecast. The company also summarized its variety of leases, cloud service commitments, and financing guarantees (mainly its recent deal with OpenAI), reporting maximum exposure of $165 billion for deals done thus far. For context, this is less than 40% of the free cash flow we expect Nvidia to earn in fiscal 2028 from its AI hardware sales. Even if most of these deals fall through, it would make an AI bust feel a bit more painful for the company, but we think Nvidia can weather such a storm if one were to arise.\nRegarding the OpenAI deal, which is by far the biggest one, our takeaway is that OpenAI and Anthropic have simply outgrown the VC industry. In any normal scenario, VC would fund the needs of a startup. However, because both have grown so large and are in much more capital-intensive businesses, they require additional funding. This is where Nvidia has stepped in, both with its own support (the $105 billion for OpenAI) and networking to arrange financing from others—namely, the $500 billion partnerships with Apollo, KKR, etc.\nNvidia is stressing that its AI gear is “fungible and durable.” If its main customer can no longer afford its GPUs, the firm can rent them out to others or deploy them for its own purposes. Given that we’re in the early innings of agentic and physical AI, we think this capacity will get soaked up, even in the unlikely chance that the main customer can’t use it.\nNvidia considers its AI gear an investable asset and this financing akin to automobile financing. These GPUs may have 5-10-year lives—a little less than a car, but a bit more durable than past tech like PCs and smartphones.\nCybersecurity stocks were an early target of the “AI loser” trade. But over the last few months, they’ve turned into winners. CrowdStrike’s earnings report last week showed why. Morningstar equity analyst Malik Ahmed Khan says the company’s strong top-line reacceleration was “driven by increased spending on cyber solutions as enterprises beef up defenses against increasingly capable AI-driven cyber-attacks. In this arms-race-type scenario, cyber vendors are seeing clear business wins.”\nKhan thinks this reflects a broader trend. Here’s what he says:\nCyber budget expansion due to AI is real. It is showing up in actual results and forward-looking metrics.\nThis is not a “quarter story.” We expect this to be a multi-year tailwind for the sector.\nWe accelerated cyber software spending in 2027, above our forecast from our landscape.\nMost of the spending is accruing to platform vendors, with customers clearly seeking to buy more from less. This disproportionately benefits CrowdStrike CRWD and Palo Alto Networks PANW .\nThe two undervalued names in our coverage are Okta OKTA (identity) and Zscaler ZS (network). We see upside to both names. Based on recent trends and commentary from other vendors, we expect Zscaler’s upcoming report to be strong.\nZscaler and Palo Alto will report quarterly results next week and provide annual guidance, which we can test against our thesis.\nTom Lauricella is Senior Editorial Director, Global Markets, for Morningstar. Email Tom at tom.lauricella@morningstar.com.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3; a=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/identity-probe/v2/candidates-v2-next.json\\\"))[\\\"categories\\\"][\\\"T3 one price compared across two years\\\"][\\\"order\\\"]; c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); [print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",x[\\\"input_id\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",c.execute(\\\"select text from items where input_id=?\\\",(x[\\\"input_id\\\"],)).fetchone()[0]) for x in a[15:17]]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3; a=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/identity-probe/v2/candidates-v2-next.json\\\"))[\\\"categories\\\"][\\\"T3 one price compared across two years\\\"][\\\"order\\\"]; c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); [print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",x[\\\"input_id\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",c.execute(\\\"select text from items where input_id=?\\\",(x[\\\"input_id\\\"],)).fetchone()[0]) for x in a[15:17]]'\"","aggregated_output":"\n### gnews:4f85affb9aaa7d877e4539c2 \n Nvidia will not ship a single new gaming GPU in 2026. Not a refresh, not a Super variant, not a budget card. It’s the first calendar year without a new GeForce launch since the original GeForce 256 shipped in October 1999, according to industry reporting tracking Nvidia’s release cadence – and a July 2026 Tech Insider analysis flagged 2026 as the year that could snap Nvidia’s roughly 30-year streak of annual GPU launches. The RTX 50 series, which launched across 2025, remains the current lineup with no successor in sight until at least late 2027, the earliest window cited by leaker Kopite7kimi and reported by Notebookcheck in January 2026. Nvidia’s own product reporting as of March 2026 still listed RTX 50 Series ownership as its current GeForce generation with no RTX 60 Series announced at that point, and that remains the case as of this October 2026 update.\nWhy Nvidia Skipped Gaming GPUs Entirely in 2026\nWhat Gamers Actually Get in 2026: DLSS 4.5, Not New Silicon\nThe RTX 5090 SE Rumor That Nvidia Never Confirmed\nNvidia’s 2026 Gaming GPU Roadmap vs Prior Generations\nHistorical Context: Has This Happened Before?\nCompetitive Landscape: RTX 50 vs Radeon RX 9000 in Mid-2026\nWhat This Means for GeForce NOW and Cloud Gaming\nShould You Buy a GPU Now or Wait?\n5 Predictions for the GPU Market Through 2027\nIs Nvidia really not releasing any new gaming GPUs in 2026?\nWhen will the RTX 60 series release?\nWhat is the RTX 5090 SE and is it real?\nWhat is RTX Spark used for?\nShould I buy an RTX 5090 or RTX 5080 now, or wait for the next generation?\nIs AMD launching new gaming GPUs in 2026 either?\nDoes DLSS 4.5 make older RTX cards feel new again?\nHas Nvidia ever skipped a full year of gaming GPU launches before?\nThat gap has turned “rtx 60 series release date” into one of the more searched GPU queries of August 2026, pulling roughly 880 monthly searches in the US alone – the same month a Tech Insider report made the core claim explicit: no new gaming GPU is arriving in 2026 at all. Gamers who assumed a new architecture would land on the usual two-year cycle are instead watching Nvidia pour its engineering resources into an entirely different product: the RTX Spark, an AI PC superchip announced with Microsoft that has nothing to do with gaming frame rates. This piece breaks down what’s actually happening, why it’s happening now, and what it means for anyone shopping for a graphics card before 2027.\nNvidia’s gaming GPU release pattern has held for nearly three decades: a new architecture roughly every 18 to 24 months, usually accompanied by a mid-cycle Super or Ti refresh. The RTX 40 series launched in 2022, the RTX 50 series followed in January 2025, and under the historical cadence a Super refresh or an early RTX 60 tease should have landed sometime in 2026. Neither happened – and it’s getting later, not earlier. As recently as September 2025, Tom’s Hardware had projected that Rubin-based consumer cards could still arrive as soon as late 2026 or early 2027, but that window has since slipped hard: Tom’s Hardware and NotebookCheck reported in February 2026 that Nvidia’s internal mass-production target for the next generation had already slipped from the end of 2027 into 2028, and The FPS Review followed in March 2026 with its own reporting that mass production may not begin until 2028 rather than late 2027, with no confirmed launch month attached to any of these estimates.\nThe reporting from Digital Foundry and other outlets covering Computex 2026 in early June points to a schedule slip rather than a cancellation. Nvidia’s next true gaming architecture, code-named Rubin, isn’t expected before the second half of 2027 at the earliest – a 2H 2027 window that Club386 and TechSpot’s January 2026 reporting pegged as no earlier than roughly June 2027 – with some leakers pushing that timeline into 2028, a date now echoed by both Tech Insider in July 2026 and Tom’s Hardware in February 2026. A separate rumor round-up from Digital Foundry and GamesRadar in June 2026 described the slip as at least a 12-month gap measured against where a hypothetical RTX 50 Super refresh would have landed, though even that round-up stopped short of naming a firm date, price, or spec sheet. PCGuide’s own tracking, first published in August 2025 – when the site still expected RTX 60 no earlier than 2026, before later reporting pushed that estimate back – and updated again in August 2026, now frames the arrival window as 2027-2028, and TechSpot’s January 2026 roundup of leaker reporting landed on that same late-2027-to-2028 consensus. By September 2026, the 2028 date had hardened further still: Tom’s Hardware reported that month that Rubin-based RTX 60 GPUs might not launch before 2028, and TweakTown’s own September 2026 reporting found multiple independent leakers converging on that same 2028 window. The reason isn’t a design failure or manufacturing hiccup at TSMC. It’s that Nvidia’s engineering and wafer allocation are being funneled into data center AI accelerators and, more recently, into a new category of AI-first PC hardware that competes for the same 3nm and advanced packaging capacity that would otherwise go to a gaming die.\nWafer capacity at TSMC’s leading nodes is the real constraint. Every Blackwell-class die Nvidia builds for a gaming card is a die it isn’t building for a data center customer paying substantially more per unit. With AI infrastructure demand still outstripping supply through 2026, Nvidia’s own incentives point toward filling data center and now consumer AI-PC orders first, leaving gaming as the segment that waits.\nInstead of a new GeForce card, Nvidia used its Computex 2026 keynote to introduce RTX Spark, a dual-die “superchip” built for Windows AI PCs and announced jointly with Microsoft. According to Nvidia’s official announcement from late May 2026, RTX Spark pairs a 20-core Grace CPU (Arm-based) with a Blackwell-class RTX GPU carrying 6,144 CUDA cores and fifth-generation Tensor Cores with FP4 precision support. The two dies communicate over Nvidia’s NVLink-C2C chip-to-chip interconnect rather than a traditional PCIe bus, and the package ships with 128GB of LPDDR5x memory delivering roughly 300GB/s of bandwidth. Nvidia confirmed RTX Spark’s worldwide launch date as October 16, 2026, opening preorders that same month with the product listed directly alongside the GeForce RTX 5090 and RTX 5080 on Nvidia’s own site – and still with no RTX 60 Series pricing or SKU anywhere in that October lineup.\nNone of that spec sheet is aimed at gaming benchmarks. RTX Spark is built to run local AI agents, large language model inference, and Copilot-style workloads directly on a desktop or laptop without round-tripping to the cloud. Nvidia’s framing, according to its own press materials, is “reinventing Windows PCs for the age of AI” – a strategic pivot that treats the PC as an AI inference endpoint first and a gaming rig second. Coverage from Tom’s Hardware’s Computex day-zero wrap-up described RTX Spark as an “assault on laptop and desktop markets,” positioning it against Intel’s incoming Xeon 6-based AI PC chips and Qualcomm’s Snapdragon X lineup, not against AMD’s Radeon gaming cards.\nThe bigger picture: this is the same Blackwell architecture family that powers Nvidia’s data center GPUs. The company also confirmed at GTC 2026 that an RTX PRO 4500 Blackwell Server Edition GPU is headed to Amazon EC2, according to Nvidia’s GTC 2026 live blog . Blackwell silicon is being sliced up for servers, AI PCs, and workstations – everywhere except a new gaming card.\nThe one gaming-relevant update Nvidia shipped this year is software, not hardware. Nvidia had already scheduled DLSS 4.5 Multi Frame Generation for RTX 50 Series owners on March 31, 2026, and by June 2026 the company counted more than 1,000 RTX-enabled games and apps across its ecosystem, including 007: First Light , which Nvidia scheduled for a May 27, 2026 release with DLSS 4.5 support built in. DLSS 4.5 Ray Reconstruction itself then rolled out in August 2026 to existing RTX GPUs, introducing a second-generation transformer model that unifies denoising and Super Resolution into a single AI network instead of running them as separate passes. We covered the technical rollout in detail in our DLSS 4.5 Ray Reconstruction breakdown , but the strategic read is simple: Nvidia is extending the useful life of RTX 40 and RTX 50 series cards through driver and AI-model updates – and by October 2026, Nvidia’s own GeForce coverage had already moved on to previewing DLSS 5, still without attaching any release date to an RTX 60 Series card.\nThat’s a deliberate move. If there’s no new gaming GPU to sell, keeping current-generation cards competitive through software is the only lever left. It also buys goodwill with the installed base of RTX 40 and RTX 50 owners who would otherwise feel stranded on hardware that’s a full generation removed from Nvidia’s marketing spotlight for the entirety of 2026.\nSpeculation filled part of the vacuum. Leaks pointed to a cut-down RTX 5090 SE built on the same GB202 silicon as the retail RTX 5090, but scaled back to 110 streaming multiprocessors (roughly 14,080 CUDA cores) against the full 170 SM count on the standard 5090, paired with 32GB of GDDR7 on a 384-bit bus and a 500W TGP. As of mid-July 2026, Nvidia had made no official announcement, and at least one outlet, Club386, published a direct denial that any RTX 5090 SE was in the pipeline. Treat this one as unconfirmed until Nvidia says otherwise; it illustrates the appetite for any scrap of new-hardware news during a year when the actual roadmap has gone quiet.\nNvidia’s gap year should, in theory, be AMD’s moment. It hasn’t quite worked out that way. AMD’s next consumer architecture, RDNA 5 (sometimes referred to under the UDNA branding as the company unifies its gaming and compute lines), is also tracking to mid-2027 or later rather than a 2026 launch. Computex 2026 hardware showcases, including an ASrock prototype Phantom Gaming card, were still built around the existing RDNA 4 flagship, the RX 9070 XT, dressed up with new cooling and a 12V-2×6 power connector rather than new silicon underneath.\nAMD did make one real move this year: the global launch of the Radeon RX 9070 GRE, a trimmed-VRAM variant that we covered in our RX 9070 GRE global launch report . That’s a market-expansion play, not a new-generation launch, and it underscores that both major GPU vendors are managing the current generation for another year rather than racing to replace it.\nIntel, meanwhile, is chasing a different opportunity. Leaks point to a budget Panther Lake-based chip with eight CPU cores and four Xe3 graphics cores aimed squarely at gaming handheld manufacturers looking for a cheaper alternative to flagship silicon – a segment Intel already has a foothold in through cards like the Arc B580, which we compared directly against Nvidia and AMD’s budget options in our Arc B580 vs RTX 5060 vs RX 9060 XT comparison .\nRTX 40 series (Ada Lovelace)\nRTX 50 series (Blackwell)\nRTX 60 series (Rubin, expected)\nThe gap between RTX 50 and the still-unnamed next generation is already tracking to be the longest in the RTX era, and GamesRadar reported in September 2026 that it could stretch into a 20-year record gap between GPU generations if Rubin keeps sliding toward 2028 as leakers now suggest. As of September 2026, that’s no longer just a projection: iTechGuides confirmed that month that Nvidia’s own public product information still lists the RTX 50 series as its confirmed consumer generation, with no RTX 60 model announced, priced, or released, and none of the reporting tracking the launch – from Tom’s Hardware to NotebookCheck to Digital Foundry – has moved beyond rumor and delay coverage to anything resembling a confirmed product.\nA generation-length pause in new gaming GPU launches has ripple effects well beyond enthusiast forums. With no successor pressuring prices downward, RTX 50 series cards have held value far longer than any recent generation. We documented this directly in our RTX 4090 vs RTX 5080 used-market analysis , where used RTX 4090 pricing actually climbed relative to the newer RTX 5080 rather than depreciating on the usual curve, because buyers have no reason to wait for a replacement that isn’t coming.\nThat pricing dynamic is compounding with a separate problem: the 2026 DRAM and memory shortage that’s already pushed component and device prices up across the board. We covered how that shortage forced Apple to raise iPad and Mac prices by as much as 29% in our DRAM shortage report , and GDDR7 memory used in GPUs isn’t insulated from the same supply crunch. With no new architecture to reset pricing expectations and memory costs rising independently, RTX 40 and RTX 50 series cards are proving unusually sticky at current price points well into their third year on shelves.\nNone of this is happening by accident. Nvidia’s data center segment has dwarfed gaming revenue for several years running, and every wafer allocated to a GeForce die is a wafer not sold to a hyperscaler at data-center margins. The RTX Spark launch with Microsoft extends that same math into the consumer PC market: an AI PC superchip carries software and platform strategy value (tying Windows Copilot features to Nvidia silicon) that a traditional gaming refresh doesn’t. From a purely financial standpoint, 2026 wafer capacity spent on RTX Spark and data center Blackwell parts likely generates more revenue per wafer than the same capacity spent on a GeForce refresh aimed at a gaming market that isn’t demanding one as urgently as AI buyers are.\nNvidia has stretched a generation before – the RTX 20 to RTX 30 gap ran close to two years, and RTX 30 to RTX 40 also landed near the 24-month mark – but a full calendar year with zero new gaming GPU SKUs of any kind, not even a Super refresh or a rebadge, has no precedent since the GeForce 256 debuted in 1999 and kicked off the branded GPU category. Even during the 2020-2022 chip shortage, when Ampere cards were nearly impossible to buy at MSRP, Nvidia still launched new SKUs; it just couldn’t supply them in volume. 2026 is different: supply isn’t the blocker, allocation is.\nCurrent high-end, holding price per our RTX 5080 vs RX 9070 XT comparison\nStill AMD’s flagship gaming card, no RDNA 5 in 2026\nBudget tier, no successor confirmed for 2026\nEvery major GPU vendor is effectively frozen on its current generation through the rest of 2026. That’s an unusual moment of competitive stasis across the entire discrete GPU market simultaneously, not just at Nvidia.\nNvidia’s cloud gaming arm hasn’t slowed down even as the desktop GPU roadmap stalled. GeForce NOW continued its RTX 5080-tier rollout through 2026, and we detailed the pricing and server upgrade path in our GeForce NOW RTX 5080 rollout coverage . That’s a meaningful signal: Nvidia can keep upgrading the silicon that powers its subscription streaming service on its own schedule, decoupled from the retail GPU cadence that gamers buying physical cards are stuck waiting on. If you want RTX 5080-class rendering today without buying a card that won’t be replaced until 2027 at the earliest, cloud gaming is arguably the more current option right now.\nFor anyone shopping for a graphics card as of this October 2026 update, the calculus has shifted from the usual “wait for the next generation” advice. With Rubin-based RTX 60 series cards not expected before late 2027 – and possibly 2028 given how leaker timelines have already slipped once this year – waiting an entire generation cycle for a card that doesn’t exist yet is a much bigger ask than the usual few-month wait before a refresh. Current RTX 50 and RX 9000 series cards are the real current generation for at least another 12 to 18 months, not a soon-to-be-outdated stopgap – and Nvidia’s own October 2026 lineup still lists the RTX 5090 and RTX 5080 as its flagship GeForce cards, now sold directly alongside the newly launched RTX Spark AI PC superchip whose worldwide preorders opened that same month.\nThe one caveat is the unconfirmed RTX 5090 SE rumor. If Nvidia does release a cut-down high-VRAM variant later in 2026, it would land as a mid-cycle SKU rather than a new architecture, similar in spirit to a Super refresh. It’s worth watching, but it shouldn’t be the reason to delay a purchase given Nvidia hasn’t confirmed the product exists.\nRubin slips to early 2028 for mainstream desktop cards. Data center Rubin parts will likely arrive first in late 2027, with consumer GeForce variants trailing by two to four months, mirroring the Blackwell rollout pattern.\nDLSS updates become Nvidia’s primary 2027 gaming announcement cadence. Expect at least one more DLSS point release (DLSS 4.6 or similar) before any new silicon, continuing the software-first strategy that extended RTX 40/50 series relevance through 2026.\nAMD narrows the RDNA 5 gap rather than beating Nvidia to market. With both vendors tracking mid-to-late 2027, AMD’s real opportunity is matching Nvidia’s launch window rather than exploiting a first-mover advantage it hasn’t seized in 2026.\nUsed RTX 40 series pricing stays elevated through at least Q2 2027. As long as no successor architecture ships, secondhand Ada Lovelace cards will keep holding value abnormally well compared to historical depreciation curves.\nAI PC chips like RTX Spark expand into more OEM laptops before any new gaming die ships. Expect Nvidia to prioritize additional Grace-Blackwell AI PC partnerships with OEMs beyond Microsoft before allocating meaningful wafer capacity back to gaming-only silicon.\nCorrect. As of August 2026, Nvidia has not announced any new gaming GPU architecture, Super refresh, or new SKU beyond variants of the existing RTX 50 series. The RTX Spark superchip announced at Computex 2026 is an AI PC product, not a gaming card.\nNo official date exists – iTechGuides confirmed in August 2026, citing Nvidia’s own public product information, that no RTX 60 launch date has been set. Industry leakers and outlets like Digital Foundry point to Nvidia’s next gaming architecture, Rubin, arriving no earlier than the second half of 2027, with some estimates extending into 2028. Tom’s Guide’s January 2026 coverage put a possible announcement as early as January 2027, with Q1 2027 floated as a launch window, though most other outlets still treat late 2027 as the realistic floor. PCGuide and NotebookCheck’s roadmap tracking, updated as recently as August 2026, splits the two use cases apart entirely: Rubin is expected to ship into data centers across a 2026-2028 window, while the gaming-oriented variant that would eventually become RTX 60 trails behind into 2027 or later. By September 2026, that later end of the range looked increasingly likely – Tom’s Hardware and TweakTown both reported that month that multiple leakers now expect Rubin-based RTX 60 GPUs no earlier than 2028. Nvidia’s own disclosures back that reading up: as of March 2026 the company’s product pages still listed RTX 50 Series as the current GeForce generation with no RTX 60 Series announced, and by October 2026 – even as Nvidia’s GeForce coverage shifted to the newly launched RTX Spark and a preview of DLSS 5 – still no RTX 60 Series release date had been given.\nThe RTX 5090 SE is an unconfirmed rumored variant using cut-down GB202 silicon (110 SM, roughly 14,080 CUDA cores) with 32GB of GDDR7. Nvidia has not confirmed the product, and at least one outlet has published a direct denial that it’s launching.\nRTX Spark is a superchip combining a 20-core Grace CPU with a Blackwell-class RTX GPU, built for running local AI agents and inference workloads on Windows PCs. It’s announced through a Microsoft partnership and isn’t marketed as a gaming product.\nWith no new gaming architecture expected before late 2027 at the earliest, current RTX 50 series cards remain the practical top-tier choice for at least the next 12 to 18 months. Waiting for RTX 60 series in 2026 is not a realistic strategy.\nNo. AMD’s RDNA 5 architecture is also tracking to mid-2027 or later. AMD’s only 2026 moves have been SKU expansions like the RX 9070 GRE, not a new generation.\nDLSS 4.5 Ray Reconstruction, which rolled out in August 2026, brings a unified transformer model for denoising and upscaling to all existing RTX GPUs. It’s a meaningful image-quality and performance upgrade, but it’s a software update, not new hardware.\nNot since the GeForce brand launched in 1999. Even during the 2020-2022 chip shortage, when cards were scarce, Nvidia still launched new SKUs; supply was the constraint, not the roadmap. 2026 marks the first time allocation, not supply, has paused new launches entirely.\nFor more GPU and AI chip coverage, visit our AI chips and GPU pricing hub .\nElias Virtanen is the Cybersecurity Analyst at Tech Insider, bringing hands-on expertise from his background in penetration testing and security consulting. He previously worked as a security researcher at F-Secure in Helsinki, where he focused on threat intelligence and vulnerability disclosure. Elias covers ransomware trends, zero-trust architecture, and the evolving regulatory landscape including NIS2 and the EU Cyber Resilience Act. He holds a CISSP certification and an MSc in Information Security from Aalto University.\n\n### gnews:0fdddf6a7cdc3904b78a8653 \n Nvidia in Talks to Backstop $250 Billion in OpenAI Data Center Financing\nNvidia is in talks to guarantee roughly $250 billion in lease and construction financing for a 10-gigawatt OpenAI data center campus in southern Ohio, first reported by the Wall Street Journal [1]\nThe total project cost exceeds $500 billion including chips, with Nvidia separately weighing a $350 billion deal to finance OpenAI's GPU purchases [2]\nOpenAI lacks an investment-grade credit rating, so Nvidia's balance sheet would effectively stand in as collateral for lenders [3]\nInvestor Michael Burry increased his Nvidia short position at $210.28, calling the arrangement circular: 'Around and around we go' [4]\nThe proposed guarantee is nearly six times larger than Google's ~$44 billion in data center rent guarantees, the previous high-water mark for tech infrastructure commitments [3]\nNvidia is in talks to provide approximately $250 billion in financial guarantees that would backstop OpenAI's lease of a massive data center campus in southern Ohio, the Wall Street Journal reported Saturday [1] . The arrangement, if finalized, would represent the largest vendor-financing commitment in technology history.\nThe 10-gigawatt facility is being developed by SB Energy, SoftBank Group's power subsidiary, on a decommissioned uranium-enrichment site roughly 50 miles south of Columbus. The full project cost — including the AI chips installed inside the data centers — is expected to exceed $500 billion [2] . The first phase, at roughly 800 megawatts, is targeted for completion in 2028 [3] .\nOpenAI does not carry an investment-grade credit rating, making it difficult to raise the debt needed for a project of this scale on its own terms. Under the proposed structure, Nvidia's balance sheet would effectively serve as collateral, allowing lenders to price the financing against the chipmaker's creditworthiness rather than the borrower's [3] . Discussions remain in early stages and no agreement has been signed [1] .\nThe $250 billion guarantee covers OpenAI's lease obligations and certain construction costs for the Ohio campus, but excludes the cost of chips themselves [2] . In a parallel negotiation, Nvidia is weighing a separate deal to finance up to $350 billion in GPU purchases by OpenAI, which would bring total Nvidia financial exposure to the project to roughly $600 billion [3] .\nThe campus will likely house next-generation Nvidia hardware rather than current systems. Nvidia's Rubin Ultra architecture, expected in the second half of 2027, delivers roughly 15 exaflops of performance versus 3.5 exaflops for current chips. The subsequent Feynman architecture, slated for 2028, features die stacking and custom HBM memory, with racks hosting up to 1,152 GPUs [2] .\nSB Energy, which raised $500 million from OpenAI in 2025 and previously built a 900-megawatt solar park near Austin for Google, is planning an initial public offering later in 2026 at a valuation exceeding $50 billion [2] . The Ohio site's power supply is controlled by the U.S. government, with Commerce Secretary Howard Lutnick overseeing access allocation [3] .\nThe proposed arrangement has drawn sharp criticism from investors and analysts who see a structural conflict: a chip supplier underwriting its customer's ability to buy its own products. Michael Burry, the investor famous for his 2008 housing-market short, increased his Nvidia short position on July 24 at $210.28 and posted on X: 'Around and around we go. Nvidia to guarantee $200 billion of ChatGPT's spending on $NVDA chips' [4] .\nNvidia's AI-related financial commitments have grown rapidly. The chipmaker has committed over $40 billion to AI equity investments, including roughly $30 billion invested in OpenAI itself. Nvidia has also funded Safe Superintelligence, the startup led by former OpenAI co-founder Ilya Sutskever, which then purchases Nvidia hardware [3] .\nTech commentator Ed Zitron characterized the deal as 'such an insane thing to do on so many levels and about as bearish as it gets, especially considering it is being built by SoftBank' [4] .\nThe proposed $250 billion guarantee dwarfs existing precedents in tech infrastructure financing. Google has guaranteed approximately $44 billion in data center rent — the current industry high-water mark — making the Nvidia figure nearly six times larger [3] .\nBig Tech's off-balance-sheet AI commitments have ballooned to an estimated $1.65 trillion in aggregate. Meta alone carries roughly $420 billion in such obligations, nearly triple its reported debt [3] . SoftBank is separately backing a $40 billion OpenAI loan through an eight-bank syndication [3] .\nAI infrastructure spending industrywide is expected to exceed $700 billion in 2026, with a cumulative $3 trillion projected through 2028 on data center buildouts [3] . The central risk is that the pace of construction overshoots demand or that returns erode faster than debt can be amortized.\nFor OpenAI, the deal represents a step toward controlling its own compute infrastructure rather than renting capacity from Microsoft, Amazon, and Oracle — its current primary cloud providers. Ownership of dedicated facilities would give OpenAI more control over hardware configurations and training schedules [3] .\nFor Nvidia, the guarantee locks in demand for its chips years into the future, though it also concentrates financial risk around a single customer that has never turned an operating profit. Nvidia shares closed Friday at $206.84, down 0.92% [4] .\nOther AI companies are pursuing similar infrastructure ambitions. Anthropic, Microsoft, and Google have all recently approached Commerce Secretary Lutnick about access to the Ohio site's government-controlled power supply, signaling a broader scramble for energy-rich data center locations [3] .\nThe negotiations remain in early stages, and the terms or structure of any guarantee are subject to change. No timeline for a final agreement has been disclosed [1] . Nvidia is expected to report fiscal Q2 earnings in August, which will give investors their first opportunity to question management about the scope of the company's financing commitments.\nThe deal's completion would also depend on regulatory and political factors, including allocation of federally controlled power at the Ohio site and a Japanese commitment to invest $33 billion in a natural gas plant to supply additional electricity [3] .\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"category\":\"T3\",\"input_id\":\"gnews:0b1b72dac911085466f3a3b1\",\"fits\":true,\"story\":\"hyperscaler-capex-2027\",\"passage\":\"AI hyperscalers continuing to raise their spending outlooks for 2026 and hinting that spending in 2027 will be even higher.\",\"expected\":\"nothing to open\",\"question_if_new\":null,\"why\":\"§4 requires a complete scope and public material to settle the question. The article does not identify which hyperscalers make up the 2027 total.\"}\n{\"category\":\"T3\",\"input_id\":\"gnews:afd03bb57a140b78c7df9dbc\",\"fits\":false,\"story\":null,\"passage\":\"OpenAI's custom chip scales to 1.3 GW by 2027 and Broadcom guides $21.7 billion in Q4 AI revenue, each cutting into NVIDIA's dominance.\",\"expected\":\"nothing to open\",\"question_if_new\":null,\"why\":\"§3 requires one quantity and one period. The article does not compare the same price or quantity in 2026 and 2027.\"}\n{\"category\":\"T3\",\"input_id\":\"gnews:c4d9dea79dca5997aa4f4d03\",\"fits\":true,\"story\":\"spacex-compute-capacity-2027\",\"passage\":\"Musk expects the company to end 2026 with more than 2 gigawatts. And by the end of 2027, he said, the total \\\"may, let's say, be closer to 10 gigawatts of compute than 5 gigawatts of compute.\\\"\",\"expected\":\"nothing to open\",\"question_if_new\":null,\"why\":\"§3 D covers quantities outside any one company. This is SpaceX's own capacity; the passage does not establish it as a company main driver under §3 A.\"}\n{\"category\":\"T3\",\"input_id\":\"gnews:db5c4d64cdbdd661f0e35836\",\"fits\":true,\"story\":\"alphabet-capex-2027\",\"passage\":\"Alphabet just raised its 2026 capex guidance this week and said it would increase further next year.\",\"expected\":\"nothing to open\",\"question_if_new\":null,\"why\":\"§3 D covers quantities outside any one company. This comparison concerns Alphabet's own spending; the article does not establish a separate shared total for 2027.\"}\n{\"category\":\"T3\",\"input_id\":\"gnews:f1af0b706051f98c7b8b3394\",\"fits\":true,\"story\":\"nvidia-revenue-fy2028\",\"passage\":\"The chipmaker also projected 70% revenue growth for fiscal year 2028, reinforcing investor confidence in the sustained demand for artificial intelligence hardware.\",\"expected\":\"nothing to open\",\"question_if_new\":null,\"why\":\"§3 D covers shared quantities. This compares NVIDIA's own revenue across fiscal years, not a shared price or quantity.\"}\n{\"category\":\"T3\",\"input_id\":\"gnews:58fb7b4d430994417016dc48\",\"fits\":true,\"story\":\"nvidia-revenue-fy2028\",\"passage\":\"For the full year, analysts expect 83% revenue growth to $396 billion, before slowing to 44% expansion next year, according to LSEG.\",\"expected\":\"nothing to open\",\"question_if_new\":null,\"why\":\"§3 D covers shared quantities. The comparison is NVIDIA's own revenue growth, and §4 requires independent significance for a separate question.\"}\n{\"category\":\"T3\",\"input_id\":\"gnews:d30249a09f7f187ba29fbbde\",\"fits\":false,\"story\":null,\"passage\":\"In July, they announced a monster $919B investment to build 8.4GWs by 2029 and 18.4GWs by 2035.\",\"expected\":\"nothing to open\",\"question_if_new\":null,\"why\":\"§3 specifies the current or next calendar year for shared quantities. The capacity comparison is between 2029 and 2035, not 2026 and 2027.\"}\n{\"category\":\"T3\",\"input_id\":\"gnews:86bbde98330831dcfcbd4b8f\",\"fits\":true,\"story\":\"nvidia-revenue-fy2028\",\"passage\":\"The company just crossed $96 billion in quarterly revenue, with growth accelerating above 100%. Management now expects revenue to grow another 70% next year\",\"expected\":\"nothing to open\",\"question_if_new\":null,\"why\":\"§3 D covers shared quantities. This is NVIDIA's own revenue growth across fiscal years, not a shared price or quantity.\"}\n{\"category\":\"T3\",\"input_id\":\"gnews:59a612d6bcf31d162a9a3a8c\",\"fits\":true,\"story\":\"nvidia-buybacks-2027\",\"passage\":\"Consensus models project NVDA buybacks at ~$73 billion in 2026 and ~$106 billion in 2027—only 36% to 37% of FCF compared to NVIDIA's pledge to return over 50%.\",\"expected\":\"nothing to open\",\"question_if_new\":null,\"why\":\"§3 D covers shared quantities. These are NVIDIA's own buybacks, and the passage does not establish them as a separate main driver under §3 A.\"}\n{\"category\":\"T3\",\"input_id\":\"gnews:7dfab0010a8ac15786f05387\",\"fits\":true,\"story\":\"nvidia-revenue-fy2028\",\"passage\":\"Chief Financial Officer Colette Kress said the company expects revenue to grow by approximately 70% in fiscal 2028, though she characterized that outlook as supply-constrained.\",\"expected\":\"nothing to open\",\"question_if_new\":null,\"why\":\"§3 D covers shared quantities. This is NVIDIA's own revenue growth, not a shared price or quantity.\"}\n{\"category\":\"T3\",\"input_id\":\"gnews:8697580fd93d9a51eefd9876\",\"fits\":true,\"story\":\"nvidia-revenue-fy2028\",\"passage\":\"Add 70% growth to the $400 billion in fiscal year 2027 revenue, and Nvidia may generate $700 billion to $800 billion in revenue next fiscal year.\",\"expected\":\"nothing to open\",\"question_if_new\":null,\"why\":\"§3 D covers shared quantities. The comparison concerns NVIDIA's own revenue; §4 also requires a separate question to have independent significance.\"}\n{\"category\":\"T3\",\"input_id\":\"gnews:a1332af414b479a05316413f\",\"fits\":false,\"story\":null,\"passage\":\"Apple reclaimed its title as the world's most valuable publicly traded company this morning, surpassing Nvidia by market capitalization\",\"expected\":\"nothing to open\",\"question_if_new\":null,\"why\":\"§3 asks for one quantity over one period. The article compares companies' current market values, not the same price or quantity in 2026 and 2027.\"}\n{\"category\":\"T3\",\"input_id\":\"gnews:73b5d87b6d484d8e54c69370\",\"fits\":true,\"story\":\"nvidia-revenue-fy2028\",\"passage\":\"Nvidia also projected that it would grow revenue 70% next year, meaning revenue could soar more than $100 billion above where analysts were modeling.\",\"expected\":\"nothing to open\",\"question_if_new\":null,\"why\":\"§3 D covers shared quantities. The projected year-over-year growth is NVIDIA's own revenue, not a shared price or quantity.\"}\n{\"category\":\"T3\",\"input_id\":\"gnews:f0ded626a98986d022fd2c30\",\"fits\":false,\"story\":null,\"passage\":\"Nvidia's CEO Jensen Huang noted that total AI spend is expected to be $1 trillion next year and $3 trillion to $4 trillion by the end of the decade.\",\"expected\":\"nothing to open\",\"question_if_new\":null,\"why\":\"§3 requires a supported period and complete scope. The article gives a 2027 total but does not compare that same total with 2026.\"}\n{\"category\":\"T3\",\"input_id\":\"gnews:c2fdbe0959518bba7d3baa10\",\"fits\":false,\"story\":null,\"passage\":\"We accelerated cyber software spending in 2027, above our forecast from our landscape.\",\"expected\":\"nothing to open\",\"question_if_new\":null,\"why\":\"§4 requires the quantity and period to be supported by the text. The passage compares a 2027 forecast with an earlier forecast, not 2027 with 2026.\"}\n{\"category\":\"T3\",\"input_id\":\"gnews:4f85affb9aaa7d877e4539c2\",\"fits\":true,\"story\":\"used-rtx40-prices-2027\",\"passage\":\"Used RTX 40 series pricing stays elevated through at least Q2 2027. As long as no successor architecture ships, secondhand Ada Lovelace cards will keep holding value abnormally well compared to historical depreciation curves.\",\"expected\":\"nothing to open\",\"question_if_new\":null,\"why\":\"§4 requires a complete scope, independent investment significance and a public way to settle the result. The article specifies neither a particular card nor a measurable price series.\"}\n{\"category\":\"T3\",\"input_id\":\"gnews:0fdddf6a7cdc3904b78a8653\",\"fits\":false,\"story\":null,\"passage\":\"AI infrastructure spending industrywide is expected to exceed $700 billion in 2026, with a cumulative $3 trillion projected through 2028 on data center buildouts\",\"expected\":\"nothing to open\",\"question_if_new\":null,\"why\":\"§4 requires the time period to be correct. A 2026 annual amount and a cumulative amount through 2028 do not compare the same annual quantity in 2026 and 2027.\"}\n{\"category\":\"T3\",\"input_id\":\"gnews:61332f6e93b88db0d3214cab\",\"fits\":true,\"story\":\"marvell-revenue-fy2028\",\"passage\":\"The company guided to $3.15 billion for Q3 revenue, and increased their fiscal 2027 and fiscal 2028 revenue expectations to approximately $12 billion and $18 billion.\",\"expected\":\"nothing to open\",\"question_if_new\":null,\"why\":\"§3 D covers shared quantities. These are Marvell's own fiscal-year revenues, not a shared price or quantity.\"}\n{\"category\":\"T3\",\"input_id\":\"gnews:1219126db2e6631aa1264142\",\"fits\":false,\"story\":null,\"passage\":\"What analysts expect the hyperscalers to spend in 2027 alone has more than doubled in the space of a year, from a consensus of $480 billion (€416bn) in August 2025 to $1.08 trillion (€943bn) this month\",\"expected\":\"nothing to open\",\"question_if_new\":null,\"why\":\"§4 requires the period to be supported. This compares two estimates for 2027, not the quantity in 2027 against its 2026 level.\"}\n{\"category\":\"T3\",\"input_id\":\"gnews:910a0652e8879b8859c8927d\",\"fits\":false,\"story\":null,\"passage\":\"The bigger cost structure includes GPUs, networking, cooling, storage, memory and other components that make large-scale AI computing possible.\",\"expected\":\"nothing to open\",\"question_if_new\":null,\"why\":\"§3 asks for a quantity and a supported period. The article describes cost components without comparing a price or quantity in 2026 and 2027.\"}"}}
